      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>AUSTRALIAN CONSCRIPTION.</head>
        <p>AUSTRALIAN CONSCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>ill</p>
        <p>L Misreading of history. 2. Mis-statement of fact.</p>
        <p>3. Suppression of truth_</p>
        <p>I 1 , 1, , i f. 1 4: J</p>
        <p>iiv., ... ', •. . flis ' is; ~ ,-;01;,</p>
        <p>itt</p>
        <p>PJ</p>
        <p>i' E ,</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>irl ' 4 , . r,'</p>
        <p>1'</p>
        <p>11</p>
        <p># i .". Z'', 1. it?</p>
        <p>7, „ 4</p>
        <p>1,1 ° "</p>
        <p>4</p>
        <p>Sir,—Captain George Crosfield, in his letter to the Press, dealing with my address at Warring- ton, accuses me of:—</p>
        <p>1. Misreading of History.—l stated at the meeting that the Labour party, without having studied the Act, passed it. The Defence Bill was not the work of the Labour party. The original Bill was framed by the Liberal party, based on Lord Kitchener's recommendations, and passed with very little discussion by the Labour party. The fact that the Labour move- ment of Australia at the present time is seething with discontent against compulsory military training is proof enough. Let me give Captain Crosfield a few facts. 1 Captain Crosfield asks why I did not quote the figures of the ballot taken by the miners of Broken Hill, when they voted solidly in favour of "National Service." This is a very unfortunate reference on the part of Captain Crosfield, because though the Broken Hill miners voted in favour of the Act, when it was first enforced, the whole Labour movement of Broken Hill voted on the second occasion (September 23rd, 1912), and declared against the Act by a 3-1 majority. They also presented a 14-year-old boy, Alfred Giles, who went to gaol, with a gold medal, because of his stand for conscience, and, furthermore, refused to join the local hospital demonstration because the authorities had asked soldiers to join the procession. The miners' lodged of Newcastle, Illawara, Ba!main, Mount Kendal and other places have passed resolutions against the Defence Act, and other trade - unions in every State have done likewise. The State Labour pal ty of West Aus- tralia have passed a series of resolutions, which, if carried by the Federal Labour party, will mean that Labour will be definitely opposed to Conscription. "The Melbourne Age," in an article dated December 29th, 1913, stated that " the most violent speeches made against the defence system have come from the Labour members, and there is little doubt that if the Labour party were returned to power the poli- tical Labour Leagues would seriously impair the defence movement, if not destroy it alto- gether."</p>
        <p>If Captain Crosfield. still thinks that the Laboni party is keen and solid on the Defence Act, and had studied its details, let him care- fully consider these words, uttered by Mr. Howe, M.H.R., in Parliament on October 22nd: "There is not one man in this House who knows what the regulations are, and almost every day every member is challenged with questions by constituents regarding the treat- ment of our youths. Instead of the Minister of Defence controlling his department, the depart- ment is controlling him."</p>
        <p>Captain Crosfield further contends that no candidate, except three Revolutionary Socialists, opposed the Defence Act. The statement is absolutely untrue and shows ignorance of Aus- tralian politics. Fully 20 candidates at the last election opposed compulsory military training." The Labour Member for Port Melbourne made amendment of the Defence Act one of the strongest points in his platform. Instead of losing his ground, as many Labour members did at the last election, he increased his major- ity from 8,472 to 17,846. Mr. Hugh Conroy, Member for Werriwa (N.S.W.), fought his elec- tion practically on this issue and was returned.' Representatives Higgs and Howe, Senators Rae and Clemons, have all spoken strongly against the present policy. 2. Misstatement of Fact.—Captain Crosfield quotes me as having said: "That a member of the Society of Friends refusing to enter the force/ is liable to a penalty of £1.00." What I said was: "That a parent (who might be a member of the Society of Friends) if he refused I to allow his son to render service required by the authorities, would be liable -to a fine Of ZOO." (Section 134.) Service may be combat-, ant or .non-combatant duties, all service is enforced by the military authorities. Mr. W. Ingle, a Quaker, refused to register his son, and I went to gaol for 14 days, because he would not I submit to the military authorities. Section. 13.5 of the Act states that any person who is liable to render service and fails is liable to a penalt7 of ..£lOO. The maximum fine for being " ineffi- cient" in drills is £2 (not £5, as stated by Captain Crosfield!), but there is a difference in being "inefficient" and not rendering personal service. I stated that the authorities have the power to try any offenner against the Act by court martial, and that the decision of the mili- tary tribunal is final and cannot be repealed by any Civil Court. I said that Civil Courts were used, as a matter of fact I quoted instances of lads who had been tried by magistrates. 3. Suppression of Truth.—Captain Crosfield contends that I ought to have told the audience that the Conferences of the Presbyterian, Angli- can, Catholic, and Methodist and others, are in favour of compulgory military training. I grant that the Churches named are in favour, but what about the others—Cong,regationalists, Baptists, Seventh Day Adventists, Church of Christ, and Friends—who have all passed reso- lutions to the contrary? Last September a' manifesto was issued by 80 Melbourne ministers protesting against the Defence Act. Captain Crosfield cannot get much satisfaction from the Methodist Conference. Last year the voting was equal-43 to ,43—the Chairman decid- ing in favour of the Act. Your readers will be able to gather from this that there is a strong feeling in the Churches against compulsory military training. Captain Crosfield infers that the Defence Act is extremely popular. These figures, just to band, are worthy of note. They apply to Victoria alone (see "Melbourne Age," January 24th, 1914) When born. Registrations. No. liable. 1896 ... 11,884 ... 12,937 1897 ... 11,891, ... 12,987 1898 ... 10,563 13,027 1899 10,037 ... 13,977 These figures show that though the population of Victoria increases, the number of registra- tions decrease. ,This year, in one State alone, 3,940 are liable for prosecution for not having registered. So much for the popularity of Aus- tralian conscription!</p>
        <p>JOHN W 13ARRY. 21, Beech-road, Horfiold, Bristol, March 4th, 1914.</p>
