      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>LANCASHIRE CRICKET.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CRICKET.</p>
        <p>.Sir,—I am sending you an advance copy of a circular letter, signed by myself, to the extent of 3,000, which will be circulated to-morrow to members of the Lancashire County and Man- chester Cricket Club, and prominent Lanca- shire gentlemen. May I ask you to give us your valuable sup- port in bringing the circular to the notice of your subscribers? The committee, on whose behalf I write, is a very representative one. They are determined to do all they possibly can to place the finance of our County Cricket on a firm and substantial basis.—Yours faithfully, DERBY.</p>
        <p>Knowsley, Prescot. [copy.]</p>
        <p>_ Dear Sir,—As you may be aware, the affairs of the Lancashire County and Manchester. Cricket Club are in none too flourishing a position, and a Special Committee has been formed to en- deavour to enlist the support of more ladies and gentlemen in the County. It is at their request that I am writing to lay before you, as briefly as I can, the full position. 1 Last year the total receipts amounted to £6,350, while the expenditure was £7,344, show- ing a deficit of £994. This expenditure can be taken as the normal expenditure for one year, even after the use of the strictest economy. It includes £4OO which went towards, rewarding Paul and Worsley, two' of the players, who, I think you will agree, were in every way worthy of this recognition, and that others will deserve in the future similar reward.</p>
        <p>_ There exists on the ground a mortgage of £14,600, which involves a payment of interest to the extent of £650 a year. To put the club, therefore, on a sound basis and to repay the mortgage requires a sum of at least £15,000. In addition to this sum, it is very desirable, if the committee is to be freed from any anxiety as to finances, and if they are to deal liberally with the players, and at the same time give encouragement to young players throughout thel county, that there should be a largely increasedil number of members, and—may I add?—lady subscribers. At present in the whobt county there are only 2,350 members, a decrease of no less than 750 as compared with ten years ago. The entrance fee is for the present suspended, and the subscription is only 265. a year. , The ambition of the committee, on whose be- half I write, is to raise .215,000 to pay off the mortgage, and to secure at least 1,500 additional members. If this result is achieved the club will be in a flourishing condition, and I have no doubt the committee will then be able to take all steps that may be necessary to put an eleven into the field that will restore Lanca- shire to the premier position. May I ask for your help, either by becoming a member or by sending a donation towards paying off the mortgage, or preferably both, if you have not already done so? I am sure it will be your wish to do something to help for- ward in the world of cricket the fortunes of the county in which you live, or of which you are a neighbour. You will find enclosed a preliminary list of donations and subscriptions, and a form, which I hope you will fill up to the fullest extent in your power, and return to me in the enclosed envelope.-1 am, yours faithfully, DERBY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WATERY BLISTERS SPREAD.</head>
        <p>WATERY BLISTERS SPREAD.</p>
        <p>53, St. Leonard's-road, Prince Rock, Illy - mouth, Eng.—" When about a fortnight old a lot of watery blisters appeared on my baby's back, and then in a few days spread all over her body till she was covered. All her body was a mass of inflammation. As one place would break so about a dozen new places would form. When they broke they left large raw places. Her body was in a terrible state. She was covered all over from head to foot and could not be put in a bath. I was afraid to bathe her, she would cry so with the pain when I tried to dry her afterwards. "I thought I would try Cuticura Soap and Ointment as I had seen of .2 case very much the same as mine being cured. After only a few applications she slept much better than she had done since she was a week old. Cuticura Soap' and Ointment cured her in less than a month." (signed) Mrs. Ethel M. Bradley, August 21, 1913. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post free. Address: F. Newbery and Sons, 27, Charterbouse-squitre, London, or Potter Drug and Chem. Corp., Boston, U.S.A.</p>
        <p>Painters in the Hartlepools have been con- ceded an advance in wages of a half-penny per hour to date from August 1 next. Alderman A. Nuttall, leader of the Blackburn Conservative party, has left home for a month's tour on the Continent, and it is understood that, Pending his return, there is to be no announce- ment in connection with the selection of a second Tory candidate for Blackburn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>EXAMINER</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER</p>
        <p>SCHOOL COIITROVERSY.</p>
        <p>The Wrangle is Carried to the Town Council.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON DEFEATED.</p>
        <p>The wrangle over the contract for the new Oakwood-avenue Council School, which has been carried on so frequently in the Education Com- mittee, was carried to the Town Council meet- ing on Tuesday. On the minutes of the Educa- tion 'Committee coming up for approval, Councillor 'ASHFORD moved that the portion relating to the contract of £16,147 be referred back.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON, seconding, said when the architects came to obtain tenders from builders for the carrying out of their designs, it was found that the lowest tender was £1,600 above the revised amount, which, in turn, was .E 630 above the original conditions—making a total excess of 1.2,200. The Council were the custodians of the borough finances, and how could they, as business men, agree to such terms as those? lie agreed with those who argued that it was impossible to build the schools ac- cording to the accepted plans for less money; his point was that the plans ought never to have: been accepted. It was. unfair to the other competing architects and to the ratepayers, generally to proceed with the work on the terms proposed. _ _</p>
        <p>. . It had been said that the difference in price was due to the increased cost of building mate- rials t he said deliberately that it was noth- in o kind. The great rise in the prices of ma e took pkee between the years 1910 and 1912. At the present time some materials were actually dropping in price—slightly, he ad- mitted—and one or two things had undoubtedly advanced, though only to an infinitesimal amount. COUNCILLOR GOUGH'S MISTAKE.</p>
        <p>oC un or (1 tron said he could only think - that the Sites and Buildings Committee was principally composed of co-opted members and not of members of the Council, and therefore one naturally expected them to support the Chairman of the Education Committee.</p>
        <p>Councillor LINDLEY : I rise to a point of order. I think Councillor Gough has not inquired who are the members of the Sites and Buildings Committee. ,</p>
        <p>Councillor Govon : I am sorry if I have made a mistake. I know there are more co-opted than elected members on the Education Committee. ("No, no.') The MAYOR: You are wrong again. (Laughter.) Councillor PEMBERTON : The members of the Council do not attend. That is the trouble. Councillor GOUGH said they should take the power out of the hands of the men who were extravagant As to the proposal that they should provide accommodation for children under five years of age, it looked as if they wanted to turn the schools into nurseries. He did not see that the town required more schools, but he believed that if the present Chairman continued in office they would not cease to build them. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>A MYSTERIOUS CIRCULAR. Councillor LINDLEY said it was only after the fullest consideration and investigation that the cost was increased. Each member of the Coun- cil had re'ceived a circular with reference to the cost of building materials. He did not know who sent out the circular, but it emanated fro, some association. The committee, however, had authentic information which convinced their' that there was no•possibility of turning back in the matter. Councillor Goma : Councillor Lindley says there is nothing on the circular ' to indicate where it has come from. It is printed on the top—" Warrington and District Property Owners' Association." The MAYOR: He mentioned an association. Councillor LINDLEY : There is nothing on mine to say what association. • Alderman SMETHURST said every precaution was taken by the committee to carry out the scheme in the most economical way. Councillor Pemberton was more to blame than any man in Warrington for. the delay which had arisen. He acted in committee in just the same style as he did at the Council meetings. If he could not get his own Way in committee he would stay away until they had dealt with the matter, and then come to the Council meeting, make a loud speech and fling his arms about as if he were doing all the work in the world. (Laughter.) Councillor WILKINSON: That is so. - Alderman SMETHURST : It is quite correct and he cannot deny it. Councillor PEMBERTON : I do deny it. Alderman SMETHURST said the members of the Sites and Buildings Committee who were suc- cessful business men gave the affairs of that committee just as careful attention as they bestowed upon their own private concerns. Councillor Pemberton had thrown away more money than any other member of the Council by his obstructive methods. COUNCILLOR ROBERTS REPLIES. Councillor ROBERTS said the Council had 21 members on the Education Committee, whereas the number of co-opted members was only 16. If members of the Council did not attend to their duties on the committee or its various sub-committees, the responsibility was theirs and they had no right, having neglected their ' duties, to turn round on the men who gave their time and abilities and criticise them in 'an adverse spirit. It was not fair play or good cricket. The proceedings in connection with the Oak- wood-avenue School had been going on since June, 1912. Step by step every matter had been submitted to the Council and approved by them. The reason for the delay was the various amend- ments moved in the Education Committee and afterwards in the Council by Councillor Pem- berton. The responsibility for the abominable waste of public time and, of the ability of public men rested more upon him than upon anyone else.</p>
        <p>The plans had received the imprimatur of the Board of Education, without any question, which showed that, in the view of that author- ity, there was no extravagance. The tender accepted was undoubtedly in excess of the esti- mate by 10 per cent. What the Council had to consider was whether there was any justification for the additional cost. If there was not, let them refer the minute bank, but if the cost was justified it was their duty, as honourable men, having approved the plans, to accept the tender. "A Cheap Sneer." The remarks of Councillor Gough about schools becoming nurseries was a cheap sneei worthy of a man like Councillor Gough. There were hundreds of little children in this town whose home circumstances, through no fault of the parents, were such that it would be a God- send if there were schools in which they could be cared for, kept clean and healthy, and have a certain amount of amusement brought into their sordid lives. He dismissed Councillor Gough's remark with the contempt it deserved. They assured him that the cost of building had gone up from 10 to 15 per cent. Alderman BOLTON: I can bear that out. Councillor Roberts added that the members of the Council had been favoured with a some- what remarkable epistle bearing the signature of Mr. Cook, secretary of the Property Owners' Association, lie had made it his business to make a few inquiries as to what authority Mr. Cook had for sending out that document, and he was informed that it was not approved of by the more important members of the association. They neither agreed with the figures quoted nor had they given the slightest authority for the document to be sent out.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor GOOGII : I say he has no authority for making such an assertion. Councillor ROBERTS: I have the authority of Mr. Cook's partner, Mr. Gittens. lam told that Councillor Gough, who is posing as a champion of the people who sent out the docu- ment, is not a member of the Property Owners' Association, so therefore you can discount what he says. The people who have had the temerity to issue this document are the same People who, through their association, are advocating the general advance of rents on the ground that building construction to-day is so much More costly than it was 12 months ago. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>AMENDMENT DEFEATED.</head>
        <p>AMENDMENT DEFEATED.</p>
        <p>„ On the vote being taken the result was:— For Amendment—Councillors Dr. Fox, Gough, Pemberton and Lashford-4..</p>
        <p>Aga i F.; st—Aldermen John Evans, Bolton, Smethurst, Burton and Bennett; Councillors Locker, Broadhurst, Roberts, Jenkins, Wilkin• son, Hawthorn; Wilson, Lindley and Chrimos— The minutes were adopted and the contract Approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>• SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>Councillor Pemberton Attacks Education Committee</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Upon the Increase.</head>
        <p>Upon the Increase.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR H. ROBERTS' STINGING RETORT.</p>
        <p>Reckless Expenditure. Extravagant Administration. Abominable State of Affairs.</p>
        <p>These were a few of the choicest phrases hurled at the heads of the Education Committee and berton. the Board of Education by Councillor Pam-</p>
        <p>They were uttered in the debate in the Town Council on Tuesday upon the LUPO increase in the cost of education in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton, who was defeated in the Education Committee, carried the battle into the Council, and once more met with defeat.</p>
        <p>He moved that the Education Estimates be not accepted, and after a lengthy speech was only able to get two other Councillors to vote with him, and his amendment was defeated by an overwhelming majority,</p>
        <p>Councillor H. ROBEICTS, the chairman of the Education Committee, submitted the estimates, which showed that the increase was £2,000, made up largely of the schemes previously sane- ' tioned by the Council. Of that sum, -01,426 represented automatic additions to the teachers'. salaries. They were also faced with the -neves- sity of providing more accommodation, and on that subject Lord Sheffield was asking a ques- tion in the House of Lords that night- The question by Lord Sheffield and answer by Lord Beauchamp will be found at the end of this report.) Some time ago it was pointed out to the Education Committee that it was necessary to provide additional school places. Why had they a deficiency of places? Councillor Pemberton had said they had as man children at school in 1905 as in 1914, and asked why the necessity for the enormous expenditure now. It was very easy to make out a case against anything if very important elements an considerations were omitted. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>I say we are no better than worms to carry out their orders. (Laughter.) If the Council was like me I would not do it: I would bring about a crisis. It is a. contemp- tible state of affairs. Here is money which should come from the National Exchequer, and as it does not come the ratepayers are foisted Upon to find it. I do not know how you coun- cillors stand it. If you were like me I would bring such a state of affairs to an end. If the Government did not find the money there would be no tune.. . (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>I move this estimate be not accepted to see of what you are made. (Laughter, and a Voice: "Foot-and-mouth disease') Councillor Gomm seconded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Vital Matters Omitted.</head>
        <p>Vital Matters Omitted.</p>
        <p>- Vital matters were "deliberately left out. He supplied the deficiency. In 1905 they had 14,035 children, and by a stroke of the pen the Board of Education altered the accommodation as to cubic space, and that reduced the school accom- modation from 14,038 to 11,863. (Councillor Pemberton: "Shame.") Of course, it is for Councillor Pemberton to express his opinion and to have his views, but there are two sides to the question. This in- crease in cubic space was only instituted by the Board of Education for reasons of health. (Hear, hear.) I have yet to understand on what ground any man who professes to have an interest in the child-life of this country would deprive the children of the working people of a sufficiently adequate amount of fresh and clean air so as to allow each child to live under healthy con- ditions. (Hear, hear.) That reduced the accommodation by 2,175 places. Since 1905 various schools had been closed because they were no longer fit for the purpose for which they were built, and they gave accommodation for 1,373. Other schools were threatened in the same way, and would have to be closed as soon as accommodation was found elsewhere; and that made 917 places less. There were 4,465 places which were necessitated by /the increase of cubic space and the closing of schools. They had by the new schools pro- vided 3,480 new places, reducing their deficit to 985. But there was another important factor. In order to provide for the fullest possible use being made of the schools in existence, when the alteration in the cubic space came into operation they had to dismiss from the schools all children they could dismiss under five years of age. They had that day 1,183 children less of that age in the schools than in 1905. They had to provide for them, and it was their duty to do so, otherwise they would not maintain the standard of educational efficiency which obtained in 1905. If they added these, it gave 2,168 places. It was said that these figures did not provide for the normal accommodation they 3ooto increase4ao aof child population. They had about 2,000 births a year in Warrington, and, allowing for deaths and other factors, they had a net incase of child population of school age of from w ere t h to up-to-date -otuol- dd aht have vien to th provide r omattervide n oo not 2,168 places, but 2,168 and 2,440 more. They had nearly 5,000 school places to provide if they made the same provision for children as was made in 1905.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton Twitted.</p>
        <p>--- • How delight delighted, said Councillor Roberts, I wasl i to receive a pamphlet of the Towns' Advertising Committee, (Councillor Pemberton : "Not yet !") It will be. has been sent out broadcast. y (Councillor Pemberton: "Perhaps.") There was never a finer character given to the ad ta n civic community than what appears in this document. (Councillor Pemberton. "True !") I believe these statements are true and I be- lieve if John Pemberton never spoke the truth before—flaughter)—he has once in his life nroved himself in that book to be capable of doing it to the very highest possible degree. (Laughter and applause.) I have read a book called "Dr. Jerkyll and Mr. Hyde," and I am really tempted to ask winch is really Councillor Pemberton, the one who makes the statement in the pamphlet and ' stands like the spider inside his web waiting for the flies to be caught, or the man we are so accustomed to see here, who stands slanging every institution of our corporate body, accus- ing us wholesale and retail of extravagance and imputing all kinds of motives to everybody. (Loud laughter.) I will not say which. (Laugh- ter.) I know human nature is a curious thing, and after that I shall be surprised if I don't find Councillor Pemberton supporting my minutes heart and soul. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON REPLIES.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON REPLIES.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON denied being the sole author of the pamphlet. His opinion on the work of the Education Committee was the opin- ion of a great majority of the people outside, , namely, there was a reckless and extravagant ' expenditure in regard to education. It was a fact that one or two prominent men seemed to rule the roost, and that the rest followed like little children. (Laughter.) The increase in the cost of education this year was a very serious matter to the ratepayers, who were asked to provide 150 per cent. of the increase, while the Government only provided 30 per cent. In spite of the figures which had been given by Councillor Roberts, he contended that the official figures he held in his hand were the correct ones. There were 371 children less in the schools to-day than in 1901.</p>
        <p>The Education- Committee never have consid- ered their expenditure. I don't know whether it is worth my while remaining a member of that committee. They are education fanatics, and money is no object. It is shown all over the country that you can get educated men cheaper than you can get labourers. There is absolutely no check on the expenditure, and it therefore behoves this Council to put on the necessary check. It is a pity the Council does not know more about this financial extrava- gance. The Chairman has spoken blandly to you to-night. He has spoken smoothly, and ,has put some sugar on the pill very nicely. (Laugh- ter.) You are bound now to Oakwood-avenue School, to the Lovely-lane School, to Parr-street 3S,ochnoohla, vae nid,u itlot naonachwahnegne Othletr-ysotureeltillclhmaovle, out of £65,000. It is easily reckoned up, and I can easily see an education rate of 2s. 64. in the x. Therefore, does it not behove us to. build schools as economically -as possible and to use that business. wit ,and enerey we use in our private business? I say deliberately that the excessive rate for education is caused by extra- vagant administration,, 4</p>
        <p>"Like Worms."</p>
        <p>I say deliberately the Board of Education are doing thinge they have no light to do, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MANY WORDS.</head>
        <p>MANY WORDS.</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADHURST said Mr. Pemberton had used words like "monstrous," "alarming," "abominable," and " reckless administration." What he (Mr. Broadhurst) wanted was some. thing more tangible than words. (Hear, hear, and a Voice: "He can give more.") Councillor Pemberton must put his finger on the spot and tell him where there had been reckless expenditure or unbusinesslike admin- istration. If he had done so possibly he might have supported him. It was all very well to say that the Government ought to pay more money and that the ratepayers ought to pay less, but the local authorities were between the devil and the deep sea. It was no use kicking against the pricks, and that was what Councillor Pem- berton's words meant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>PAYING THE PIPER.</head>
        <p>PAYING THE PIPER.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor HE:TRULL said it was supposed to be an axiom that those who called the tune paid the piper, but that was reversed and the Gov- ernment had always called the tune without paying. With one stroke of the pen they had swept out places, roughly speaking, 20 percent. ,He was not saying a word about the desirability. There might be a gain in the health of the tiiil- dren that could not be translated in ot s. d., but why should the Government who called the tune not pay for it? They did not pay their own proportion. Year after year they had been promised a larger contribution from the Exchequer, but "hope deferred made the heart sick," and he was afraid that some of them were near the vomiting stage. (Laughter.) "I am almost sure, although I am the mildest - mannered man who ever cut a throat, I almost share the mental attitude of such a desperado as Councillor Pemberton. (Laughter.) I feel I could almost become a passive resister on the question." (Laughter.) Our position is: Ours not to make reply, Ours not to question why, Ours but to pay and sigh, Helpless three hundred. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>OTHER POINTS OF VIEW. Dr. JOSEPH inquired what would happen if the EdUcation Committee refused to provide any further school accommodation? Alderman BOLTON said they could not do otherwise than build schools as they were re- quired, and it was no good cavilling when they had to pay the piper. The salaries were guar- anteed on certain lines. The Education Com- mittee were doing the work better than the Council could do it. (Hear, hear.) , Councillor HAwnioaN said all the trouble arose through Warrington not having appointed a School Board in 1870 to develop the education upon proper lines. Education should be a national charge, but until it was they would have to bear the present system. The sugges- tion by Councillor Pemberton to bring educa- tion to a deadlock was futile and would not bring about any improvement. (Hear, hear.) ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>BRAVADO NOT BRAVERY. __ _ BRAVADO,</head>
        <p>BRAVADO NOT BRAVERY. __ _ BRAVADO,</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERT'S, in reply, said they had had a promise from Mr. Lloyd George to give more aid to local authorities to meet the cost of education. He hoped and believed that before the next Budget came they would receive a very Substantial amount in aid of education. (Hear, hear.) lam amazed at Councillor Pemberton's bravado; it is not bravery. &amp;Hear, hear, and laughter.) One got to know a little bit about the bravery of Councillor Pemberton on a specific occasion, and I venture to say he /could have been the last man to fight anyone, far less to fight the Government. (Laughter and ap- plause.) It would be a silly, absurd and childish attitude for a member to set himself up to defy a Government. They would do the same as they did in Wales, where the Board of Educa- tion said they would do the work themselves, or would obtain an injunction against the authority and charge them with the cost. That is the happy position an extremely fine business man like Councillor Pemberton suggests for Warrington. The thin,'" really is too absurd. (Laughter and applause) On a vote the amendment only got three sup- porters—Messrs. Pemberton, Gough and Lash- ford—and was beaten by an overwhelming majority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WARRINGTON SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>A Lord Makes Inquiries in the Gilded</p>
        <p>Chamber.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords on Tuesday, Lord Sheffield (L.) asked the Government whether their attention had been called to the answer given by Mr. Pease in the House of Commons , on the 3rd April, 1913, as to school provision and overcrowding at Warrington That there would shortly be opened two schools of 1,260 and 420 places each, and that in addition the Board had proposals before them which would add 4.000 places to the school accommodation of Warrington"; whether by the return of schoolr. up to 31st July, 1913, there had been closed in Warrington since July, 1912—Christ Church of England, 181 places; Sankey Bridges Infants, 212; Warrington Trinity Church of England, 171; total, 564. dion i t fte What was the stage of progress of the 4,000 places proposed to the Board of Education, whether there are still seven schools departments in Warrington where the yearly average attendance exceeds the accommodation, and what steps the board of Education to prevent this over-crowding and tohaisstenktthinge provision of the 4,900 places mentioned by Mr. Pease nearly a year ago.</p>
        <p>EARL BEAUCHAMP REPLIES. Earl Beauchamp (First Commissioner of Works), who replied, said with regard to the first part of the question that the school for 1,260 children was opened on the 19th August, 1912, arid that for 420 on the 31st March, 1913. With regard to the places still to be provided the local Education Authority had accepted tenders for one schocd of 1,260 and were taking steps to obtain plans for another of the same size. In a third case, where 840 places are to be provided, plans for one department of 392 have recently been before the Board. In a fourth case the authority have stated that they will proceed with the provision of 420 places as soon as the tenders for the school Arst mentioned have been accepted. The latest re- turns in the possession of the Board show thay in 13 departments in six schools the average attendance during the school year 1912-13 ex- ceeded the recognised accommodation. The Board made deductions amounting to £5O from the grants in respect of those four schools, and in the two other cases have given a warning to the authority. The Board are pressing the authority to expedite the provision of the new schools which I have mentioned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
