      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TOWN PLANNING APPOINTMENT. Some Councillors Raise Objections.,</head>
        <p>TOWN PLANNING APPOINTMENT. Some Councillors Raise Objections.,</p>
        <p>There was a long discussion at -Warringt Town Council on Tuesday over the appointme of a temporary assistant to the Surveyor wi an experience of town planning, the salary be ..e3 3s. per week. The Streets Improveme Committee recommended the appointment Mr. W. Walker, of Warrington. Alderman BaNsurr, who moved the adopts of the minutes, said they advertised for a ta with experience of town planning. Th had three candidates, and of the three 3 Walker kad the least experience. He candid admitted that he had never read the Toi Planning Act or the regulations: _ _ .</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON : I -think it is unfair for thl Chairman of the committee, Alderman Bennett to move the passing of the minutes and thel throw cold water upon them. The town plan ning in Warrington is not such a great think that we require an expert. Councillor JENKINS : It seems ungracious flat unkind of Alderman Bennett to make such re marks about the gentleman appointed. Councillor HILISHALL : It is implied that V,l; Walker has been appointed because of his et' solute ignorance of town planning. It is a re section upon the committee, for like mast&amp; like man, and they do not know anything aboa town planning. (Laughter.) Councillor BROADHURST protested against stte, "an improper discussion taking place." SO' he thought the Chairman was justified in e pressing his views. Councillor Pemberton he( in the education debate spoken about worras and he (Councillor Broadhurst) would conside‘i the Chairman a worm if he knuckled under things of that description. (Laughter.) Councillor OSBORNE thought the committei had done wrong in not appointing an esPe.,,le enced, man. Alderman Bennett had only tested against what he considered It tly01 i;, methods. (Hear, hear.) He moved that matter be referred back.</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADHURST seconded. Councillor WitxuisoN said Mr. Walker 118(1 done much more expert work than town pie; ning, and had great ability as an ~atehitt"-. (Hear, hear.) ff +he Councillor HAWTHORN said if they passe(' minutes they would be told they had done P piece of jobbery. Councillor ROBERTS said they had in the rot' ough Surveyor's office an expert in town plan' ning. The amendment to refer the matter back IcBs defeated by 15 votes to 11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CO-OPERATORS AND OUTSIDE LABOUR.</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATORS AND OUTSIDE LABOUR.</p>
        <p>A Vote of Censure on the Committee</p>
        <p>Meets With Defeat.</p>
        <p>The 213th quarterly meeting of the Warring- ton E.I. Co-operative Society, Ltd., was held ni the Co-operative Hall on Monday. Mr. J. Upson presided, and was supported by the whole of the committee, and about 150 members were also present. The President laid special emphasis on the general upward tendency of the business of the society, and also gave par- ticulars of proposed future developments. The report for the half-year ended January 20th. showed that sales had been £163,630, an in- crease of £5,733 over the corresponding period, making the sales for the 12 months £326,820, a total increase of £24,599 over the year 1912. The membership had also increased to 13,575, being 257 on the three months and 1,028 for the year. The net profits again allowed a dividend of 3s. 2d. in the .2 on all purchases. The report was adopted, following which the' auditor, Mr. Green Chappell, made a brief statement, congratulating the members on the sound financial position of the society, which he stated was equal to 275. for every -21 share held by the members. He also reported having examined the whole of the deeds and securities of the society, and that the same were in order. The balance sheet for the half-year was passed unanimously. I The election of two members' delegates to the C.W.S. meetings resulted in Mr. F. Gill and Mr. Howliston being appointed. For three vacancies on the Benevolent Fund Committee, Messrs. Hibbert and Rushton were re-elected, and Mr. Lomas appointed in place of Mr. Seed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HIRE PURCHASE SYSTEM.</head>
        <p>HIRE PURCHASE SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>The recommendation of the committee that .210 be invested in the Wigston Hosiers, Ltd., met with opposition from a number of mem- bers on the grounds that co-partnership was opposed to co-operation and trade unionism, and on being put to the meeting was eventually thrown out, although a full explanation was given to the members of the position and the reason for the suggestion. The recommendation of the committee that the society enter into the hire purchase business was adopted, after a short discussion, it being understood that every effort will be made to safeguard the interests of all members who might avail themselves of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>BUILDING CONTRACTS.</head>
        <p>BUILDING CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>A long discussion ensued on the action of the committee in letting a contract for the erection of a new branch to the C.W.S. Building De- partment, and a resolution was moved and sec- onded that the meeting resented the action of the committee in accepting a tender from out- side Warrington, when a lower tender had been received from a firm in the town capable of carrying out the work.</p>
        <p>„ The action of the committee was defended by several members, and explanations and par-</p>
      </div>
