        <p>Old. qiabar CHICALA A DRY FEED FOR CHICKENS</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp;SON Ltd. BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>POULTRY PEN..</p>
        <p>. - 14 Uiiita and Interesting Facts and Chat for Amateurs. '1"1"14144.44•14•144444444441014,1401014+1. " EXAMINER '!.SPECIAL. Amateurs have frequently a difficulty in select' an, lag breeds. Many go in for cross-breds, even mongrel races. Sometimes the latter 'e good results in the matter of egg produc- tion, but the eggs are generally upon the small Seale, and do not bring top prices in the market. Neitherl do the birds sell as well for table pur- it-17;8, as do those of the better breeds. A pure best in the end, although about half ezen cross-breds of good quality are on the -s,atket. Pohl, ing trrY experts are now strongly recommend- great" Wyandottes. These have come into the layrest popularity through their remarkable ti;:ng results. In the Utility Club's competi- inls for last year the Wyandottes scored easily w the matter of egg production. The White and3-andottes defeated all other breeds as 'layers, he t • has Y proved all-season layers, ,which fact doubtlessly made them more popular.</p>
        <p>THE DIFFERENT VARIETIES. ._There are nearly a dozen different varieties of 'YYti.ndottes. They are known as the Silver, 1(len, White, Buff, Partridge, Silver-pencilled, ‘-elumbian, Blue-laced, and Black. Of all the i ch e 4 species seems to be the White, both in 'egard to utility and hardiness. The others Ilfe good layers and are more ornamental than e Whites. Thus, if an amateur is about to I'e-stock his pens, the claires of the White -</p>
        <p>,Yandottes should be given consideration. These birds are good for the table and are so 1141(1Y that they can thrive anywhere, either in a Pen, or on a free range. As the utility coin- Petitions have proved they are very free layers, and the eggs are large and coloured, after the kind now so much in favour with the public. Dressed for culinary purposes, the birds can hold their own against all-comers in the market, 11, nd generally bring about top prices., The !.e13,8 make good sitters and mothers, and,,, the "l'eed suffers little from any form of disease. •</p>
        <p>OLD-FASHIONED DORKING. We seem to he again on the eve of a boom ktgotaa old-fashioned Dorkings. Thirty years the tile Dorkings were pre-eminent. Since then dit/have , receded in popularity, but why it is to say, except it be this purely English tot:i' was not so fashionable as the more gaudy varieties introduced during the Past ~1•411ta, )tkiugs are fine birds, with one disad- they t from the amateurs' standpoint, that or :13 liot thrive when penned in small runs,l birds 'atop places. With that exception, the heft ,Itts excellent and well worth keeping steari-;;4Y The good-sized white eggs, and lay fairly although they are not so j,iolthc as the lalottes As a; table bird the Derking out- -10;8 other breeds. They are weighty,. with °f firm, white flesh, and cocks have been ited at weights of 141 b. and upwards. Sil . e .'ere,, " are four varieties—the Coloured, the .rey, the White, and the Cuckoo. The th, nied is the largest of all, and is, perhaps, best for table purposes.</p>
        <p>THE STURDY BRAHMAS. atAnotber useful breed for the emateur is the ifurdY 13rahmas. They are still in great request vatileY have been somewhat eclipsed for show 1.1)(1ses by some ,of the more recently intro- -aced strain I write about birds for use than sha__ wri ws and can commend the Brahmas, as they wn. 'a advantages which are Peculiarly their o</p>
        <p>Titer° are two kinds—Light and Dark Brah- -</p>
        <p>mas—and there is not much to choose between them, although, if anything, the Dark have been the more popular. They make heavy table birds, but from the edible standpoint are slightly inferior in quality and flavour to the Dorkings. Cocks make a weight of about 121 b. and hens up to 91b. Their weight prevents them from flying, and this' is an advantage, as they can be kept in low-fenced enclosures.</p>
        <p>- _ One of their strong points is winter laying, the eggs coming at the time when the supply is short and prices good. The eggs are well-shaped, rich and brown. In summer the hens are apt to become very broody, and this is a disadvan- tage in the eyes of some. They are faithful sitters.</p>
        <p>iras nommowsliOle •</p>
        <p>t vjt,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>COCHINS AS WINTER LAYERS.</head>
        <p>COCHINS AS WINTER LAYERS.</p>
        <p>Winter laying is always a strong recommenda- tion. in any brood, and this is a feature which appeals strongly to the amateur. Another good breed for this purpose, like the Brahmas, al- though not quite up to the standard of the latter, is the Cochins, of which there are several varieties. Their massive beauty always makes them attractive features of the poultry pen, and they are valuable for producing cross- breds. They, like the Brahmas, are not fliers, and are generally tame, well-behaved birds.</p>
        <p>- - - The eggs are deep brown, but are not of large size. This is deemed a defect by some poultry. keepers, and has led to fewer Cochins now being</p>
        <p>seen than was formerly the case. If they lay smaller eggs, the quantity is large, one lent often laying six a week for the greater part of the winter. They make first-class sitters and mothers. The Cochins are as. nice a class of fowls as any amateur can desire to possess. ,</p>
        <p>(Other breeds will be dealt with next week.)</p>
        <p>HINT TO POULTRY-KEEPERS.</p>
        <p>Stronger germs and more vigorous chickens are usually. produced from 'two-year-old hens than from pullets, says E. T. Brown in "The Smallholder." At the same time, the difficulty may be overcome by mating a cock at least two years old with the pullets, as the offspring from such are usually strong and hardy. Pullets mated with a cockerel are generally unsatisfac- tory, and lack stamina. In the same Manner, two-year-old hens are better if they are run with an early-hatched cockerel, and they throw better chickens than if both parents are in their second season; be- sides which, it is invariably found that a larger percentage of eggs contains a germ. Early in the season old cocks are often sterile, as a result of delay in recovering from the moulting period. It is important that the male bird shall be unrelated to the pullets or hens, for the crossing of unrelated strains undoubtedly . makes for health and vigour. In-breeding sometimes cannot be avoided, and provided that it is done carefully by a fully experienced person, no harm follows, but it is merely running an unnecessary risk to use related parents when it is possible to avoid doing so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>BENEFITED BY INSURANCE ACT.</head>
        <p>BENEFITED BY INSURANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>Lancashire Women Get Much Relief. Sr eiitsby having been made of excessive sick- nessati women under the Insurance Act, have been obtained from the Secre- p marlchester Unity of Oddfellows of tary of tile the sic pay sickness ments made by 'that society to t a claimscteln r s both men and women during the nine months ended October 12 last. The figures cover the ex- l.perience of 872,608 members-704,950 Men and 6o 6.58 women—in all parts of the United King- , Payments on the average for . both men and King- dom. were: England, 7s. 9d. .per member; Scotland, Gs. 10d.; Wales, 9s. 9d.; and Ireland, ss. 2d. The only excess is in Wales, which bears out the previous experience of all friendly socie- ties that sickness claims are much heavier there thanin other parts of the kingdom. England isabout the expectation, and Scotland and Ire- land are well below the expectation. In all cases the payments to the men were below the actuarial estimate of 95, 9d. In Kent the payment was only 6s. lid., and in Lancashire Bs. 11d. With the women, however, it was differ- ent. The actuarial estimate was 6s. Gd., but in Lancashire, -where large numbers of Married women are engaged in the mills, the 'cost was Bs. Bd.; in Clieslire it was 7s. 4d.; in Kent, where women workers are fewer, the payment was ss. sd.—about 16 per cent. below expecta- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>EMPHATIC TALK.</head>
        <p>EMPHATIC TALK.</p>
        <p>The Kind that Carries_ Conviction to Every</p>
        <p>Header.</p>
        <p>Conviction must follow such emphatic proof as this. Here lis an Earlestown case. Read it, and see if doubt can exist in the face of this evi- dence. "My work in the bakehouse is very trying for the kidneys," says Mr. J. F. Singleton, of 2, Brunswick-road, Crow-lane West, Earlestown, " for I work in a very warm atmosphere and have a lot of bending to do. I had a bout of kidney trouble about twelve months ago. I was nearly doubled up with sharp, cutting pains across the small of my back. "For two or three months Lsuffered terribly. Even at night I could not get much sleep. I used to wake up feeling worn out. ,The bladder was weak, and it was a painful matter to get relief. " At last I thought I would see what 'Doan's Backache Kidney Pills would do for me. I sent for a box, and began to take them, and it was not long before they gave me relief. As I per- severed with Doan 's Pills all the pain left my back, and the kidney weakness was corrected. " I have not bad any return of the complaint though it is twelve months now since Doan's Pills put me right. Needless to say, I heartily recommend the medicine. (Signed) J. F. Single- ton."</p>
        <p>Price 2/9 a box. 6 boxes 13/9; of all dealers, or frofromFoster-MeClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oz- ford-street, London, W. Don't ask for backache or kidney pills—ask DISTINCTLY for DOAN'S Back- ache Kianey Pills, the same as Mr. Singleton had. •</p>
        <p>RUNCORN VOCALIST'S 100 SUCCESSES. Through winning the baritone and challenge solo competition at the Widnes Eisteddfod, Mr. Walter Shaw, of Runcorn, celebrated his 100th success at eisteddfodau. Mr. Shaw is a working carpenter,. ,employed by the Manchester Ship Canal Company. As a Volunteer he won the regimental bugling prize. lie is an excellent cornet player, and no mean performer on the fiddle and the pianoforte.</p>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN—continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1914.</p>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>A FAVOURABLE SEASON.</p>
        <p>The improvement in the weather last week was appreciated in the southern and western counties, where work had been at a standstill on account of the excessive rainfall, says "The Times." The land has recovered sufficiently for tillage operations to be resumed. The February rains were unevenly distributed. Correspond- ents in Sussex and Berkshire state that they have been unable to do anything on the land for a fortnight, whereas in Lincolnshire and in the North field operations have gone on steadily under favourable circumstances. Notwithstand- ing the interruption, work is well forward, many having planted their beans and earl' potatoes in a good seed-bed, -while considerable areas of corn have been drilled, and if the ' weather permits things will go on rapidly now. , There has been a lighter rainfall north of the ' Tweed than in the Metropolitan area. The situation in parts of Aberdeenshire is creating no little anxiety, and inconvenience. Wat„r has to be carted for stock and domestic pur- poses,. an experience Which is regarded as</p>
        <p>capable only of the alternative interpretation., equally perturbing, of drought or excessiVo rain- fall in late spring and summer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>FOOT-AND-MOUTH DISEASE.</head>
        <p>FOOT-AND-MOUTH DISEASE.</p>
        <p>The Irish Department of Agriculture on Sun- day night issued a notice stating that three.ozit- breaks of foot-and-mouth disease have been dis- covered in County Cork—at Ballynacourty (near Kinsale), at Douglas, and at Bally- nacrusha (near Queenstown). The Department has made the usual orders regarding the move- ments of stock in these districts. They regard as a possible source of infection, in. these cases the movements of drovers or dealers who have recently been at Birkenhead. -</p>
        <p>- A resumption of the cross-Channel trade will be delayed in consequence of the recurrence of foot-and-mouth disease at Birkenhead, says "The Times.'! There is no reason for thinking that infection now exists anywhere in Ireland. The state of things before the recent outbreaks in County Kildare is inconsistent with the view that Ireland has, knowingly or unknowingly, been , harbouring the disease. Foot-and-mouth disease is not a scourge that can be concealed. The problem centres chiefly in Birkenhead itself,, and in the boats that conveyed the in- E•cted cargoes; and with the scope of operations narrowed down to these limits the authorities may be able soon to extirpate the disease. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>TO ENLARGE THE ROYAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>TO ENLARGE THE ROYAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The special committee appointed by. the Royal Agricultural Society of England, to consider what steps could be taken to increase the mem- bership, and in what Manner the society could be made of still more use to its members, met under the chairmanship of Sir Ailwyn Fellowes at the Society's House.</p>
        <p>It was decided to invite the members of Coun- cil to submit suggestions that would be helpful to the committee at their next meeting on March 30th, and it was intimated that the committee will also be pleased to receive suggestions from members of the society and others interested in agriculture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LIBERTY AND EXERCISE FOR COWS.</head>
        <p>LIBERTY AND EXERCISE FOR COWS.</p>
        <p>After milking, a large sliding door in the centre of the back wall is opened, and the cows, unchained, pass through it into,a.large covered yard, wonderfully well lit and ventilated, and covered with clean litter on a chalk floor. Here they live clean and at liberty, with mangers before them, instead of being tied up in their stalls, at all times when the weather is unfit for them to go out into the fields. This plan ap- pears to me to be one of the simplest and most rational that could be devised. Liberty, exer- cise, however small, and purer air has always appeared to me to be the great desiderata of the cow, chained from morning to night, and from night to morning, in winter; but here the ques- tion is solved—excepts° far as very large herds are concerned—although even with these it should pay, if four yards became necessary. The one item of cost which divides Mr. Mend's sys- tem from that of the average farmer lies in the cost of the plant for refrigeration and sterilisa- tion. There is nothing else that is not possible on the rent-p4ing farm.---JAMES LONG, in , " Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>FARMERS AND TARIFF REFORM.</head>
        <p>FARMERS AND TARIFF REFORM.</p>
        <p>_ - At the annual dinner of the South Hereford- shire Farmers' Union at Ledbury, Mr. J. M. Parry, chairman of the Ledbury branch, pre- sided over about 150 members of the union. He was supported by Mr. Rowland Hunt, MX-, who, in the course of a speech, made a refer- ence to Tariff Reform. Agriculturists, he said, must refuse to vote for any \member who is not going to give fair play to agriculture. Under any system of tariffs which did not include agriculture the wages in the town would go up, and the wages in the country would then have to go up, too. The farmer would have to pay higher wages, while he would not get any benefit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>The next few weeks will be a time of consider- able activity in preparing the soil for the re- ception of the various seeds of vegetables and flowering plants. Before sowing seeds the soil must be in it perfectly friable condition.. There would be fewer complaints about the non-germination of seeds if more attention were given to the preparation of the soil. Amateurs will be well advised, in those cases where the soil does not contain sufficient humus, to sow all seeds (excepting top-roVed subjects) in boxes of loam, leaf mould and sand.</p>
        <p>The result will convince amateurs, who have not hitherto raised their plants in boxes in a good rooting medium, the folly of sowing out of doors in poor soils. The plants comes along much more quickly when sewn in a good com- post, and the extra time taken in transplant- ing well rebays for the trouble. The plants are ready for putting out some weeks ahead of those sown in the open, and if seedlings are well watered before lifting from the boxes, to enable them to carry a nice ball of soil! the disturbance will scarcely be recognis- able.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>VEGETABLE SEEDS.</head>
        <p>VEGETABLE SEEDS.</p>
        <p>Vegetable seeds should not be sown until the soil is in a fit state. The soil should break down finely and be just sufficiently moist to hasten germination. Those who have had the soil turned over for some months will, after levelling the soil, be able to make sowings of early vege- tables as soon as the Eipil is sufficiently dry. A good dusting of soot over the surface before levelling will be most beneficial. It is really a waste of time to sow 'seeds on hastily-dug ground. The earliest seeds to sow will be broad beans, which should be got in as soon as pos- sible.</p>
        <p>In March sow or plant the following:, Broe- cole, cabbage, cauliflower, carrot, cress, mustard,</p>
        <p>Continued in preceding column</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>aljurrij</head>
        <p>aljurrij</p>
        <p>HALF-PENNIES AT CHURCH.</p>
        <p>During the past year 63,282 coins and one cheque were received:in the Parish Church col- lections. Half-pennies numbered 11,506; pen- nies, 39,650; threepenny bits, 7,966; sixpences, 3,038; shillings, 913; florMs, 118; half-crowns, 58; crowns, 2; half-sovereigns, 22; and there were 9 sovereigns.</p>
        <p>The average number of coins collected for church expenses was .4,062. The average num- ber collected for special objects was 858. The collections averaged all the year round 989. The average amount of collections for special objects was £lO 28. The average amount of collections for church. expenses was £7 lie. sd. The aver- age 'amount of. colleqtions generally was £8 9s. 10d. The average amount required every week for church maintenance was £6 lie. 10d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>' ESTABLISHED v. CAMOLIC</head>
        <p>' ESTABLISHED v. CAMOLIC</p>
        <p>CIIURCII.</p>
        <p>In his Lent address to his parishioners the Rector says: " There never was a time when it was more necessary to understand the Faith of the Church, and few indeed are they who can b trusted to p o gotteion a right answer to those who question of the Church of Englandas the only true branch of Christ's Holy Catho- lie Church in this' land. The Church of Rome Iprofesses to be the Onlyleacher'of what is truly Catholic. A few great scholars, of acknowledged ability are openly denying what have hitherto been accepted as articles of the faith, while others, holy men of God, in their zeal for souls, want to yield certain- points which they think are non-essential in order to preserve and extend outward unity. Now it is in answer to these that we desire to teach ' The Faith once for atl delivered to the sante,' and it is this that we intend to Proclaim in the instructions we pro- pose to give." The subject is being dealt with by lectures during. Fridays in Lent.</p>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH The Pulpit of Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday was occupied by the Rev. 11. Wil- liamson., who spoke on the words found in Philippians 1. xxiii., "To be with Christ, which is far better." Man to-day had little desire to depart this life. His aspirations were rather to live on, and even to those for whom life had ! proved hard, existence was dear. The pessi- mist might say "Life is not worth living," yet to the vast majority it Was a good thing, after all, to be alive. Occasionally, said the preacher, one meete with those who wish all were over, and to some ! extent sympathy goes out to these sorrowful ones. Yet there is a certain element of cowardice, a shirking of duty, in this 'Attitude. Life bolds greater purposes for all, and each one must summon courage to do his duty, and seek, if need be, to suffer. and be strong. But to the greater number it is a glorious thing to be alive. Young life especially is radiant with hopes of plans maturing, and the future bringing the fruits f past labours. Paul had no cowardly desire to depart this life. To him it was no poor thing to live, nor did he desire to shirk life's sorrows and tribulations. To Paul the great message of this life, with its trials and persecutions, was that the whole was prophetic of an existence richer, fuller and in- finitely better than this life's best. All wrong pointed to the time when sin should cease; every joy told of the greater joys that were to come. To die was merely to surren- der a good life for a better. Such thoughts as these are full of comfort to all. By them life is sweetened immeasurably, and the latter days are Made brighter and nobler. With such an outlook on life, old age is radiant with glad- ness and inspiration. To die is to be with Christ, which is far better.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WORKERS BETTER EDUCATED.</head>
        <p>WORKERS BETTER EDUCATED.</p>
        <p>Pointed Speech at St. John's Bazaar.</p>
        <p>The final day of the bazaar held in oCaitil•loe- ' Cairo- street Schools in aid of the St. John's Presby- terian Church, Wilderspool Causeway, was Sat- urday. The enthusiasm was 'very keen n receive. absence ice The part of the stall holders and large numbers al- in to the iv tended during the afternoon and evening. Alto- getherwere close upon £6OO has been raised. Mr. J. A. Ransome opened the bazaar, and the Rev. W. Reid (pastor) pr Mr. J. Longhand, who had suffered from a family bereavement. The Revs. T. T. James and Stanley Mellor were also present. : The CHAIRMAN said Mr. Longhand had sent a cheque, which they we_se previous day's takings amounted to only £7B lks. lid., but they hoped to reach the mini- main of ot:500, in addition to what would cover the expenses of the bazaar and the legal iex- penses n conveying the land. (Hear, hear.) Mr. RANSOME trusted they would raise more than £5OO, which could go towards the exten- sion of the schwa accommodation. They were, he said,' living in an age of education, and there was no doubt that what was known as "labour unrest" as well as unrest generally, was very largely due to the fact that the working classes were becoming more education and wanted what they had a certain right to, and that was more of the joys of life. (Hear, hear.) He thought some of the £75,000,000 expended on the Army and Navy should be devoted to • • giving a broader education, because the expenditure on education nationally Was £18,500,000 : yearly. They might very well speak of the burden of armaments instead of the burden of education. '(Applause.) He hoped their efforts would be successful. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>SYMPATHY BETWEEN CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>SYMPATHY BETWEEN CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>_ . Proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Ransome, Dr. MELLoa expressed his personal sympathy and that of his own church with the efforts of the St. John's congregation, for there was a close link between Cairo-street Chapel and St. John's. He advocated the existence of a con- siderable amount of sympathy hetween churches and it was gratifying to find it had been niwdisy- P H layed in practical ways. e thought it was a bad thing that the best causes did not get on without money, but under the present state of things they must have mon,ey, and even religion needed it to keep going. ghat, therefore, tutide bazaars. a necessity, although unfortunate and regrettable. Bazaars brought - different pele together and thus enlarged the bond between them. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>GOODS ON TRAMCARS</head>
        <p>GOODS ON TRAMCARS</p>
