      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>The Free Wheel Not a Recent Invention.</p>
        <p>TO PREVENT ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will probably' be sur- prised to hear that the free-wheel is not the recent invention they generally suppose. True, it only came into common use about twelve or thirteen years ago, when every new machine had a free-wheel and every owner of an old one quickly got rid of his no longer satisfactory fixed wheel and plunger brake. But in prin- ciple the free-wheel' is only a revival of forty years ago. At that time one of Nettleford's workmen in* Birmingham was in the habit of riding to and from his work on one of the old high bicycles. This man, who was evidently a mechanic niuch above the average, made his machine into a free-wheel by means of a simple clutch, and when going down an incline could stop his pedals just in the same way as we do now. The remarkable part of the invention was that no one seemed to recognise its possibilities, and what Might. have been a lucrative idea completely disappeared. Ten years later the same principle was tried on a tricycle, but with no better results, as none wanted it. n was not until the beginning of the present century that both makers _and riders alike were fully awake to the advantages and the now universal improvement.</p>
        <p>Periodical Cleaning.</p>
        <p>In cleaning a machine it is always as well to stand it upside down so that the wheels can easily revolve, and to lock the head to secure complete steadiness. Very often it is also better to loosen the bell and let it swing clear of the handles. Dry mud should always be wiped off with a wet cloth, but care should be taken that the latter is not wet enough to let water drip on to any part of the machine, as it has a way of running down tubes and spokes, ultimately working its way into the wearing parts of the machine. The enamel, when cleaned, should be polished with a soft cloth, and although some people, in order to give it a glossy appearance, wipe it with a slightly oiled rag, the deposit of oil has a tendency to attract the dust and make' the machine look dull very soon after riding. On the other hand, caked mud wipes off very much easier from an oiled surface. If any polishing pastes are used for the nickel, be care- ful that they are well cleaned off afterwards,l and not allowed to remain, as some pastes eat into the nickel in a comparatively short time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Cycling Clubs.</head>
        <p>Cycling Clubs.</p>
        <p>_ Every year the question of the value of cycling clubs turns up. Yet it is always fresh. There are invariably two points in question, first, the value of the cycling club at all, and, second, the preference or otherwise for the " mixed " club. But surely the value of the club scheme has been demonstrated too often to require further argument, yet there are still some sceptics. Many young riders are fond of the sport, but object to cycle alone, and for them the club is a decided boon, adding much to the enjoyment of a run in the country. There is a feeling of loneliness which comes over every rider near the end of a day's run; when the darkness is coming on, and there seems to be a greater distance between every milestone, but it is appreciably lessened if one is in the company of several others. Of course, there is the old objection that the pace of the club must be that of its slowest member, and punctures seem even more annoying when the whole party has to dismount for their repair. Still, the general verdict on club runs is a favourable one. The question of a mixed club is another matter, and the complaint that it becomes very "cliquey" is very often justified. Perhaps it depends on the club and its organisation. Or is it the secretary?</p>
        <p>Motor Signalling. Herr Bartlimann, a German engineer, has recently introduced an ingenious device for avoiding accidents to cars at street corners. Two movable arms are attached to the front and back of the car,; by means of which the driver indicates the direction he is taking on turning. When at rest the arms are horizontal, being brought up to an angle by the controls. At the end are bright red discs, which are lighted up at night-time. Another suggestion for the safer negotiation of corners is the more sensible use of the horn instead of the meaningless blasts which merely serve to disconcert both pedes- trians and other users of the road. When a Motorist is approaching cross-roads, for in- stance, and intends going straight forward, he should sound his horn once; if he intends turn- ing to the right lie should give two blasts, and when he wishes to go to the left--which is the most dangerous position—he should give three blasts. Furthermore, only motors should have horns. Bicycles and auto-wheels require bells only, and should follow the same rule. Of course, no rules are of any use without almost universal application, and legislation would probably be necessary before this could be secured. The Motor Liner.</p>
        <p>The internal combustion engine has already proved its suitability for ocean traffic, but the ' "Mississippi." the first motor-driven liner, which was launched several days ago for the Atlantic Transport Line, nevertheless marks a distinct advance in marine engineering. The new vessel, which is intended for the Atlantic trade, will hi equipped with two four-stroke cycle single-acting Diesel engines, each coupled direct to its line of shafting; also two four- stroke cycle single-acting auxiliary Diesel engines, each coupled direct to its dynamo and air compressor. Each of the main engines has six cylinders, 261 inches (670 m.m.) in diameter and 39 inches (1,000 man.) stroke, with 105 revo- lutions per mintite, and a horse power of 1,600. The builders, Messrs. Harland and Wolff, also lia4-e similar vessels in course of construction for the Leyland Line, the Pacific Steam Navi- gation Co. and Messrs. Elder Dempster 44; Co. It will be remembered that the "Zulandia," the first ocean-going vessel of any size to be fitted with internal combustion engines surpassed even the expectation of her builders, perform- ing a long aild exacting voyage without the slightest hitch, a performance which speaks well for the motor liner of the future. Prevalence of Accidents.</p>
        <p>The motor car is still, unfortunately, the cause of far too many street accidents, both in England and elsewhere. Of course, culpability on the part of the driver only causes a very small percentage of the total, but the few reck- less ones more than counteract the honest efforts of the great majority of motorists to placate the feelings of a susceptible public and keep within the law. During the last few days reports from all parts of the country tell the same depressing story of fatalities and mishaps, many of them probably easily preventable by the exercise of I a little foresight and care on the part of the drivers and pedestrians. In the Metropolis, for example, two people were knocked down and killed by motor 'buses a couple of days ago, and another fatal accident is reported from Barns- ley, where a pedestrian, stepping carelessly into the road, was knocked down by a passing car travelling at a very moderate speed. A defective steering gear is stated to have been responsible for a serious smash at Blackpool, where a heavily-laden car collided with one of the tram- way standards. The body was torn completely off the chassis, which continued to run up a side-street, all the occupants being injured. The old dangerous practice of starting up the engine without seeing that the starting lever is out, and the brake on, was responsible for the death of a London taxi driver a day or two ago. Here is surely an opportunity for some enterprising inventor to introduce a really efficient safety device.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>FOOTBALL COUPON CASE.</head>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL COUPON CASE.</p>
        <p>Warrington Men Found "Not Guilty."</p>
        <p>The remarkable football youpon case, in which two well-known Warrington men were charged with fraud, was heard in the County Sessions at Liverpool. At the end of the second day's hearing on Wednesday a verdict of " Not guilty" was returned. The defendants, Richard Percival Walmsley (32), described as a dental mechanic, Arpley-street, and Walter Eastham (34), tanner, of Robson-street; were discharged. The allegations were that the defendants at- tempted to obtain by false pretences the sum of Ils. from Walter Page, a commission agent at Warrington. Page promoted a football coupon on January 17, and both defendants, it was stated, were interested in forecasts which were sent in on that day. It was alleged that defendants' forecasts, together with a postal order, were enclosed in an envelope, which was delivered at Page's house shortly before seven o'clock on the night of January 17. The envelope bore a postmark and was stamped 3 p.m. Page said there was no postal delivery at his house about seven o'clock. It was also alleged that an old address on the en- velope had been erased. Both defendants denied the allegations. Walms- I ley said he addressed the envelope for Eastham, who had come to see him about some teeth. After he had closed the envelope East ham sud- denly discovered the postal order had not been enclosed, and the enyelope was re-opened and then sealed. lie had no interest at all in the coupons sent to Mr. I'age by Eastham, as that day be had despatched his own coupons some two hours previously. lie denied also the statement made by a witness named Atkins, who alleged that Walmsley told him of a system whereby book- makers might be defrauded. Eastham said he posted the letter about a quarter to three, and had acted perfectly hon- estly throughout. It was a bona-fide bet, and he had no intention of defrauding Mr. page.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN CANAL TRAGEoy.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CANAL TRAGEoy.</p>
        <p>insanity" Averdict of "Suicide during temporary was returned by a Runcorn jury on Monday on Mrs. Jane Wilkinson, 68 years, widow of a retired stationer, who had lived in Widnes, and whose body had been found on Saturday in the Bridgewater Canal. Ellen Mary O'Connor said her mother lived with her at 127, Mersey-road, Widnes. The last time she saw her alive was on Friday evening, when she left her mother in the house by her- self while she went to the theatre. Mrs. Wil- kinson had been attended by the doctor, and had not been out of the house since Christnnis Day. She had been an invalid for two years. Witness could not account for her going to Run- corn. Lately she had heard her mother threaten to do away with herself.</p>
        <p>George Hayes, a lodger, said he heard :Mrs. Wilkinson say she would do away u ith herself. She had asked him to look atter btr until she died. Constable Waring stated that the bodtv was found on the top sill of one of the locks. There were no marks that suggested foul play. There was a mark over the eye and the cheek, which, he thought, had been done by a passing boat. A good deal of correspondence was found in the pockets of the clothing. One letter dated back to 1821, and related to some people born at that time. Another letter bore a date in the year 1587. •</p>
      </div>
