        <p>"PIUS"</p>
        <p>St.. OvrOS,</p>
        <p>MENACE BY ROOKS.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Chamber of Agriculture, at Chester, on Saturday, heard read a letter from Mr. R. W. Cartwright (Malpas) complaining of the damage done to crops by rooks. He sug- rested that owners of rookeries be requested to shoot old birds when sitting. The number of rooks had enormously increased of late years, and become such pests in some districts that tillage farming would have to be abandoned if something was not done to destroy the rooks. Mr. R. Shepherd (Preston Brook) said that last year the rooks attacked the corn, and made the fields look as if sheep or calves had been tres- passing. He never saw a rook on his farm with- out it was committing some depredation.</p>
