      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1914</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Boundless Resources of Our Overseas Dominions.</head>
        <p>The Boundless Resources of Our Overseas Dominions.</p>
        <p>A CANADIAN STORY.</p>
        <p>TIIE LIFE-PICTURE OF A MAN: BY MRS. C. HAMER-JACKSON.</p>
        <p>, Ten years ago a man of thirty-five stood out- side the Canadian ,Pacific Railway Station at Winnipeg, leaning against the railings lof a little open passage which led from the station to the Emigration Offices. The man's whole aspect was that of complete dejection and hopelessness. He was decently dressed, and a muffler round his neck hid the linen, which might not have been , spotless. He was evidently fighting an inward battle of some kind; for his clean-cut features showed clearly the struggle that was going on between pride and obstinacy, and the hard calls of necessity. At last he roused himself, and, setting his face towards the Emigration Offices, turned a short corner and walked into the large hall, which , has seen so many thousands of men and women from all parts of the globe, and where as many have been helped to fight and conquer in the great battle for independence. He stood against the long counter which runs right across the hall, and waited until one of the officials approached.</p>
        <p>" I give it up," he said at last, "I cannot find a clerk's position anywhere; give me what you can. I will do anything. I suppose I must un- less I starve, and it is now a whole week since 1 started tramping the Streets for a position of some sort."</p>
        <p>The official looked at him a moment, then dis- appeared for a minute, and on returning lifted the top of the counter at the side, over an entrance space, and bade him come in. He then led the man into a private office, where the Commissioner for Emigration greeted him with a smile, and, showing him a chair, said: " I am afraid you have found that I was right, after all. Well, it is so; there is no room here for clerks. All the openings are filled up by men trained in Canadian ways and means of doing business, and a man of your age, as a clerk, is difficult to place. lam afraid the only thing I can do for you is to send you to the Municipal Offices, where they want men for labouring. , You will get two and a half dollars V. day (10s.) for a start."</p>
        <p>. _ . The man had an involuntary movement of repulsion, then shrugged his shoulders, and said " All right; I'll take it." He went. A week after he was seated • on a stone heap outside Winnipeg, his lunch, which had been carefully wrapped up in paper and carried in a little wicker basket, laid in front of him. He looked at his hands, pulled a pocket-knife out of his pocket, and began the strange occupation for a street worker—to clean his nails. He was Just in the middle of this little performance when the head of the gang of workers to which he belonged passed him on his way to his own lunch. The little incident was the crowning point to a series of observations, for there are . no men so keen, so observant and so critical as the Canadians where men fresh from the Old Country are concerned, and the clean, quick, and yet thorough way in which lie had per- formed his daily duties had not remained un- noticed. A 'few days after: our man was called. into the office of the Municipal Department to which his work belonged, and after a few questions he was promoted to the position of head of a gang of workmen, getting a higher salary.</p>
        <p>Pluck, Patience and Fortune. He then began to look round, study.the ways of the country, the new systems of work, abso- lutely unknown to him. He educated himself to the level of a Canadian-born. This second stage in the development of his career lasted a few months, during which his work was always well done, his weekly reports perfect, system- atic, clear and concise, until a report of .his own doings reached higher quarters, and at last he got what he had come in search of—a clorkship in one of the Municipal Buildings. This was ten years ago. To-day this same man, after having passed all the different stages from an , ordinary clerk upwards, sits on the Municipal Board, a full-fledged councillor, who was put up at the last General Elections as a fit member of the community to become a representative of the people in the Dominion House of Par:ia- ment at Ottawa, Fortune, brought on by sheer pluck, indomit- able energy, Patience, delerMinatn to succeed at all cost, has not spoilt jam, He is the same man, as proud of the achievement of his own ig work. as he used to be of the whiteness of his hands. Proud to have been able to shake the prejudices of his race and thrown, off the ways of the Old Country, which, although they may be good in an Old Continent, are absolutely out of place in a new country, where everything iS done on the latest modern system of improve. i ng ments, where customs' have melted in the inter- national pot of the early pioneers, and where ways and means have s into existence through the very needs of th country, therefore more adapted to the comfort and welfare of the community than our old ideas can possibly be, however clever or clear-sighted one may be. I have boa 414 tO the present state of his bank balance, never asked this man, for ally informa- but he owns his own motor car, lives in a, very Picturesque Colonial home with all the com- forts which England can produce and has, in the full sense of tile word " made good," as the Canadians say. He has done nothing wonderful, lie is not an inventor or a man of science, a brilliant explorer or an artist. He is just a hard- working, persevering, man, who had to go through his disappointments, heartaches, priva tions, and even hunger, but he had the rigln spirit which the Canadians love and admire so much, realising that, as lie could not start half- way, he must begin right at the bottom of the ladder, which leads to success and wealth. That, however, does not by any means necessitate the losing of one's personality, and it is the retain- ing of that very moral superiority over the ordinary alien which leads to fortune, if .one is tactful enough to know how to use his advan- tages of education and upbringing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Canadian Ideals.</head>
        <p>Canadian Ideals.</p>
        <p>Canada has had no transition stage. As a • country she has sprung up, actually putting into practice all . the ideals which in Europe sound almost as coining from the realms of Utopia. All her streets are wide and Straight, her systems of city drainage perfect, her towns planned on paper before they are carved out of the open lire are the realisation of an imaginary modejt everything is as perfect as modern science cal' make it. Tier schools are wisely built with large, wide claSs-rooms, and playgrounds, gymnasiums and laboratories, full of light and sunshine, and are- equipped with the latest developments of scientific educa- tional systems. Here, under carefully selected Toasters, is trained the new generation of Cana- dians, which, born and bred amongst all these developments anti perfect surroundings, which are the fruits of centuries of training in older coutavies, will form the most perfect race in the history of the world, an ideal one, broad- minded, free from prejudices, with all the hap- piness born out of contentment and freedom, and with perfect health, which is the result of that splendid open-air life and ideardry, invig- orating and sunshiny climate which is the only secret of Canada's immense' wealth and con- tinual prosperity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ONTARIO.</head>
        <p>ONTARIO.</p>
        <p>THE KIND OF WOMEN TIIAT THIS PROVINCE NEEDS.</p>
        <p>"Ontario, this year, is still needing more British girls," says Miss Grace Kennedy, of the London office of the Ontario Government—more girls of the good old stock who are helping to build up Canada. " Women do well in Ontario; for the capable and adaptable domestic there are good oppor- tunities for advancement. As to the kind of woman Ontario needs, it has been said that we are endeavouring to persuade girls to go to Ontario who would otherwise have been in a comfortable situation or home, . perfectly willing to stay here. where a girl is with an assured income and no need of thought for the ' future, it is not our desire to persuade such a one to remove. But that there are in England ; numbers of clever domesticated women who are born home makers with no possible outlet for their energies on this side other than that of earning a mere pittance by working for someoae else, I think no sensibly-minded person will deny, and it is to those girls that Ontario offers a chance—a home; an opportunity for advance- ment and a hope that some day they their place among the real home makers of Canada. may take m</p>
        <p>There are several parties going out this year under the direct auspices of the Ontario Gov- ernment.. Upon arrival,in Toronto, the girls are given 24 hours' free board and lodging at the Women's Welcome Hostel, and during that time they are able to meet the prospective mis- tresses and decide upon their new homes. The practice of the Ontario Government Office here is to obtain references and other particulars usually sufficient to give the mistresses a good indication as to the kind of girls who are com- ing' and their abilities, &amp;c. This information reaches Toronto some thile before the girls arrive, and the mistresses look over the paliera and choose the girl, so that on arrival the girl only has to have a brief interview with the mis- tress who is already acquainted with her quali- fications, and the engagement is made."</p>
        <p>Care of the Newcomer. Having decided in which province of Canada YOU will make your home, the next important thing is to consider the arrangements made by the Government for providing you with work and looking after your interests. In this con- nection some interesting details are given by Mr. J. M. Clark, who is the principal officer of the Distribution Branch of the Ontario Coloni- sation Department, stationed at Toronto. "Every applicant for employment as a farm hand in Ontario," he says, " is required to fur- nish information as to his age, experience and requirements. From the farmers we get from 5,000 to 6,000 applications a year for labourers. It is our business to send the right labourer to the right farmer. Each farm-help gets a card ,of introduction to a farmer. Married men 'ire never sent off without first telephoning the farmers to whom they are going, to meet them. Thus we, do away with the chance of a family getting by some accident stranded. On the back of each card of introduction is the address and telephone number of our office and instructions to telephone in case of any mishap. We do not lose sight of the men until they have been at work for 12 months, when we ,consider they ought to be well able, to do without any assist- ance or advice. Of course it sometimes happens that a man, although a good workman, does not get on with the, farmer to whom he has been sent. In such cases the men are asked to coin- ' municate with us, and we find them fresh posi- I tions.</p>
        <p>Farming as a Career for Young Men. There can be no question that Canada offers magnificent opportunities. It is surprising to find that so many people refuse to accept them, and "rub along" discontentedly in the same narrow groove, under the impression that to go to the Dominion necessarily implies undertak- ing strenuous pioneer work, . amid wild and desolate surroundings, with adequate reward for one's toil as only a remote possibility. The reverse, however, on investigation, will be found to exist. Given that the intendtng settler is not too proud to .seek the advice of people who know the country, and by their aid select a suitable locality, he is assured of a free, open, healthy life and pleasant conditions, with Plenty of scope for his energy and enterprise, an intEresting occupation and ample reward for his industry. Moreover, he will have the satis- faction of knowing that in a great and growing country, such as. Canada, his children will have by far brighter prospects than if they were in over-crowded England. Professions Over-crowded. It is little use putting your son into business nowadays, anti many a young mall's prospects have been ruined by lack of foresight in choos- ing for him a career. Why not set him up with a farm in Canada? Mr. N. B. Coleock (late Agent-General for Ontario) offers a unique opportunity to gentle- men to start their sons in a profitable manlier on farms in one of the finest parts of Ontario. All the properties are paying concerns, with no development work to be done, and are, of course, freehold. The prices 'are very moderate, and include house, Outbuildings, &amp;c. The purchase price, can be arranged . to be spread over an agreed time, so that only a minimum of capital is required. One of the farms (80 acres) last Year made a net profit of over £2,400, and, •as the lands throughout the district are increasing lin value, it can be confidently anticipated that an increase of 25 per cent. to 50 per cent. will be shown in the value of the farms in a few years, so that this opportunity, besides offering the young man a fine career, also offers an excellent investment. Write for illustrated booklet and full parti- culars to Canadian Freeholds, Limited, 46, Kingsway, London, W.C. .</p>
        <p>ESTABLISHED FARMS IN otormleeisls hIigSOuTHERN ONTARIO. The prospect of having to clear land before it call be tilled is seldom attractive to the agricul- turist in the Old Country, who has been accus- allliiiisstlaiftee toofdeetatiltiuv•aittliiofna, and ready at in a more any time for the plough, the roller, or the sot- ing machine. _ . .</p>
        <p>Tree-felling and stump-grubbing is admittedly laborious and tedious work, and even the, fenc- ing of a farm, and the erection of a homestead and the necessary outbuildings occupies minuet' time and involves such an outlay that the pros- pective settler with a limited capital may well hesitate before ibarking on the enterprise. Drawbacks of this nature are, nevertheless, by no means inseparable from settlement on the land in certain parts of our Overseas Empire. In Canada, and notably in Southern Ontario, there are abundant opportunities for the acqui- sition at a moderate inclusive price of estab- lished farms, equipped with convenient dwel- ling houses and the needful barns and shelters, and upon which the newly-arrived settler may start right away with cultural operations, with- out loss of time.</p>
        <p>Amongst the a4vantages wlinli Southern Ontario presents to the farmer accustomed to</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>QUEBEC.</head>
        <p>QUEBEC.</p>
        <p>LORD STRATHCONA'S PRACTICAL AID TO EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Gifts to McGill University total £700,000. The magnificent educational system of the Province of Quebec is in no small measure due to the splendid example set by the late Lord Strath. cona, whose gifts to McGill University, from first to last, have exceeded £700,000. His be- quest of „t 200,000 to the Royal Victoria College, Montreal, though subject to deduction of pay- ments made during his lifetime„ is in addition' to the sum of about £BO,OOO e4ended on the college buildings and site. His ffiterest in the educational institutions of the eimmercial capital of Canad/i was very keen and entirely unsectarian. He took an active interest in the co-operative scheme put in operation in 1912 by the Montreal Theological 'Colleges affiliated with McGill, and in the sum Mer of 1913 contri- buted £25,000 to a half-million dollar (e 100,000 fund raised by the friends of the four institu- tions concerned Anglican, Presbyterian, , Methodist and Congregational—to place the joint work upon a perthanent basis. With a University such as McGill, which takes rank ' in the New World with Harvard and Yale; and with a total of 6,760 schools, colleges and uni- versities, Quebec may well be proud of her educational system, and thankful for the im- Petas given to education in this Province by the generous benefactions of Lord Strathcona.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Quebec Wants Emigrants.</head>
        <p>Quebec Wants Emigrants.</p>
        <p>A number of the Quebec Legislative Assembly recently declared that there are thousands of acres of land in Eastern townships of Quebec which are not productive because the Western farmers are taking away the Eastern labourers. Every year it is getting harder to obtain farm help in this province. Important legislative proposals are now under consideration, how- ever, which will, if carried into effect, give a new impetus to colonisation in Quebec, and enable the province to keep within her own borders numbers of the immigrants now going West.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Cafe System to Replace Saloons.</head>
        <p>Cafe System to Replace Saloons.</p>
        <p>In pursuance of a consistent progressive policy, the Provincial Legislature of Quebec are now considering a measure which has as one of its important features the gradual replaeing of the drinking bar by the cafe system. There is also a clause in the Bill prohibiting the adver- tising or sale of any intoxicating liquor "by means of suggestive illustrated advertisements having a - tendency to induce the public to be- . lieve that the cse.of intoxicants is beneficial to mind or, body." The Bill provides for the reduc- tion of Montreal's hotel and restaurant licences, and retail liquor shop licences to 350 of each class. At present they number respectively 473 and 548. The provisions of the Bill closely fol- low the recommendation of the-Royal Commis-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Satisfactory Farming Operations.</head>
        <p>Satisfactory Farming Operations.</p>
        <p>_ Farming Operations in the Province of Quebec •during ,the year 1913 were attended with .most satisfactory results. High prices were obtained for all farm produce, and the money results •to the farmer surpassed all anticipations. Hay, which is one of the principal crops of this pro- vince, was harvested in excellent condition, and turned out an exceptionally good erbp on the whole. .The reduction of the United States duty on bay to two dollars per ton resulted in in- creased exports to American points at remuner- ative prices. The various cereals were up to the average and brought good prices. Root crops were generally satisfactory, especially Potatoes, which brought high prices, and in many districts were a record crop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Maple Sugar and Tobacco.</head>
        <p>Maple Sugar and Tobacco.</p>
        <p>The output of maple sugar in the Province of I Quebec, Canada, is about 14,300,000 pounds per annum. The amount produced last year was fully up to the average, with excellent prices. The tobacco crop, as a.Whole, was satisfactory, of about the same quality as in 1912, with prices on the whole well maintained. A few instances of damage by frost are reported, but the greater part of the crop was harvested in good condition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Quebec's Home Markets.</head>
        <p>Quebec's Home Markets.</p>
        <p>Increased home consumption of cheese and butter .causes decline in exports. The total ex- ports of cheese from the Province of Quebec. Canada, during last year, were 1,571,165 boxes, decrease of 151,856 when compared with 1912. and a decrease of 239,501 when compared with 1911. There was litgain little or no export busi- ness in butter, only 1,728 boxes being shipped. Increased home consumption accounts for the decrease in the exports of both commodities. This great increase in the home consumption of these commodities is partly attributable to the increase in the wopulation, and partly to the hoom in prosperity which the Province of Quebec has been enjoying for several years past. Last year no less than 16,000 immigrants settled In this province.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ONTARlO—continued.</head>
        <p>ONTARlO—continued.</p>
        <p>the mildness of the British climate, may, he mentioned the freedom of the territory from , great extremes of heat and cold, due to the fact that it is situated in the same latitude as Southern Prance, while the rainfall is amply sufficient for .the growing of luxuriant crops,l thus avoiding any expense for artificial irriga- tion. The social life of the district is fully equal to rural lite in England, while communi- cation with the cities is well provided for by the several railway systems which traverse the country, and which, besides affording travelling facilities for the farmer and his family, give hini cheap and ready access to the best markets for everything he has to sell in the way of grain, market garden stuff, meat, poultry, eggs, and dairy produce.</p>
        <p>The Ontario Agricultural College at Guelph is claimed to be the finest and best-equipped institution of its kind in the world, and its ad- vantages are placed at the disposal of all who care to avail themselves of them. Experimental stations have been established throughout the province, 'with offiCial. instructors located in nearly every county, .</p>
        <p>Land in Southern Ontario is al present quite moderate in nice, although for some time past there has been a decid4upward move in values. If, however, the settler secures an established or unproved farm in the first instance, his hold- ing is not only actually productive from the first, but he has the satisfaction of knowing that, apart from the labour and manure lie puts into it, its market value is all the time steadily increasing, so that, should he desire later oil to sell, lie will be certain to be able to rtiiiise VOU- si de r ably more for his land than Oat he gave for it.</p>
        <p>Freehold fruit, dairy, or mixed farms situated in the most favourable portion of Ontario aro now obtainable on advantageous terms by apply- Ng to, Canadian Freeholds, Ltd:, KingswaY Cliambers,•l6, Kingovay, London, W.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>If you are lookimi for a splendid future\ for yourself or your family write us for our free illustrated booklet on " Ready Established Farms "ill beautiful Southern Ontario. Each farm is complete with good house and outbuildinus, is ready for full mill% ation and good</p>
        <p>profits are assured from the first year's \soil:.</p>
        <p>You cannot allord to neglect this opportunity.</p>
        <p>ENQUIRE NOW FROM</p>
      </div>
