      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>kaiXAMJNER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE kaiXAMJNER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1914.</p>
        <p>NEW</p>
        <p>The Unlimited Opportunities They Offer to the Settler.</p>
        <p>"BACK TO THE LAND."</p>
        <p>ALBERTA'S PRACTICAL EFFORT TO SOLVE THE PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>It has always been a difficult problem, "the placing of the inexperienced man on the land." It is so attractive that thousands would take the matter up quite seriously if they could be shown any way of becoming successful. It is therefore interesting to know that there is a really practical effort being made in the Province of Alberta, Canada, to solve this problem.</p>
        <p>An English gentleman of many years' farm- ing experience in the West has undertaken to settle a large tract of land of some fourteen thousand acres, and in the centre of this land he is erecting a model farm, on which he will reside. One can thus buy land close to this farm and have the advantage not only of seeing exactly how such a farm is run on strictly ' business lines, but at all times one can have the advice and help of the Model Farm Superin- tendent, which will enable one to get experience on one's own farm, instead of sweating for some- body else. Readers who are interested will see particulars of this scheme in the advertisenieut of Messrs. Hunt and Carleton on this page.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>"THE LAND OF THE SKY,"</head>
        <p>"THE LAND OF THE SKY,"</p>
        <p>A RICH COUNTRY THAT IS SERVED BY THE SOUTHERN RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>Where the sun shines brightly, but not op- pressively, where the days are warm, but not too hot, and the nights are cool, where scenic attrac- tions are many and both summer and winter resorts abound, that is a good land to live in or visit as a tourist.</p>
        <p>In the south-eastern section of the United States of North America, traversed by the Southern Railway system, is every facility for pleasant and enjoyable winter and summer life; populous cities, great seaports, seashore and mountain resorts, picturesque sea islands, and quiet country places; hills, dales, valleys, high mountains and gently rolling plains; a great variety of scenery.</p>
        <p>In this district is the far-famed "Land of the Sky"—a peculiar title, but one fully justified. It has a delightful climate, suitable for the athlete, sportsman, or invalid, and likewi,e for the home-seeker. It is situated further south on the globe than Southern Italy, yet within about 24 hours' rail journey from New York City. Its southern latitude tempers its winters and robs its summers of extreme heat, thus pro- ducing an invigorating atmosphere which lures the tourist to outdoor exercise and enables the farmer to grow many crops profitably. Here the traveller has a new attraction for every day in the year, and the agriculturist something to do for profit each day. "The Land of the Sky" is that portion of western North Carolina lying between the Blue Ridge Mountains and the ranges known as 'ton, Smoky, and Unaka of Eastern Tennessee. Asheville, North Carolina, is its central city, and the radiating point of a complete circle of attractions of this favoured region. 'II ere are any number of delightful resorts near Asheville, and at a station called Black Mountain, 13 miles from that city, passengers leave the Southern Railway for Mount Mitchell, 6,711 feet (about 2,040 metres) above the sea, the highett peak east of the Rockies. The Southern Railway system begins at Wash- ington, D.C. (" District of Columbia"), the capi- tal city of the United States, and with its allied lines extend from the Atlantic Ocean on the east to the Misissippi River on the west, and from the Potomat River on the north to the American shores of the Gulf of Mexico on the south.</p>
        <p>The system and it associated lines reach nearly all the fertile farming districts, the finest forest areas, the rich deposits of coal, iron and other minerals, and the most valuable regions for manufacturing, and progressive cities and towns in Virginia, North Carolina, South Carolina, Georgia, Florida, Mississippi, Kentucky, Alabama, Tennessee, Southern Illinois, Southern Indiana, and the chief South- Eastern Atlantic and Gulf of Mexico ports. The Land and Industrial Department of the Southern Railway system has been in operation for twenty years, and is .in a position to give Prompt and valdable advice to anyone seeking new land locations, homes, or manufacturing sites in any of the States traversed by the lines of that railway system. The company has no land of its own for sale and it and its agents are not interested in any way in the proceeds from sales of ;ands. The railway company has now permanently on view daily (free) a fine exhibit of grains, grasses, &amp;c., which were grown on the cele- brated Biltmore Estate Farm in North Caro- lina. Call and see it, or write for Booklet N 0.6, "Facts" (13 illustrations and map), copies of . illustrated magazine kwith map, "The Southern Field') and other printed matter always on hand and sent free and post-paid.—Southern Railway Co., Whitehall House, 119 and 30, Char- ing Cross, London, S.W.</p>
        <p>_ .</p>
        <p>MERRYWEATHERS'</p>
        <p>FM Stock, Poultry and lick raising, Dairying, Fruit grow— ing, Truck Crop; Alfalfa yields and General Farming, the SOUTHEASTERN STATES present soil, climatic and market condi— tions unsurpassed by any other region. Farm land values are most tempting. Undeveloped tracts cell frota SO an acre up; improved properties range from 820 to 850 per =re. THE SOUTHERN RAILWAY Mobile &amp; Ohio Railroad or Georgia Southern &amp; Florida Railway will help you find a desirable farm location where two and three crops grow annually. Alfalfa produces 4to 6 tons, Corn yields 60 to 100 btr., Truck crops make $lOO to $4OO, Apple Orchards $lOO to $ 500 per acrew and Beef and Pork are produced at 3 to 4 eta, per lb. Let us know in what state and branch of farming you are interested; Information given and free publications furnished on applicationt The Southeirn Railway Co. (U.S.A.), No. 23. VVfeteha ra.. Charing Cloolge. London. SW</p>
        <p>leap. ro table Farm Lands • .-A WV ; 4, -1 /41ANY OF THESE I BOOKLETS FREE • *144,44 C."°°"-„,_ ".;,.."1,,1A • FLORIDA r",..:io"'""k , efismehmjim: '• •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>BRITISH COLUMBIA.</head>
        <p>BRITISH COLUMBIA.</p>
        <p>" TIIE ORCHARD- OF THE EMPIRE."</p>
        <p>British Columbia has been aptly described as the "Orchard of the Empire." Its land and climate alike are eminently suitable for agri- culture, horticulture, and floriculture, and its apples have secured many awards in the United States and in Great Britain. The fruit-growing lands of the province are of large extent, and include all the fertile valleys from the Rocky Mountains to the Pacific Coast, and from the International boundary north to Cariboo. Apples, pears, cherries, plums and small fruits grow over the area mentioned, whilst peaches, grapes, and other delicate fruits are cultivated to perfection in the Southern districts. Dairy- ing is an important British Columbia industry; which is still in its infancy. It pays hand- somely, and the co-operative and private cream- eries already established are doing well and earning satisfactory dividends. Markets are near at hand, and the local prices for poultry and eggs are so high as to place them in the class of luxuries. The enormous growth of population ensures a corresponding increase in the demand for dairying products.</p>
        <p>A Great Dairying Country. The Government affords financial aid in the establishment of co-operative creameries and cheese factories. The province possesses all the conditions necessary to constitute it a great dairying country. There are extensive areas of pastoral land in the interior, whilst there is a plentiful supply of good water and luxuriant and nutritious grasses.</p>
        <p>British Columbia is one of the best countries in the world for the farn;ier with some capital. and for all classes of agricultural workers. The estimated production from agriculture in 1913 is 21,000,000 dollars, and proof of the pros- perity of this Canadian province is afforded by the fact that the production from its five lead- ing industries are estimated to be equal to over 300 dollars for every man, woman and child in the country. In addition to agriculture, British Columbia is the mining province of Canada, has important fisheries, and unlimited forest wealth, whilst the scenery is amongst the finest in the world.</p>
        <p>State-aid to Agriculturists and Domestic Servants.</p>
        <p>The Agent-General for British Columbia, Mr. J. H. Turner, has received the following cable- gram from Hon. Sir Richard Mcßride, K.C.M.G., Premier of the Province:—</p>
        <p>"The report of the Royal Commission on Agriculture was presented yesterday. 3n the Legislature, recommending the establishment of an Agricultural Credit Commission. "The Commissioner to be empowered to grant loans from 250.00 dollars to 10,000.00 tiollars on the productive value of improved land, with loans under 2,000.00 dollars having priority; loans not to exceed sixty per cent, of the esti- mated productive value. "The Commissioner to be appointed for life by the Lieutenant-Governor in Council. "The maintenance of the present protective tariff on agricultural produce is further recom- mended.</p>
        <p>"It is proposed that State-aid shall be given to agricultural immigrants and domestic ser- vants, and that resident farm schools shall be established for Barnardo Homes' immigrants, aided by assisted passages. It is proposed that State-aid shall be given for the extension of rural telephones. " The principle of co-operation and aiding central markets is urged. "It is suggested there shall be a separate portfolio for agriculture, and that the Govern- ment shall select areas for closed settlement with small holdings on the Australian plan, and the extension of agricultural education in rural elementary schools and high schools is also sug- gested.</p>
        <p>"The report is to be considered next session."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>SALVATION ARMY</head>
        <p>SALVATION ARMY</p>
        <p>ADVISE THAT PEOPLE SHOULD NOT FLOCK TO THE TOWNS.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding that there has been a falling off of about 50 per cent. in the inquiries from some of the districts from which the conducted parties to Canada organised by the Emigration Department of the Salvation Army have been made up in recent years, the numbers of the first party sailing on the 21st of February were completed, and the accommodation for the earlier sailings generally is gradually filling up, the Army guaranteeing work to all who go out under its direction. Latest reports from the Salvation Army Im- migration Officers in Canada state that as far as agricultural labour is concerned, the demand , for skilled and unskilled workers if anything 11 exceeds that of previous years, and for people prepared to settle on the land the prospects I have never been more encouraging. This, they state, is due naturally to the increase of the population and the consequent call for a greater food supply-, while the progressive extension of railway enterprise is opening up new land for cultivation. While admitting that to some extent the temporary distress in the greater cities this winter has been unusually severe through the slackness in various industries caused in the earlier part of last year by the money strin- gency, the Army Emigration Authorities point out that they have always discouraged people from flocking to the large towns, advising them that the surer way to success lay in the occupa- tion of the land or settlement round the grow- ing but comparatively recently formed town- ships.</p>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL DEVELOPMENT IN ONTARIO</p>
        <p>Industry, apart from agriculture, is forging ahead in Ontario. The province will possess the second largest steel centre in the world, the proposal of the United Steel Corporation being to erect at Sandwich a plant that will cover 1,000,000 acres and cost A:4,000,000. The town of Welland, Ontario, is expected soon to rank as one of the cotton centres of the country. The latest industry to decide to locate there is the Toronto Cotton Company, a company with a capital of 200,000 dollars subscribed. The site is to be five acres and the main factory will have a floor space of 82,000 feet, while the floor space of the boiler house will be 10,000 feet. The company agrees to commence the manufacture of cotton cloths in the building on or before October Ist, 1914. It is interesting to note the practical encouragement given by Ontario towns and cities to new industries, as, for instance, those embodied in the proposals made by the company, which are, of course, to be submitted to the electors. The agreement provides that Welland is to pay 00 per cent, of the cost of the , buildings and site, and the company 10 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>NE W ZEALAND.</head>
        <p>NE W ZEALAND.</p>
        <p>'WHAT FIFTY YEARS MEANS IN A NEW COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>Some idea of what fifty years' industry repre- sents in a new sountry may be gathered from an interesting census compiled in Auckland, a flourishing commercial city of New Zealand, and a semi-tropical centre of great beauty. Naturally the discovery of refrigeration has revolutionised the export trade in the Dominion and trade. As late as 1873 she was sending a , snilyowtsoa gigantic increase in her the value of .088, and cheese t:t ltaolktelnried only of .016, whilst Auckland sold in the overseas markets in 1912, .033,965 worth of butter, and .836,796 worth of cheese. In now m i 63 she export of meat was undreamt of, and w coming to the front in the export of mutton, lamb and beef. Fifty years ago the Province of Auckland had something over 50,000 acres under grass, while to-day she has more than 3,500,000. Fifty years ago Auckland Possessed 58,000 sheep, 31,700 cattle, 3,839 horses, 11,461 Pigs. To-day she is mistress of over 2,000,000 sheep, close on 800,000 cattle, 120,000 horses, about 100,000 pigs. Fifty years ago the occupied dings of farm lands of the Auckland I'rovincehdoildnot exceed 97,000 acres, to-day there are nearly 8,000,000 acres under occupa- tion. Nevertheless, as a recent writer expert pointed out, "but never in the past were there anything like the favourable conditions that exist now. The insatiable demand for food-stuffs in the countries of the Old World and in : America, the high prices offering for all kinds of farm produce, have never before been Paralleled."</p>
        <p>RE m p oyed "' Casuals." that is afoot for a novelty in Commissions must present the Dominion of New 'coAalamncol:s7imaeoesgn Regularly alsonit rmething of a dockers' paradise to the men here at the London waterside who are thankful enough, actual thousands of them, for two days' work each week. It is proposed by one of the principal Harbour Boards, that in each of the major ports in New Zealand there shoji be set oh a Standing Committee, or Corn_ mission.of nine members, representing the fol- lowing interests :—Harbour Board, ocean-going, inter-colonial, and coastal vessels, coal workers, railway department (one each), waterside labourers (two), and a chairman, who should be a stipendiary- magistrate, representing the Gov- ernment. The duties of this body would be to determine the amount of casual labour required from month to month at each season of the year and employ that number of hands at a minimum w waterside labour to be engaged from thlertlgeoMmission, and none other employed till the panel was exhausted. The Commission could also inquire into and adjudicate on all complaints against or by the labourers, and have power to keep the lists purged of undesirables, provision to be made for a provident fund and accident insurance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Labour Exchanged for. Gum.</head>
        <p>Labour Exchanged for. Gum.</p>
        <p>gum are e mS beddedo ils awrlei i c fl ol u lnadr g et 0 q In) ea n anythingt it ie s o f but bad agricultural land in New Zealand. An interesting system of exchanging commo- dity for labour exists in connection with this industry. Since it has been found that the fer- tility of gum soil amply justifies cultivation, teamsters have also been found who will readily the ground to do furrow the land if they are allowed all the gum that is brought to light. There are many in- stances in which the gum found 1)3 ploughing ohvaesr, beenfor sfsufficient, engt tfoorpafeyrtfiolriseturrns arid seed the area treated. It has been found that turnips make a fine initial crop, and that the yields are surprisingly good. Gumfields soil is also pecu- liarly favourable to tree growth, pine, and the black wattle that is such all asset to New Zea- land farmers especially flourishing in it. Such successful experiments have also been made with apple and peach trees that some owners have decided to extend their orchards and plant every variety of marketable fruit. Oats and results. grasses also yield highly encouraging</p>
        <p>Curious Trap for Deception. An extraordinary case of deception on the part of a wholesale trader was cabled out to New Zealand from London recently. It appears that while a grocer in Sunderland was cutting through a cheese, some weeks ago, his knife en- countered a hard substance. On hacking out the obstruction, he found that it was a small tin box contaming some pennies, with a strip of paper, on which was written a request that the finder should communicate with a certain New Zealand manufacturer and inform him whether the cheese was sold as New Zealand or Canadian made. The grocer has since replied, stating that the cheese was sold to him as a Canadian article.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>CANADIAN INVESTMENTS.</head>
        <p>CANADIAN INVESTMENTS.</p>
        <p>The Alberta Loan and Investment Company, Limited, which has offices in Canada, in the cities of Calgary, Edmonton, Lethbridge, Macleod, and Vancouver, 8.C., has opened an office e directlyin London with he n itto etnablieeteascionmPany to deal mg de in ads of British . investors for first-class Canadian securities. The directors of the London Board are Mr. Robert Logan, Sir Gilbert Parker, m.p., Colonel Sir Gerard Smith, K.C.M.G., and the London office of the company is at Trafalgar Buildings, company are Northumberland -avenue, -aven, London, W.C. re'frehremsiertNoiceisnunadnertaalntb,isemytheent in to-day's "Examiner."</p>
        <p>STUDENTS OF BOOKS AND MEN. Some of the best men and most successful men I know, says Lord Rosebery, have had no pleas- ure in reading at all, but they have had to re- place it—they have chosen to replace it—with the society of the best men and a-omen they could find, and I am not at all sure, fond of books as I am, if that be not a better and more Auccessful education than books themselves. But at any rate, a combination of the two is the ideal. Remember this, that books are good friends and good servants, but bad masters. The bookworm who embeds himself in his books in the solitude of his library feeds on his lonely mulberry leaf for all eternity without producing any silk at all. I would earnestly warn those most enthusiastic readers, lest they allow their besetting delight to become something like a besetting sin. Man was riot meant to live by books alone, but the opportunity of reading, of plucking and culling, of selecting a literature for yourself is one of the greatest boons which poor humanity can conceive.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Spencer Greenway, lord of the manor for Darwen, has received an appointment as attache to the British Embassy at St. Peters- burg. lie is only 22 years of age, and succeeded to the Darwen estates, which have been in the family over 120 years, in 1901,</p>
      </div>
