      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>LANCASHIRE CRICKET.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CRICKET.</p>
        <p>The appeal which Lord Derby Makes in our correspondence columns to-day must not go un- heeded by lovers of outdoor games in general, and of the finest of all outdoor games in par- ticular. For generations Lancashire has occu- pied a proud position in•the cricket field. Her champions have not, perhaps, been able to reach the top of the tree a. 4 often as they and their admirers would have liked, but they have always been sufficiently formidable to be a force that had to be reckoned with, and not even Yorkshire, with its broad acres and districts that produce cricketers almost spontaneously, has been able to show the slightest unconcern for .the sister county across the Pennines. But the Lancashire Club stands in need of money, and Lancashire being a rich county, and a proud one withal, can scarcely let a game in which she is pre-eminent languish on that account. There was a deficit last year of £l,OOO and Lord Derby is at the head of a movement which aims at seeing that deficits do not ham- per the movements of the executive in their efforts to put the best possible talent into the field. The aim of the committee, for whom Lord Derby writes—their " ambition " his Lord- ship aptly puts it— is to raise £15,000, which will liquidate afl. annual charge of £650 interest on mortgage on the ground, and to induce 1,500 additional ladies and gentlemen to become mem- bers. As there are now fewer members than there were ten years ago by 750, it should not be difficult for the committee to realise their ambition. There must be plenty of ground upon which to work. The list his Lordship sends us shows that donations amounting to £1,707, and annual subscriptions for three or five years, amounting to £B5l, have already been paid or promised. - Sir Gilbert Greenall, who is on the committee, has given £l5 15s. as a life member. Lord Lilford has given £lOO. The ordinary subscription is 265. a year, and the entrance fee is for the present suspetided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>u THE LIT. AND PHIL."</head>
        <p>u THE LIT. AND PHIL."</p>
        <p>On Monday night the members of the War- rington Literary and Philosophical Society will assemble in the Cairo-street School for the pur- pose of holding thZir annual meeting. "The Lit. and Phil." has long been an institution of the town. Papers read by its members and in- vited contributors, carefully preserved for generations in nicely pfinted volumes, reveal how accurately the society has kept pace with the learned movements of the times. As a matter of fact, these local records give in many cases a remarkable forecast of what we now know was to be realised in the course of time. Unfortunately the society is in a stationary con- dition as to membership. One would have thought that an increasing population woTild have provided an increasing roll of members of such a useful society. Has the rush for flimsy excitements, that appear to be the charactu- istic of the present day, affected adversely the thoughtful section of the community, so that that secTioh may be said to be at a stand, so far as growth is concerned? Or has the multiplica- tion of kindred societies been a drain upon the supply of material that twenty years ago would have gravitated towards our older friend? Dr. Manson, the devoted secretary, is quite pre- pared to argue upon and prove the prior claims of the ." Lit. and Phil." upon all who are inter- ested in subjects that are taken into its special keeping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>PERMANENT BUILDING SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>PERMANENT BUILDING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A 41 per cent. Dividend and more Promised Next Year.</p>
        <p>A fi.e per cent. dividend! That is what was promised for next year at the 47th annual ing Society, held on Thursday evening n meet- ing of the Warrington Permanent Benefit Buittle- i Cairo .1-street Schools. The President, Mr. W. . otacwia,lsp,rweseirdeedp,raesnednta.ll Athveodtierecotfeoorns Passed to Mrs. Clive, widow of the late secre- tary,'ilwolietinlc and the Chairman eulogised on the oet‘hvaers pains- taking labours Mr. Clive had given the society. Mr. .1. Wilkinson is the present secretary. The annual report stated that the reserve funds of the society exceeded .22,000. The net Profit on the year's working amounted to £761 138. 10d., and the amount brought forward from last year's accounts was £B4 65., making a total Profit of £845 19s. 10d. for distribution, out of which the directors proposed to pay a dividend of 4 per cent. (free of income tax), to carry for- ward the sum of £lOO to next year's account, and Place the balance fund." to a "dividend equalisation The Chairman moved the adoption of the re- port, and said he hoped to be able to declare a dividend of 5 per cent, next year—(applause)— which would be money actually earned. The sales of profits had been successful. They had advanced .83,025 on mortes, which was more than ever before gag . There were only six proper- ties in possession, and practically each sale had meant a profit. (Applause.) one Mr. fFatihresecoaudnidtedors,anidn Msfir.p7ort. Ilin.g7.sooaiddcotefike, society was in a strong financial position and showed no weak places. The report was adopted. The following retiring directors were re- elected: Messrs. T. Ince, Thos. Partridge, and Richard Shackleton. -Mr. Aquila Cook is the treasurer; Mr. William Twiss, surveyor, and Mr. Woodcock and air. B. Bradshaw are the auditors.. Totes of thanks, proposed by Mr. Milling and Mr. Taylor, closed the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>PASSIM WEEK.</p>
        <p>" WORMS " !</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton, evidently taking his cue from the suffragettes or the Ulster civil warriors, advocated drastic measures when dis- cussing the education budget at Tuesday night's Council meeting. The education rate continues to mount up, much to the concern of Councillor Pemberton, for two reasons. He thinks the Education Committee is composed of a set of education fanatics, whose extravagance should be checked by the Council, but he also com- plains that the Board of Education persists in heaping burdens upon the country for which there is no legal warrant. Considering that we , are a lot of worms for carrying out these orders, he proposed to bring about a deadlock by stop- ping subplies and defying the Government. He moved that the estimates for the year be not accepted—just to see what the .Council ,were Made of. All but three risked being called worms rather than take the extreme step advo- cated, and the estimates went through. On a subsequent school building discussion Council- lor Pembertoh succeeded in attracting Dr. Fox to his side, a rather notable achievement, as the doctor had challenged him to give a specific instance of extravagance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL BAZAAR.</head>
        <p>A SUCCESSFUL BAZAAR.</p>
        <p>The bazaar held last week in aid of the funds of St. John's Presbyterian Church was highly successful, £532 being raised at the three days' sales. It is understood that this sum will be considerably enlarged by certain returns yet to be made. Some goods remained unsold, and it is hoped that by the promotion of a small sale of work later in the year the total sum aimed at (£750) will be fully realised. As will be seen from our advertisement columns, the people of St. John's gratefully thank all who assisted in making the bazaar such a brilliant success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>N.U. CuP DEFEAT:</head>
        <p>THE N.U. CuP DEFEAT:</p>
        <p>The Nvarrington Club met with a nasty down- fall in the first round of the N.U. Cup. After playing a drawn game, which should never have so resulted, at home, they went to Batley and met with a one-point defeat. The critics are agreed, however, that Warrington were the better team in' the re-play, and that Batley had the luck. That is a small crumb of comfort. Warrington this season seem to have lost their cup-fighting abilities. The nasty defeat at the hands of Barrow in the Lancashire Cup at Wilderspool must be fresh in the memory, and now the N.U. Cup defeat comes even as a worse blow. The only good that has emanated from it is that Warrington have got a good share of the £433 taken as " gate " money. Hard cash is supposed to be a solatium for injured feelings, but it can scarcely console Warringtonians for the Batley defeat. It has been stated that the honorary members did not pay to witness Sat- urday's match like all the rest of the public. That statement. is an erroneous and unaccount- able statement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>ALMOST A P.S.A. MIRACLE.</head>
        <p>ALMOST A P.S.A. MIRACLE.</p>
        <p>The members of the Wycliffe P.S.A. have been rejoicing over their society's coming of age, and well might they do so, for it has not been given to all such bodies to reach their 21st birthday, for some may come and some do go. This at Wycliffe seems destined to go on for ever. It was founded in almost a miraculous fashion—as the Rev. John Yonge narrated on Monday. A few minutes before the first meet- ing was timed to start, 21 years ago, only 50 persons were in the church, and it looked as if the meeting would prove a fiasco. Then the tide turned, and a huge wave of humanity, esti,' mated at GOO men, swept into the church, and the P.S.A. was launched successfully. It reached the high-water mark -within the suc- ceeding years, then through churches and chapels imitating the Wycliffe example, the membership declined somewhat. Still it has gone on for the 21 years. It is now better organised than ever, and we see no reason why it should not reach its jubilee in the excellent work the P.S.A. performs. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WARBURTON WORTHY.</head>
        <p>WARBURTON WORTHY.</p>
        <p>"An Old Journalist writes : The honourable discharge from bankruptcy reported under last week's Lymm news reminded me of one in particular among many altogether different ones that have come under my notice. The debtor hung on as long as he could borrow from relatives and intimate friends. These, having no reason to suspect that he was in diffi- culties, were easily induced by plausible.stories and specious promises to part vvith their hard- earned money. When the unexpected crash came, several poor folk were involved in the ruin, and it was generally supposed that the unscrupulous borrower would be. severely handled in the Bankruptcy Court. We reporters confidently anticipated sensational " copy " for the local papers, but the Official Receiver, who was usually stern and severe, treated the aston- ished debtor with extreme gentleness, and, whitewashed in double-quick time, the fellow was in the end almost obsequiously bowed out of court. He seemed without knowing it to have hypnotised the very officials in his favour. The Difference.</p>
        <p>The late Sir W. Snagge, who died the other week in Oxfordshire, as a County Court judge on a Yorkshire circuit once confided to me that he had had a good deal to do with the drafting of an amended Bankruptcy Act, but I. thought then, and I think still, that such efforts have left much to be desired. As a capitalist saisl bitterly, apropos of, a dishonest liquidation* wherein he had been caught, " There are pessi- mists who say that marriage is failure; but between a marriage and a failure there's this ft difference: in a marriage the wife takes the hus- band's name, while in a failure the husband takes the wife's name."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PLEA FOR ESPERANTO.</head>
        <p>A PLEA FOR ESPERANTO.</p>
        <p>_ Anonymous.</p>
        <p>We'll 13.6 gin with a box, and the plural is boxes, But the plural of ox should be oxen, not oxen. Then one fowl is goose, but two are called geese, Yet the plural of moose should never be meese.</p>
        <p>You may find a lone mouse or a whole lot of _r__</p>
        <p>mice, But the plural of house is houses, not hice. If the plural of man is always called men, Why shouldn't the plural of pan be called pen? Then cow in the plural may be cows or kine, But a bow if repeated is never called bine, While the plural of vow is vows and not vine. If I speak of a foot, and you show me your feet, And I give you a hoot, would a pair be called beet? If one is a tooth and the whole set are teeth, Why shouldn't the plural of booth be called beeth?</p>
        <p>Then one may be that, and three would be those, Yet hat in the plural should never he hose! And the plural of rat is rats and not rose.</p>
        <p>We speak of a brother and also of brethren, But though we say mother, we never say inefhren.</p>
        <p>Then the masculine pronouns are be, his and</p>
        <p>him, But imagine the feminine she, shin and shim. So the English, I think, you all will agree, Is the most wonderful language you ever did see.</p>
        <p>It is; and with all its faults we love it still.— The EDITOR.</p>
        <p>PREMIER MOTOR CYCLES. Light-Weight model, £.36.--Sole agent, J. S. G.tawm, 51 to 55, Bridge- street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>MARCH 7i 1914.</head>
        <p>MARCH 7i 1914.</p>
        <p>From Everywhere.</p>
        <p>It's the city soil that is best adapted to raising a crop of wild oats.</p>
        <p>Tact is the art of convincing a man he knows more about a thing than you do.</p>
        <p>Many' a man's virtues wouldn't be suspected if he didn't tell us about them.</p>
        <p>Probably Getting Even. • Brown (to his friend Clippe, the barber): "I say, that swell you shave every day didn't respond to your greeting. He fairly snubbed you." Clippe: "Never mind—l've ()Tien cut him!"</p>
        <p>How They Are Occupied. "HOW does your husband spend his even- ings?" "He stays at home and thinks up schemes to make money." schem "And what do you do with yourself while he's thus occupied?" Not a d Idea.Ohalthiril:llP es to spend it 1"</p>
        <p>" I wish I knew of some Way to make religion more attractive to the mas,, ses.</p>
        <p>" Vvlly not have a description of heaven written by one of these men who write descrip- tions of summer resorts for the railroads?"</p>
        <p>Philanthropy.</p>
        <p>There were once two boys, James and John. James was impetuous.</p>
        <p>..I will refrain from taking what doesn't !belong to me, first, last and all the time lie declared, jre don was,tiandsoa lived%s4 ta.nmdodrieedcrianftoybscurity. But "I will take whatever isn't nailed down, and then, in the fulness of my years, I will give it back !" quoth lie, and became a philanthropist whose name filled the earth.</p>
        <p>How She Obeyed. The new school superintendent was chock full of pedagogy. "Never ask leading questions when examin- ing your pupils," he commanded his teachers. "Do not hint, at the answers. Make the learner find them, unassisted."</p>
        <p>This is how ,the young lady' , teaching Greek history, obeyed: •. " Willy, who dragged whom how many times around the walls of what?"</p>
        <p>Hitting Her Harder. Miss Thynn : "1 saw Jack put his arm around you?" Miss Plumleigh : "You didn't, either." Miss Thynn : "Well, then, as far around as he could get it."</p>
        <p>He Gave Cheerfully. "Benny," said father, desiring to test the generous impulses of his small son, "here are a penny and a quartor. You are going to Church and you may, put into the contribution box whichever coin you please." When the lad returned his father inquired Which coin he had given and why. " Well, pa," replied Benny, "it was this way: The preacher said (hat the Lord loved a cheer- ful giver, and I knew I could give the penny a lot more cheerfully than the quarter; so I put the penny in."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>REMARKABLE STRIKE.</head>
        <p>REMARKABLE STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Position in Connection With the Pletcher Russell Dispute.</p>
        <p>MAYOR'S INTERVENTION DECLINED,</p>
        <p>The large works of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and ,count of the strike which started on Monday. Co.. Ltd., is now indefinitely closed on ac- The men were paid the money in hand last 'evening, and one of the most bewildering strikes ever known in Warrington is in progress. During the week the strikers, who originally numbered 900, picketed so effectively that their ranks were increased to over 1,000, and on Thursday so few men, other than the clerical staff, were in that the firm posted a notice stat- ing that the works were "closed indefinitely."</p>
        <p>TROUBLE BEFORE CHRISTMAS. The trouble began before Christmas over the subject of recognition of the various unions who have members in the works. Mr. A. Bennett, the secretary of the Joint Strike Committee, stated to "The Examiner" representative that the firm had said they had no objections to their employes being trade unionists, but they would not recognise the trade unions. As the men had no other means of collective bargaining than through the union officials, they resented this decision, and after futile attempts at negotia- tions the strike began. The men were reported last night to be unani- mous in their decision to carry on the strike. That the firm's plumbers, joiners and brick- setters have thrown in their lot with the others has infused fresh life into the strike. THE MAYORAL INTERVENTION. There have been some attempts on the part of those outside the dispute to intervene with the view of a settlement, but in vain. The Mayor, through the Town Clerk, wrote offering his ser- vices as arbitrator, or mediator. The firm re- plied:</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,---We beg to thank you for your I letter of the 4th inst., and my directors are pleased to hear that his Worship the Mayor has kindly offered his services if they are required in connection with the strike at our works. "My directors think, however, that all over- tures should come from the workmen, who have struck for what we term unsatisfactory reasons.—yours faithfully, W. RUDGE." Mr. A. Bennett wrote: "The firm has been written to .suggesting a conference. Immediately we hear from them we shall be pleased to avail ourselves of your valuable services."</p>
        <p>A DEADLOCK. About the same time the General Secretary of the Stove and Grate Workers' Union, whose headquarters are at Rotherham, was in War- rington, and he wrote asking for an interview. The reply was that the request for an inter- view must come from the men themselves. The General Secretary, before he left War- rington yesterday, replied to this that before the men decided to cease work they had repeat- edly requested that the firm should receive a deputation to discuss the matter. Thus a deadlock has been reached. The firm contend that any request must come from the men, and the men contend that they have asked for a deputation to be received, ,and that re- quest had been refused. With a view of clearing up the matter "The Examiner" representative sought to interview the management, but he was told that the firm had no communication to make'</p>
        <p>n SUCCOUR FOR PICKETS. Amidst shine and rain the works have been closely picketed all week. During the rain the lot of the men o duty was so hard that the women in the neighbourhood sought to encour- age them with warm refreshments, and this " kindly spirit has been much appreciated. "The as Examiner" h been asked to express the men!s thanks thus: The committee of the joint unions involved in the dispute at Fletcher, Russell's beg to ten- der to the Women in the neighbourhood their heartiest thanks for the care and sympathy they have extended to the pickets on duty."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>BOOK FOR EVERYBODY,</head>
        <p>A BOOK FOR EVERYBODY,</p>
        <p>Many suitable hints on subjects ranging from the law of arrest to the cleaning of clothes, and the preparation of whitewash to the making of a trousers-press, are contained in a concise little book for the home, suitable for every-day refer- ence. It is offered without charge to readers who will write for a copy. The title of the book is `f Everybody's Book," and it will prove as useful to the busy housewife as to the handy man. A copy will be gladly sent free of cost if your name and address is written on a postcard addressed to: Department No. 116, c/o Dr. Wil. Company, 46, Holborn Viaduct, Loadon.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>DEBACLE OF UNIONISM.</head>
        <p>DEBACLE OF UNIONISM.</p>
        <p>What Has Become of the "Cultivated," "Constitutional," "Patriotic" Party?</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S INDICTMENT.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—On more than one platform in Warring- ton the week before last I emphasised the fact that the Unionist party are bent on making use of Ulster as a tool with which to destroy the Parliament Act. That policy is now being developed and made more patent to us all day by day, and I venture to say that, well consid- ered, and having regard to all the consequences to which the present proceedings of the “Die- Hards" may lead, such tactics are but one more illustration of that declension of modern Tory- ism to whieh I have also had occasion of late years to refer in Warrington. There was the Insurance Act, on the introduc- tion of which Mr. 11. W. Forster, a reputable and responsible member of the Tory party, said in effect—l have not his exact words by me— that no mere 'party considerations would be allowed to prevent Conservatives in the House of Commons giving fair-minded consideration to a measure which they welcomed as of national importance. I do not think I have exaggerated the purport of language from the Conservative Whip, which was certainly in amazing contrast to the attempts of the Tory wirepullers imme- diately afterwards to make party capital out of the Act in the etnstituencies by shameless mis- representation of it, and not less shameful efforts to stir up prejudice against the Act, and blind people to the immense national safeguard it provides against bitter calamity and distress. And this from a party which once (unfairly enough, it is true, however ostentatiously) arro- gated to itself in a special degree the title of Patriotic !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>TORY "STATESMANSHIP."</head>
        <p>TORY "STATESMANSHIP."</p>
        <p>Those of us who vividly remember the politi- cal battles of five and twenty years ago and more will remember how in those days Conser- vatives claimed that they had nine-tenths, and a good deal more than nine-tenths of the culture and intellect of the country on their side* Contrast that with the ten year's record of the Thrill Reform fiasco, .which has led the Opposi- tion into such a Serbonian Bog of contradiction and confusion that the supporters of the present Member for Warrington simply don't know where they stand, or what they stand for, while Mr. Smith, if he knows (and I have my doubts as to his ability to give any accurate and com- plete definition of his policy)* dare not tell them. Is this the statesmanship of a cultivated, intel- lectual party? Has not the whole paraphernalia of the Protectionist propaganda reminded us a great deal more of the political economy of "Alice in Wonderland," and the economics of the Mad Hatter to be found there, so familiar to us all in the delightful cartoons of Sir F. Carruthers Gould?</p>
        <p>Then once upon a time the Conservative party, according to themselves, was nothing if not Constitutional. Is it any exaggeration if, as we recall this, we tell them that in these latter days they are the very negation of what they formerly claimed to be? Take the extra- Constitutional and un-Constitutional powers with which they sought to invest the House of Lords wheh the Die-llards of the party—with whom the present Member for Warrington has always been identified—made their desperate effort to wreck the People's Budget of 1909. The charitable intent of Mr. Smith and his friends at that time was to save great wealth and luxuries from the additional taxation to be placed upon them, which was to be transferred instead to the shoulders of the poorer and less fortunate part of our people. They were de- feated, but not before they had showed us plainly enough that they were prepared to throw all Constitutional precedent to the winds, and to shatter the Second Chamber itself in their reckless attempt to accomplish their politically disastrous and nefarious purpose..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>GOSPEL OF ASCENDENCY.</head>
        <p>GOSPEL OF ASCENDENCY.</p>
        <p>As if such a record was not already badH enough, they seek to make it worse now for purely party purposes in Ulster. That many Protestants in that province, more especially I mean in .the North-East corner of it, are brought up with a feeling of aversion and con- tempt for any of their fellow-countrymen who happen to be born of Catholic parents is no doubt very unfortunate, but it is nothing new. It is a fact with which all of us who have been accustomed to give attention to these questions have long been familiar. We do not question the sincerity of this long-standing prejudice, but it is only right to point out now, as we have pointed out before, that prejudice of this kind is born of the gospel of Ascendency, and too often leads men into the violent bigotry of the extreme Orangeman, who looks upon everyone who happens to be brought up in a Catholic household, as, ipso facto, unclean; who is accus- tomed to compare the Pope disadvantageously with the Devil, and who, by way of helping to make the wheels of society run smoothly, is accustomed to greet his fellow-countrymen with the polite suggestion that His Holiness the Pope I might With advantage be speedily sent to— well, a very warm quarter. Now for many years past there have not been wanting signs that the extreme bitterness of feeling which this sort of thing is calculated to generate had begun to abate. We heard little or nothing about it when the chief organs of the Tory Press, and some of their most impor- tant statesmen, were discussing only a little more than three years since the question of Devolution, and the necessity of governing Ire- land according to the ideas of the majority of Irishmen. Is the case for this any less strong now than it was three years ago? Is the con- gestion of work at Westminster less patent? Is opinion throughout the white population of our entire Empire less pronounced? Is the impor- tance from every National and International point of view of cementing permanent and good relations. between the British Fintp.ire and tb€ United States of America less than it was ther:: To every one of these questions the answer 'a an emphatic "No." There has been no such change. What has happened in the meantime is that an opportunity to work upon prejudice, and fan the flames of religious antagonism, has presented itself and has been seized upon by the Die-Hards, who have once more discarded every shred of scruple and patriotism which they ever possessed in this desperate effort of theirs to use Ulster as a paeans by which they may scramble back to power. Five years/ ago they brought things to a final issue with the House of Lords and calculated then that their victory, b the help of that House, would have left not 'y only the , Liberal party, but British democratic govern- ment itself, in ruins. Now more dangerous still they are trying to tamper with the Army the new theory apparently being that if a British workman, or an Irish Nationalist, breaks the law, military force may be used against him fast enough, but never against an Ulster Orange- man whatever laws he breaks. He is to be treated as sacrosanct: and so arming and dril- ling in Ulstee is to be freely encouraged with the tacit understanding that Orangemen may be as lawless as ever they please, because under no circumstances would the Tories ever permit force to be used to compel them to respect the law.</p>
        <p>Here we have the doctrine of Ascendency and inequality before the law, naked and un- ashamed. Nothing more dangerous could be Propounded; nothing more certain ultimately to be subversive of discipline and good order in the State. It is no wonder that many a sober- minded Unionist is shaking his head at these desperate attempts to re-awaken religious animosity in any part of the United Kingdom. Unfortunately too many of the more moderate section of Conservatives allow their dislike of democratic progress and taxation to induce 1 them to wink at these proceedings of their immoderate colleagues, and at the truculent audacity with which the insolent political tub thumper who now (to their ill-concealed chagrin and dismay) leads their party in the House of Commons, seeks to inflame every passion, wad</p>
        <p>(continyied in vat tsos</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>LATE SIR JOHN TENNIEL.</head>
        <p>THE LATE SIR JOHN TENNIEL.</p>
        <p>This week "Punch" issues a special supple-1 merit in honour of the late Sir John Tenniel,l the prince among cartoonists, who for half a 1 century drew pictures for "Punch" with a 1 conscientiousness and success that materially ' assisted his paper to reach, and to maintain against all corners, the rank of head of the comic journals of the world. Sir John Termiel died last week, and the sup- plement giving selections from his work is per- haps the best monument to labours that ex- tended over fifty years as full of historic events of outstanding moment as any in our annals. By permission of the proprietors of Punch"" we are able to reproduce a cartoon dealing with the great John Bright so long ago as 1859. It is typical of Tenniel's work. He had the his- toric instinct that seizes on essentials and re- jects trivialities. The land question fifty years ago was not in the advanced state in which we find it to-day, but Bright's words at that far-off date were prophetic to a degree, and are even</p>
        <p>A Fiftr•Year-Old Cartoon With its Modern Message.</p>
        <p>more applicable to the modern state of the Pub: lic mind than when uttered in the 'Fifties. An° so it is throughout this remarkable supple' meat. Cartoon after cartoon, reproduced afte generations have passed away—Tenniel lived to be 94—is a still fresh and living picture, not A dead ephemeral thing that died with its Pub. lication. Another cartoon illustrating Glad. stone's attitude on the Irish question, so far back as 1881, has its special lesson to-day. Thie we shall reproduce next Saturday. It is thus insight with respect to men •and affairs P°6- sessed by Tenniel and his colleagues that bas made "Punch" the institution it is, not oull in journalism but in politics. We have mentioned only two cartoons with a Liberal tendency, but it must not be inferred that "Punch" is partisan. Both Liberals and Tories come under its impartial lash and the pictures are as true to lifo and conditions as it is possible to make them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>QUAKER AND THE .BAUB:</head>
        <p>THE QUAKER AND THE .BAUB:</p>
        <p>the Land which the territorial party represents in Parliament. . . . That is thl theory of the Constitution; Blackstone says so. But it is a thing which is not likely to 13: respected ranch longer, and it must go, even if involving the destruction of the Constitution. —Mr. Bright in his "Penny Organ." Feb. 5, 1859.</p>
        <p>"THE REAL PRESENCE:'</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Ogden Replies to the Catholic Evidence Lecturers.</p>
        <p>WHERE PROTESTANTISM DIFFERS. A pointed reply was made to the lecturers connection with the Catholic Evidence Lecturee by the Rev. IL Ogden, preaching in Golhorne' street Baptist Church on Sunday evening. Mf; Ogden took for his subject -The Real Presence, basing his remarks upon I. Corinthians xi. " For as often as ye eat this bread and driall this cup, ye do shew the Lords death till lie come." The preacher mentioned. that during the last week there had been a series of Evidence Lee' tures in connection with the Roman Catholle Church in the town. The desire expressed 13! the lecturers was to remove misunderstandille and to enlighten. One welcomed this desire eu the part of the Roman Catholic Church. pe°; testants, whether justly or unjustly, believe" that mystery and silence, and the end justify" the means was the mode and practice of the Roman Church, and one readily welcomed the Publicity. The preacher took exception to the use of the word "Catholic." He was a Catholic, but cer' tainly not a Roman one. Catholic meant liberal' universal, cosmopolitan. Would the Roma° Church admit him (the preacher) to the Mlle! or Communion? Would a Roman priest gic" unto him the wafer 'whilst he remained a Pre' testant? He ventured to doubt it. But iveret any Roman Catholic to enter Golborne-streee Baptist Church the Communion .would not 13 refused to him. If, with a clear conscience Wit° his Master, Christ the Lord, the Roman Cattle:. lie would remain, he would be welcome. Whil was the Catholic Church? That which include. or excluded? He ventured to believe that wide); included. A Protestant on those grounds raigl3 be more Catholic than a Roman Archbishop. No Harmony. On the question of the Real Presence there 05„ no harmony and no agreement betwixt Rome' Catholicism and Protestantism. The Rome! Catholic declares that in the bread and in th' wine are accidents and essentials. Colour! shape, and appearance are accidentaLs. The° always remain, but when the magic words of tile priest are uttered the essence of the bread di°'ci appears, and in their place is the humanity allo the divinity of our Lord. That position.% Protestants was unintelligible. To Proteetautc the bread is nothing more than the syfuhe.,, We do it in remembrahce of the Lord. Just tr some fond son preserves the relic of a parell".; just as he cherishes some heirloom, so to us tblo service is ever a reminder of Him "who liv?a,, and died for us." The origin of the Lora', Supper was extremely simple. The Agape 01' the Early Church was nothing more or nothiag less than an evening supper. The loaf was talgell,, from amongst the rest of the bread. The will' was selected from any cup or bottle. The rite' hyter or Bishop, or minister, all the same tel for the same office, blessed the elements, Oh the deacon handed them round. How has sue' an enormous ritual been conceived from such simple origin? Amongst the Free Churches there is unlir!; imity of opinion. Churches may differ in the,i mode of distributing the elements, but all agrs that the elements on the Lord's Table are h"0 symbols. One hardly relished the gibe by °,flie of the lecturers at the Church of England. inquired what she did believe on this matter' The High Church will have the Real Preseneue; The Broad Church advises us not to worry. Low Church has a distinct negative. That 040 as the lecturer put it. All Free Churchine,n hope that the Established Church will agaill.:r ;all her bodies, hold the Protestant view cerning the Lord's Table, and no section of ,nt Church shrink from the Protestant name, until that day arrives Free Churchmen are 10 emphatic about the elements being only spill° t ,as the Roman Churchmen are emphatic ul,lll the elements being the actual body and lo° of Christ the Lord.</p>
