      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Dread of Smallpox Infection.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council was held in Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday, Councillor Mather presiding. The Chairman asked the Clerk a question with reference to the accounts. They paid rent, rates and gas for that room, and the Board of Guardians was bound by law to pay all ex- penses. The present system was like taking money out of one pocket and putting it into another. He did not see why they should not call upon the Guardians instead of the Council. Councillor Knowles: They would reply and tell you to hold the meetings in their Board- room.</p>
        <p>Councillor Mather: They are bound to find us a place. • Th'e Clerk: They are not. They have no room sufficient for your purpose. Councillor Mather: If that is so, all right. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: It is as well to have raised the point. TOWN PLANNING SCHEME. In regard to the Town Planning proposition the Clerk said the London and North-Western Railway Company had written saying they would object to the company's property being included in the scheme, but when the scheme was taking shape they ~hoped that any point that might arise would be arranged satisfac- torily to both parties. Mr. Newcombe (town planning officer) said he had no report of the Town Planning Committee to submit, as he was awaiting the Local Govern- ment Board's letter in reply to a question he asked as to whether they could extend the scheme mentioned by Mr. P. Bennett at the Conference. lie had had three objections to the scheme, but hoped to settle points with the r:ep"oTrhtactoannta objectors. einifedorttshheofuollii UNVACCINATED CHILDREN. loTwhinegl‘rletimcaml eOnffideeatriosn be made for the reception of nfectio smallpox should any arise—into hospital. trh cases— was specially situated to catch i e district eases, first, from the number of unvcinatu:s dis- eases, and, secondly in a direct line between, f ac ed rom the district being Liver ter, where tramps w •pool and Manches- ter, continually passing be. twe.en those ports.</p>
        <p>The Doctor thought arrangements should made with Warrington, and, failing them, with Widnes. It was decided to try and make arrangements and the pay to be so much per week per person, and not a permanent charge on the District Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>DEBACLE OF UNlONlSM—continued.</head>
        <p>DEBACLE OF UNlONlSM—continued.</p>
        <p>aggravate every difficulty which may enable him to prevent a settlement.</p>
        <p>THE EXPLANATION.</p>
        <p>It is all a spectacle of which law-abiding citi- zens who believe in steady and regulated pro- gress will do well to take note. The explanation of such a debacle of former Conservatism—an explanation to which I have already alluded— is, I think, sufficiently summed up in a remark made by a member of the present Cabinet a few months since, when he said, speaking of the Opposition, "They simply cannot endure exclu- sion from power; and still less the knowledge that we not only possess, but can give effect to it."—Yours, &amp;c., ARTHUR CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>*How well, too, one remembers the claim the Tories made in those days that they had the non-political, impartial, moderate men on their side. Oh, these moderate, non-political politi- cians; don't we. all know them! But where I do they disappear to whenever the immoderate' Imen of their party embark on one of their 'political escapades? Where were they when, in the Parliament of 1906, measures passed by the Liberal party in the House of Commons, with their unexampled and overwhelming majority, were recklessly thrown out by the Second Chamber? Where, again, when the Die-Hards played the desperate game in 1909 to which I have just alluded? Opposing and restraining this immoderate section? Trying to hold the balance fairly? On the contrary, as we all know, aiding and abetting these extremists; egging them on and encouraging them, and in not a few eases in positive energetic alliance with them. Why, the very leader of the non- politicals—if we are rightly informed that Lord Milner has been instrumental in forming the latest league of them—Lord Milner himself, five short years ago, was one of the most reckless extremists of all. ancl the author of the famous policy of "To the bitter end, end 'dam tie consequeaces.'''</p>
      </div>
