      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>EXAMINER</head>
        <p>EXAMINER</p>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Alderman Pierpalnl Returns. &amp;Warman Pierpoint attended the meeting ,of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday for the first time since his recent severe illness.</p>
        <p>Motor Lurry Law. .kt Runcorn on Monday Patrick Collins, jun., 9f Warrington, was fined 10,s. and costs for an infringement of the heavy motor car law, which -ileessitates the attendance a-a the vehicle of a third person.</p>
        <p>„ Smallpox Hospital IVanted. 12:6 Town Clerk was authorised at a meeting the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday to a!'alio application for the borrowing of •E:5,860 4to‘r alterations at Sankey Sanatorium, arid for ne Provision of a Smallpox Hospital.</p>
        <p>Life and Death. The number of births in Warrington last week. :s'its. 35 U 7 males, 18 females), giving a Kate of. Z,47. The deaths numbered 18 (10 males, • ",,efelnales), giving a rate of 12.4, compared with In the same week in 1913..</p>
        <p>\vjKnocking Down Walls.. a len the Council on Tuesday agreed to grant • . 0 Week increase to the nightsoil men, Alder- th ' nutton, amidst laughter, said he supported ProPosal if instructions were given to the 110 t to knock so• many corners of walls I own.</p>
        <p>Tramsport Motor's Adventure. transport motor waggon belonging to the nepartment, and travelling from Warring- r, had an adventure near Northwich on to4rday. Through the salt workings the ha.k_waY subsided and the waggon wheels got • `q in the hole, where it remained a consid- I am "le time before it was extricated.</p>
        <p>Buckley-street P.S.A. At 0,. the meeting on Sunday the chair was 4,‘-`llPied by Mr. J. Fleming. The lessen was " IT Mr. IL Woolley, end two solos, de alilee and The Land Beyond,'' were ren- -1)1'01 red b3r,Mr• • Ilanoers The address, on three )1, we)-, Ills, Pleasure, Perplexing, and Drink," given by Mr. S. Briggs, of Leigh. The mivnist was Mr. G. Cox.</p>
        <p>'Our Lady's," Latchford. • tnight's Mission, conducted by the Jesuit l'athers, Revs. Henry Day and Edward Buck- began last Sunday. Father Day, son of !lie late Justice Day, is well known in the neigh- „ ,t;rbood. His knowledge of social questions :(Kts weight and interest to his eloquent ser- `ll(lns• Father Buck-land, by his 'energy and I,alarvellous power of organisation, has succeeded In establishing at Oakwood Hall, Romiley, a 'louse of Retreat for men, where great spiritual good has already been accomplished, for the %vorking man in particular. Two such eminent !hen ought to bring many to the various services being held.</p>
        <p>Trade Unionist's Death. The death took place on Sunday, at the e,aelY age of 42, of Mr. Richard Leyland Green. Wilderspool-causeway, a well-known and •,!Ighly-respected agent for the Royal Liver fldly Society, and a prominent member of National Union of Agents. Deceased, who ^as a staunch Churchman, was a chorister at yieetton Church, and also a member of St. bila9raas' Men's Bible Class. The funeral took krIZ en Thursday afternoon at St. Thomas' fqhlreh, when a large number of deceased's etaq-agents attended. Many beautiful floral Itik6helsls were sent by sorrowing relatives and Deceased leaves a widow, having only ‘ukrried a short time. At urrington Philomathht Societ3-. held at ea." last meeting of the above society, 4,4 ii-;,143-Street Schools, Mr. Norman Niebi gave l'toseeesting address on "Science and Human the VaS." He said that nobody would question Voild'aet that there was such .a thing in the libon as progress. Civilisation must depend 'aits something capable of increase, and this tevidently scientific knowledge. One of the 11,;-,,iateresting things about human progress lea steady opposition, experienced in every taa'.l,e inevitable advance. That attitude was 4TY giving place to the spirit of inquirr fiIIIZY everything was questioned. Mr. Nl\ d alaPles of the benefits that had accruedlo from the introduction of more seien- Inea, leethods, as in medicine, manufacture, tio-4.1/8 of transport and locomotion, and educa.</p>
        <p>(,) Successful Property Sale. ((Inducted eatnO of the most successful sales by public b°n held in Warrington for some time was bY Mr. J. Wilkinson (Messrs. A. H. 111" CO on Wednesday, 4 the Blue Bell oto , when valuable freehold and leasehold IN:41/er,tios and improved leasehold ground rents eln the market. There was a very good Rttead Lot ance, and the competition was brisk. 71, ~°n.e, comprising the dwelling-houses, 69 and •1 nestley-street, was disposed of for £297 10s. krt two consisted of the dwelling-houses and tefis, 73 'L(11( and 75, Priestley-street, and these were I'-1 for £225 and .£252 10s. respectively. An `)4Praved leasehold ground rent on property in 'flestley-street was included in the third lot, Val this was sold for £365. Eight dwelling- Uses in Lloyd-street comprised lot four, but toese--were withdrawn at £650. The fifth lot Wak the dwelling-house, 45, Mill-lane, which 6rought in £149, whilst the next lot, an im- Dtoved leasehold rent, was sold for £l7O. The .Pretnises known as "Plinston's Model Lodging- "ouse," in Pose and Crown-street, was sold for ...6041- Black Bear House, Knutsford-road. was *Cad to the Corporation for £695. The ninth lot, was regarding four freehold dwelling- "old„ 14:Inse'l and premises in Knutsford-road, was for 4385, and the last lot, the freehold.' ‘l%.e"ing-house and premises, "Brook House; eekton' Heath, was disposed of for £BO Robert Davies and Co., Warrington, 're the solicitors to the vendors.</p>
        <p>tie Warrington's Efficient Police. 1)f Colonel Eden, his Majesty's Inspector Ilkev-Zitabulary, made his annual inspection of ittwarrington County Borough Police Force Cr,,,134,14,,,(1av afternoon, at the Pollee Buildings. "ni -" tden was met at the Chief Constable's 11:L13.3' the Mayor (Councillor Peter Peapoek), Itleiiihe7l3tity Mayor (Dr. Joseph), and several th-,18 of the Watch Committee. The whole 6-„'urce, with the exception of a small num- Itilsrk,,-„duty in the streets, was drawn up for °li in the parade-room, where the men's ft'e produced, and also the equipment of • *tioirOonted section. After a minute inspe?- he(K, '23' Colonel Eden of the men and 6 ~tne 1 their e ,i,ll'a,slll.„'elnents, the men fell in for drill, which •`tl smartness arid Precision' lisPecting Officer next made .a tour of the ileil°cll,B rooms, cells and offices in the Police Arld r, after which he went over the boo: l'irecorcls of the force. Colonel Eden ek N'ieriju himself as highly pleased with the ittliltiv. effi- ° the force, condition of the police "tilißervnl i F.s, &amp;c. As most townspeople know from raiike aulon the local force now numbers n its, /nen,a large proportion of exceptionally tali 1137 of whom have served in crack regi- flii, In. the Army, including Life Guards, Foot tilatrds, Iforse Artillery, Sze., and the result is raosttile force when assembled lit, a body has a tile "lart and businesslike appearance. Since bac„rl 4,1.4 annual Government inspection thirteen lieigh“taive_ioined the force, and their average 8 0 feet 1 inch.</p>
        <p>Stomach 8( Lir 4., 'There is nothing better ti Seigel's Syrup for weak stornachs, sluggish liver, a hovvels. Experience has shoN extracts of which it is composed the stomach and liver, and rest( and Vitality to the digestive • lAKE MOTHER SEIGEL SYRU</p>
        <p>\</p>
        <p>Warrington People's College. We are pleased to report that the special con- cert given by the pupils of the People's College in aid of charity, on 29th January, was a finan- cial success. The gross takings were £3B 18s., and the total expenses £24 15s. 9d., leaving a balance (profit) of £l4 2s. 3d., of which £7 2s. 3d. will be handed to the Infirmary, and .87 to the Nursing Association.</p>
        <p>Billiard League Matches. The following are the results of this week's matches in the First Division: Rylands' 794,, Whitecross 699; Emmanuel Institute 985, Junior ' Liberals 602; Parish Church 858, Latchford Con- servatives 830; St. Mary's 866, Junior Liberals 852; Senior Conservatives 800, Walker's Social 658. In the Second Division the results were: St. George's 633, Rylands' 613; Our Lady's 640, Crosfield's 505; League of the Cross 689, Howley Tannery 505; League of the Cross 683, St. Mary'. 597.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. I Another full audience was attracted on Tues- day by the title "Rome, Naples and Southern 'ltaly," a lecture by Mr. T. E. Briggs, of Black- pool. They had the pleasure of hearing one of the finest lectures of the year, for Mr. Briggs has taken his camera into most unusual scenes, and has somehow obtained pictorial facts that are almost unique. The listeners were taken through the most historical parts of Italy, and the evening was over all too soon. Mr. Hartley and Mr. Wilkes ably expressed the thanks of the society at the close.</p>
        <p>Children's Birthday Party. An enjoyable birthday party has been given to about 200 children connected with the Go!- borne-street Baptist Church. The host and hostess were Mr. and Mrs. Ben Few, and numerous presents were handed to the youthful guests.</p>
        <p>River Mersey in Flood. A 4.8 hours' rainfall and a miniature deluge along the watershed of the Mersey caused the river to run in full flood. An exceptionally huge volume of water was passing through War- rington last night, and during the day thb, stopped some of the operations on the new bridge works.</p>
        <p>Arpley-street Concert. The Honorary Treasurer of the Warrington and District Nursing Association (Mr. II Roberts) tenders his grateful thanks to Mr. Wallington, the teachers and pupils of the Arpley-street School, for their donation of £.7, being one-half proceeds of the concert given January 28th.—Mr. Roger Parr sends a similai acknowledgment on behalf of the Infirmary.</p>
        <p>Prison Gate Mission. The Police Court and Prison Gate Mission will, by the kindness of the Mayor, hold the annual meeting at the Town Hall next Wed- nesday afternoon. This will be a unique event, for never before in the history of the Mission has the annual meeting been held outside Liver- pool. Following upon the recent visit by the Mayor and Mayoress to Liverpool, in order te see the work of the Mission, his Worship arranged for a drawing-room ;neeting at the Town Hall. This opportunity was seized upon by the officials to make the gathering into the annual meeting.</p>
        <p>Funeral of Mr. Donaghy. The funeral of the late Mr. Donaghy, formerly a science master at the Warrington Technica: School, who was found dead on Dartmoor, took place at St. James' Cemetery, Liverpool. Th( first part of the service was held at Aigburth Vale Wesleyan Chapel, where a large numbei of relatives and friends were present. Among the mourners were Mr. J. B. Baxter and thre4 senior boys—H. Emerson, B. Farrar and W Mason—representing the Warrington Secondary School, and Mr. H. H. Albery (headmaster of the Earlestown Technical School). There was a large number of wreaths, including one from the family, and one from the staff and pupils of the Warrington Technical School.</p>
        <p>AVell-known Liberal Dead. It was with deep regret that the members of the Liberal Club learned of the death of Mr. George Simpson, who, for about 15 years, had been a very prominent member. The death took place on Thursday, after a comparatively short illness. The deceased was identified with the sports and recreations of the club and had won a number of prizes both for bowling and billiards. During the present season he had been the captain of the second billiard team and had shown himself as a very capable cueist. The death has cast a gloom over the club, for he was very highly respected by his fellow-members. He was of a very quiet and reserved disposition, and a staunch believer in the Liberal cause.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. To-day.—Children's Bulb Show, Drill Hall. Opened by the Mayoress at 3 p.m. To-day.—Female Foresters' Festival, Wycliffe Memorial Buildings, 7 p.m. To-morrow.—Warrington P.S.A., Park Picture Palace, 3. _ _</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Liberal Association Annual Meet- ing, Liberal Club, 7.30 p.m. Wednesday.—Police Court Mission, Town Hall, 3.</p>
        <p>March 13.—Warrington Church Society Lectures, Parish Church, women 3, men 8 p.m. March 13.—United Kingdom Alliance, Public Meeting, Co-operative Hall, 7.30 p.m. March 14.—Liberal tea and concert, Metho- dist Assembly Room, Grappenhall, 6 p.m. March 15.—Hospital Sunday. March 25.—Warrington Musical Society, Third Subscription Concert, Parr Hall. Preachers for To-morrow. Legh-street Primitive Methodist (Choir Festival)—Morning and evening, Rev. A. J. Chessum. Sacred cantata, "Penitence, Pardon, and Peace," 2.30 p.m. Latchford Baptist—Morning and evening, Rev. J. S. Chedburn.</p>
        <p>_ Wycliffe Congregational—Morning, Rev. T. T. James; evening (young people's special service), Rev. T. 5. James.</p>
        <p>Our Lady's, Latchford (Special Mission)— Morning, Rev. Father Buckland; evening, Rev. Father 11. Day. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.—Morning, Mr. M. Osborne; evening, Rev. W. A. Beckett. Bold-street Wesleyan—Morning, Mr. T. IE Gregson; evening, Rev. E Morgan. Christadelphians, Toft's Assembly Rooms— Evening, Mr. J. E. Bamford, Oldham. St. John's Presbyterian—Morning and even- ing—Rev. W. Reid. Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and evening, Dr. Mellor.</p>
        <p>Golborne-street Baptist Church—Morning and evening, Rev. 11. Ogden. Emmanuel Church—Morning and evening, Rev. W. T. Jaye.</p>
        <p>Age of Unprecedented Interest. The lectures given under the auspices of the Warrington Church Society are proving erE, popular as ever this year and there was a large congregation of women yesterday afternoon, when the Rev. P. L. Parkyn, vicar of Holy Trinity, Southport, took for his subject "The Dignity of Womanhood." lie spoke of woman's Power in the home and the sanctity of marriage, and then described woman as having two supreme gifts, the inestimable gift of Purity— which always created the highest atmosphere for a human being, no matter what outward ' circumstances might be—and the refining in- fluence of life, which was described as woman's highest power. Human nature, the preacher said, was what woman, not man, made it; and their power was almost supreme. From th€. home there radiated the influence for good or evil of the national life, and he implored his hearers to realise their dignity in wielding that power.—ln the evening the reverend gentleman lectured to a large congregation of men on "Is Christianity Practicable?" He drew attention to the extraordinary age in which we lived and described it as the age of unprecedented interest, illustrating it by repeated discoveries and in- ventions, with which it was almost impossible to keep pace. Like all great eras, it was a time of corresponding responsibility. Materialism by itself was a great danger, and into the mate- rial they must bring the spiritual, in order to get the highest good. He drew attention to the practicability of Christian religion, which was the only power that had ever done any real work for uplifting humanity. It attached responsibility to all who wielded power, which, without responsibility, was always a disastrous danger. .</p>
        <p>STAR KINEMA. Oln Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday "The Open Gate," a story of love, hatred and jealousy, will be the principal film, whilst the " Slaves of Ambition," a drama of human passions, is at the top of an exceptionally good bill for the last three days.</p>
