      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WOOD-GAS.</head>
        <p>WOOD-GAS.</p>
        <p>Since the earliest days of gaslighting the gas- making properties of certain classes of wood I have been recognised, but on account of the difficulties connected with the supply and treat- ment of this material wood-gas is generally re- garded as a somewhat interesting curiosity. This view is not generally shared by engineers. In the smaller works in Australia, however, the carbonisation of wood intermixed with coal has now become an everyday practice. In numerous cases the mixture of wood and coal charged into the retorts contains as much as 25 per cent. of the former, and the resulting product offers many advantages over ordinary neat coal gas. Only certain classes of timber are suitable for the purpose, the best being those of the box aud [red gum.</p>
        <p>ton, Lancashire, England, he was born at that place in November, 1883, and there he stayed until he came to Canada. His early education was acquired in the common school, and at the age of ten he started to work for William Bar- low and Sons, in the flour, feed and grain busi- ness; this was his vocation for 22 years. He came to Canada in April, 1912, his destination being Winnipeg, and where he stayed for about five months. He then came to Morse in Sep- tember of that year and entered the services of Williams in the coal business, and with whom he stayed till May, 1913, when he bought out the coal business from Mr. Williams. Later in the year he entered into partnership with W. B. Bell, and since that time the business has been operated under the firm name of Shaw and Bell. He was married at Warrington, his birthplace, in June, 1911, to Miss M. A. Thomas His policy of "live and let live;" which he exercises on all possible occasions, has made him a host of friends, and while he keeps some- what in the background in regards to municipal affairs, he nevertheless takes a keen interest in all that pertains to the advancement of the community. He is also a lover of outdoor sports, his partiality being for football, and at which he engages in practice at every Oppor- tunity. He is connected with the Presbyterian Church, and is a valued member of the choir,</p>
