      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>WARRINGTONIAN IN CANADA,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTONIAN IN CANADA,</p>
        <p>Councillor Shaw's Son "Making Good" Quickly.</p>
        <p>From a recent copy of " The Morse News" (Saskatchewan) we learn that Mr. James T. Shaw (son of Councillor James Shaw), who left Warrington two years ago this Easter, after having worked for Messrs. William Barlow and Son, corn merchants, near the Central Station, is progressing satisfactorily. He appears to have had exceptional luck in becoming a partner with a gentleman • who went to Canada from Australia as a child in 1879, and had had expe- rience of many parts of the country. The firm went into the coal and grain business at Morse. "The Morse News," which gives a portrait of , the young Warringtonian, says :- - -</p>
        <p>JAMES T. SHAW Is one of the Old Country boys who have come to this land of magnificent distances and are steadily hewing their way to the point where they may bask in the smiles of Dame Fortune. A son of Councillor and Mrs. Shaw, of Warring-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>LOCAL PATRIOTISM,</head>
        <p>LOCAL PATRIOTISM,</p>
        <p>des,T ptfioolillowofin3ginisshthawe,sclpoasirntgnerpa:_ ,ssage in the closing this sketch we feel it incumbent upon clre in us not to overlook one item that has meant something during the fall. Mr. Bell, along with his partner, Mr. J. T. Shaw, while interested in e prices of grain the handling of grain as a business proposition, has not overlooked the interests of the town by any means. Practically it is to the eiorts a this firm that the thanks of the town are due uf opr tu theirtiie highestendeavours notch, to k thuseep t hi inducing farmers who were shipping from other place,s to transfer their shipment to this Point, thereby bringing a little more business our way with the conse- quence that the town benefited by the increased trade that naturally resulted." Mr. Shaw has written a letter home, giving advice and cautioning intending emigrants, a letter which we shall reproduce in next week's " Examiner."</p>
