      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>BRITANNIC ASSURANCE COMPANY.</head>
        <p>BRITANNIC ASSURANCE COMPANY.</p>
        <p>. A Record of Continued Progress.</p>
        <p>payments to the company's The directors' report, Presented to the 48th general meeting of the above .compati__, the year ended 31st Dec- ember of which er £610,000, and exceeded Previous Mr. T. Boyd, Midland Buildings, Winwick- street, Warrington, is the local superintendent, the ndining1,425,690. teis policy-holders bti ceeaydrin-lai)oIlnli thatcnoieturisltp athedeiddu ritiiioannygo tvh e p Year by over £68,000. There had been substantial increases ,per cent..for the year accu- mulated boto t, ncora eincomea The premium come is Total claims paid amounted. to 0t9,43._ ,885. The surplus would again provide a reversionary to all participating the total policy-holders in the imme- diate profit class, and in adequat, provision for the policy-holders in the accumulated profit bonus at the rate of 325.. class. It was proposed to continue to allot a cash bonus at the rate of ,k 5. per cent have upon the sums assured under industrial policies of five years' duration and upwards, which become Claims by death or maturity during the yea, commencing 9th March,. 1914, e-rul terminating 7th March, 1915, except ?il certain cases, effected prior to 1890, where additions h..- already been made under the terms of the policy, The Chairman (Mr. F. T. Jefferson), moving the adoption of the report, said he was pleased to say the additional claims met were not due ' in any measure to adverse or unfavourable mor- tality experience. In fact, the Mortality expe. rience during 1913 was slightly More favourable wi than was that of 191., and quite thin their expectancy. The excess was due, entirely to the larger number of maturing policies falling in during the year, and to the increased bonuses paid in both the ordinary branch and industrial branch. The amount paid under maturing endowments and endowment assurance policies amounted to no less a sum than £187,491. This large sum had been distributed during the year to living assurants, who had adopted this means of protecting their savings. The loss of the Submarine A 7 would be fresh in their minds One of her gallant, but unfortunate crew assured himself for the benefit of hi S wife only a short period before the disaster. Ile need hardly say that they had not waited for the usiial prelim- inary technicalities, but they assumed that death had taken place, and they had promptly met the claim. (Hear, hear.) Such disasters as the dreadful explosion at the Sengenhydd Colliery contributed to heavy the on their funds, and they were met promptly and Byrn,- pathetically. The moment they got information of such a disaster a telegram was sent to their local representative to be on the spot, so as to do all he could to alleviate the miseries involved by such a calamity. The payment of these large sums involved an enormous amount of work, both on the outside and inside staffs. Our total accumulated funds, said the Chair- man, stand at £3,544,980, which is double, exactly double, what they were seven short years ago—the period with which I have already instituted a comparison. I think you are al- ways interested to know the relation of our life assurance and reserve funds to our life premium income. I am glad to be able to tell you that its ratio to-day is 275 per, cent. That is to say, we have more than 21 years of life premium in- come in hand for the security of our policy- holders. The report was adopted.</p>
