      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>AVesleyan Choir Social.</head>
        <p>AVesleyan Choir Social.</p>
        <p>The members of the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Choir held their annual social on Tuesday in the Winmarleigh Cafe, When they were hon- oured by the presence of some of the officers and members of the church. There was a varied programme, which kept everyone in such good humour that the constant query was—" How soon are you going to have another?" The prize-winners for the various competitions, Bze,, were Miss G. Pitchford, Miss A. WinstanleY, Mrs. E. Pitchford, Mr. Bowers, Mr. T. Maddock, and Mr. Ashworth.</p>
        <p>£lOO, for Lost Finger. At the Warrington County Court on Thurs- day Mr. P. R. Bennett successfully applied for sanction of settlement of a claim on behalf of May Cooper (18), 181, Wellfield-street, against Messrs. Armitage and Rigby, Limited.—The girl received injuries to her hand and lost a finger in an aceident at the works, and the plaintiffs were prepared to accept the £lOO compensation and £l5 15s. costs offered by the iesoon hmc.s.— Mr. N. H. I'. Whitley said the firm thought the he sum a generous one, and quite adequate._T girl's father said he was going to set his daugh- ter up in a chip potato business.-=His Honour said the proportion of successes in such small businesses was not many, and full investigation should be made by the father. He sanctioned the settlement. .</p>
        <p>A Naturalist's Wanderings. The story of his wanderings as a naturalist in British Central Africa was told by Professor It. Newstead in a lecture, under the auspices of thelluseum Committee and the Ogden Trustees, in the Co-operative Hall on Saturday night. a crowded audience. Mr. L. There was Greening presided. His expedition, said the lecturer, had to build their own huts, and that was done by the 1" boys," but when the mud had to be put on the walls the "boys" struck work, because they 'd such Was women's work, and so women had to be got to do it. They employed native cooks, who,with primitive appliances, prepared the food as well as one got it at home. These men were all satisfied with ss. a month. They were named Corbett and Sullivan. Excellent slides illustrated the lecture.</p>
        <p>A Question of Roads. Warrington Town Council sat for two and a half hours on Tuesday and was adjourned with- out the Budget being taken. This will come before a special meeting on Tuesday next. When the meeting adjourned it was discussing whether granited asphalt should be used to pave Steel- street, Oakwood, Elmwood, Pinewood, Asliwood and Briarwood-avenues. Alderman Tinnion ob- jected to the action of the Property Owners' Association in writing members with the object of having a cheaper type of paving installed. Alderman Bolton said the new streets were narrow and were to be planted with trees. When the trees grew they would close up the streets so that carts could not get through. Councillor Gough said unless the property owners ,were encouraged to build, the Council would have to do so. At this stage the debate was adjourned until Tuesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>True Liberal.</head>
        <p>A True Liberal.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. M. Ryan, of 6, North-view, Wimble- don Common, a member of the Wimbledon Bor- ough Council and of the Surrey County Coun- cil, and son-in-law of the late Mr. John Cros- field, was recently adopted as prospective Liberal candidate for Plymouth, with Mr. Dobson, formerly M.F. for the borough. Inter- viewed after his selection, Mr. Ryan said no true Liberal could be anything but satisfied with the way in which the affairs of the nation were being conducted by the Government. He was a strong Free Trader, and the Government had successfully resisted all assaults on the economic principle of Free Trade. He was a strong Home Ruler and the Government had brought Home Rule to an issue which would result in the triumph of the principle of self-government in. Ireland. He was a progressive in social politics, and the Government had given a hundred evi- dences of its broad progressive ideas about social affairs. Mr. Ryan is described by the "Western Daily Mercury" as an experienced and an able speaker.</p>
        <p>Working Men and the Infirmary. Men's Infirmary The final meeting Committee tingfor itlree wasyear held n ofv of the in Assembly boom on Wednesday evening, when there was a large attendance over which Mr. Geo rsgenuecdcWebsays lf kut ehl re p ris eot sniaodnre dad a r. y sheo;eftualryf u(tli rr e. J. eHr e. outlined J. Hampson). A few years ago, he said, the amount raised by the committee reached £l,OOO in the year, and that was thought to be very good. They were now approaching the £1,500, upon which they had set their hearts. The net amount raised last year was £1,205, a net loss of £4O on the year, which, in face of heavy losses sustained through the breakdown of the organisation in two works, following upon the passing of the Insurance Act, was highly satis- factory. They had had increases from other quarters, which had kept the lose down to the small amount named, and now the difficulties arising out of the Insurance Act had been settled they might look forward to a more suc- cessful time this year. mirtrteoemhtande rebuetcivheedrst'hefosottrabalolf match huthBde their(. e ied sharefrom of theAtr EdwardP iple ~caomnd. 'BWC:llllienggetocno,newerta; 2s. 3d.—an announcement that was received with applause. Among the plans for the future was a suggested Musical Society open-air conceit in June, and negotiations had already been begun with a view to getting a repetition of the August open-air concert, in which the Nonconformist choirs and Crosfield's Band took part. Mr. Daintith gave his experiences as a repre- sentative of the committee on the House Com- mittee of the Infirmary, and said that if finances would allow, more 'beds could be ' brought into use. All the patients he had spoken to had had nothing but praise for the An manner in which they had been treated. , , iron girder had been placed between the Nurses' I I Home and the Infirmary, so that in case of fire in either escape to the other building would be possible. Mr. Daintith was thanked for his services during the year. . Alderman Burton (chairman of the commit- tee, who arrived late) expressed the opinion that the result of the year's working was satisfac- tory. There had been 13,000 patients treated at the Infirmary, a thousand more than in the previous year, showing that the institution was needed even more than before the passing of the Insurance Act. Out-patients had also shown an increase. An agenda for the annual meeting of the com- mittee to be held at the Town Hall next Satur- day was approved; and Alderman Burton was warmly thanked To kis services as shairosan luring, the year.</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>~ SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 19144</p>
        <p>I</p>
