      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>THREE LICENCES FOR ONE.</head>
        <p>THREE LICENCES FOR ONE.</p>
        <p>Brewers Offer is Declined by Licensing Bench.</p>
        <p>NO NEW PREMISES FOR LONGFORD.</p>
        <p>At the adjourned Licensing Sessions yesterday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, an ap- plication was made for permisaion to remove the licence of the Golden Grove to a proposed Dud- ley Arms in Longford-street. The manager was Mr. Alfred Peters, and the hoopoe belongs to Meesrs. Peter Walker and Son. Mr. Gordon Hewart (instructed by Mr. T. S 'Steel) for applicant, said there had been a great 'deal of building in the Longford-street district, ,and a large number of comparatively new houses were occupied by the better-class citizens There was, therefore, a requirement for a fully- licensed house, and if the Bench could see theft way to authorise the removal of the licence, Messrs.. Walker were prepared to give up the licence of the Golden Grove, the Crown Inn beer licence, Queen-street, and also premises in Pinner's-brow. The firm were prepared to sur- render the premises to the Corporation on rea- sonable and easy terms:</p>
        <p>Mr. T. B. Leigh (instructed by Mr. A. Browne) opposed on behalf of seven licence-holders in the neighbourhood; Mr. Battye opposed for the Temperance party. Mr. Ellis, secretary to the company, was cross-examined by Mr. Bettye. Brewers do not, as a rule, expend money if they are not going to get more?-011. yes. Mr. Hewart : Is it a fact that for 55 years Messrs. Walker have not had an application granted by this Bench ?—Yes. Are you satisfied there is a demand for this house !—Yes. •</p>
        <p>Alfred Peters, the manager of the Golden Grove, considered the site a good one for a public-house. Mr. Battye : Do you suggest people who cannot walk 103 yards are going thirsty ?—Witness made no reply. Mr. Battye: Do you know anything about the demand of the neighbourhood ?—No reply. What class of people would you cater foi there?—The working-classes. DRAM AND DEERSHOP.</p>
        <p>Do you think extra facilities would • benefi• L' them?—lt might be a bit handier for them.</p>
        <p>Close tet,this•proposed. house therepawn- shop. Does, that look as if the people wanted more spirits?—No reply. It is not for commercial travellers, tourists, 86c. It ie to be a drinkery place pure and simple. A dram and beer shop, naleed'a-nd'unrelieved?— No reply. Can you suggest one reasonable cause for this house?—NO reply. Albert Hall: 37, Dudley-street, contended that there was a full-licence needed, because it was a long way to go for brandy in case of sickness. Mr. Battye: IS 100 yards unreasonable? —Quite at times. Mr. Battye: Next door and let on with a tap J A licensed house for everyone within 106 yards. John Thomas, 159; Longford-street, also gave evidence as to the need for the house, and said .18 people were there to say the same. Mr. Battye said there was one question to be answered—Was the house required in the dis- trict? He considered every othr e question foreign or extraneous, and must not be consid- ered before the first and foremost question was settled. The Bench refused to grant the application. ONE LICENCE FOR ANOTHER. Mr. H. Madden, barrister, made an applica- tion on behalf of Matthew Cunningham for the removal of an off-licence from Froghall-lane to the Brewery premises. After a lengthy argument the application was granted, subject to the applicant giving up the licence in Froghall-lane and undertaking not to sell less than six bottles at the brewery, Mr. Madden was instructed by Mr. Longton, solicitor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>MAYOR'S OLD SCHOOL</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S OLD SCHOOL</p>
        <p>Presentation of a Photo of Himself to Wycliffe Scholars.</p>
        <p>"It affords me very great pleasure to be with you this afternoon for, and it is only one reason among many, to tell you all, you boys and girls, there is nothing impossible in this world if you study carefully and take advantage of youi opportunities." These were the opening remarks made by the Mayor (Councillor Peter Peacock), who, accom- panied by the Mayoress, attended Wycliffe School yesterday afternoon to present a framed photograph of hirdself to the institution where he, as a poor boy, received his early training. Mr. J. Longton presided and others present were Councillor 11. Roberts, Mr. Corney (the formed headmaster), the Rev. T. T. James, Mr. Jeffery (headmaster), and Mr. J. Moore Murray.' Looking back for about 28 or 29 years, said the Mayor, when he was a scholar at the school, he little thought that the lessons he was learn- ing under Mr. Corney would have been so pro- fitable as to enable him to further his position and merit promotion. He was proud that he was the first Wycliffe Day School boy to become Mayor. (Applause) Mr. Jeffery expressed the thanks of the chil- dren and the staff for the lilayor's kindness. They could not all be Mayors of county bor- oughs, but they could try t,o fill honourable positions, if not in this, in other towns. (Ap- plause.) COUNCILLOR ROBERTS'S HINT. Councillor Roberts thanked the Mayor on be- half of the Education Committee. That little function was not simply associated with that particular school, although it was quite true that the Mayor owed a great deal to the teach- ing he received there. It might be in the cir- cumstances which obtained that in a year or two that school might have to be closed. If it was he hoped that the portrait would be pre- sented to one of the new schools, in order that it might act as a stimulus to the scholars to get the full benefit out of their education.. (Ap- plause.) Mr. Corney feelingly added his thanks as the Mayor was one of his scholars. The Rev. T. T. James said there had hardly been a function of greater interest in the school since it was erected, and the photograph would be preserved and would encourage both teachers and scholars. (Applause.) The photograph was handed to Enoch Thomas (the top boy of the school), on behalf of the scholars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>"A WALTZ-LiREAM " AT THE ROYAL COURT.</head>
        <p>"A WALTZ-LiREAM " AT THE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>Large and delighted houses have ruled this week at the Royal Court, where Mr. George Dance's company have been presenting "A Waltz Dream" in a manner that has fully jus- tified the glowing accounts which preceded the coming of the comedy. It would be indeed diffi- cult to imagine a more charming evening's entertainment than is provided by this company and this opera. The work itself is perfect in every way. The light comedy is of the best, the plot has a substance about it that elevates the opera far above the majority of its class, and the music is rich and melodious to a degree- I Given these conditions, only good artistes are' needed to make a complete success, and it may be said fearlessly that seldom has a more tal- ented and attractive company than this one been assembled in comic opera. Miss Frances Midgley, as Franzi Steingruber, acts, sings and dances in the most fascinating manner, and manipulates the threads of a play that centres round her doings in delightful fashion. Miss Maude Powell, as Princess Helene, cold and im- perious (to order) makes a striking contrast to the lively, light-hearted Franzi, and playa her part well. Lieut. Niki (Mr. Cyril Thompson) is a decidedly good character in capable hands. Most of the fun is provided by the Prince of Flausenthura and his cousin, Count Lothar (Mr. Herbert Williams and Mr. W. P. Morgan), who can be relied upon to make you laugh -If you never laughed before. The dresses, as well as the waltz, are a "dream." There should be a lug house to-night. Slaves of Vice" Next Week. " Slaves of Vice," by Dennis Clyde, described as a new and original drama, Will be presented next week by Arthni4 Hinton's company. There will be two performances nightly, and a matinee , at 2.30 on Idoday..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WAKES WEEK WANTED.</head>
        <p>WAKES WEEK WANTED.</p>
        <p>Should Warrington Follow Other Towns in Matter of Holidays?</p>
        <p>DISTRESS CAUSED BY PRESENT SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>Should Warrington have its Wakes Week like other towns in Lancashire? This is a question which is arousing much interest amongst all classes of workers, who feel that the existing holidays are not so suitable as. they might be. They came throughout the stmanter as a sort of patchwork, and never give more than a few days at a time. _ _</p>
        <p>The idea of a Wakes Week was mooted at a meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labouz Council on Tuesday, and althouzli the matter was not fully discussed the feeliwg seemed to be that the Warrington workers would prefer to have a whole week's respite from. labour than odd days throughout the summer. Other Lan- cashire towns appreciate such a method and are dead against any change back to. holidays broken up through the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>DATE OF WALKING DAY.</head>
        <p>DATE OF WALKING DAY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shimmins said there was a great deal to be said in favour of a Wakes Week. It would give the workers a longer and better holiday, and he thought steps should be taken to ap- proach the proper authorities with the view to securing a change. The date of the Walking Day should be changed to Whit-Week, and thus coincide with the Manchester holidays. A great deal of dis- tress was caused to the workers by the present system of holidays in vogue-in Warrington. A delegate thought a Wakes Week would in- terfere with the stock-taking of the firms, but it was pointed out that this was overcome in other towns.</p>
        <p>Another delegate thought if the Walking Day was changed to Whit-Week' it would be too early. If was decided to ask tlie Mayor (Councillor P. Peacock) to call a conference of the parties concerned, with a view of coming to an arrange- ment about the holidays. The idea of a Wakes Week is one which ought to become popular amongst the workers, and more will' probably be heard- of it. Although it is too late to interfere with the holidays fixed for this season, some re-arrangement may be effected.before next season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>MALE CHORAL UNION CONCERT.</head>
        <p>MALE CHORAL UNION CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The annual concert organised by the War- rington. Male Choral Union attracted to Parr Hall on Saturday night an audience whose numbers—in these days of ever-recurring dis- appointments in such ventures—may be re- garded as quite satisfactory to the promoters. 8o full a measure of public support is a matter for further congratulation, in that it signifies that an interest in vocal music still survives, in spite of the gloomy prophecies of the Musical pessimists.</p>
        <p>Without minimising in any unnecessary way the position of the local Male Choral Union, it is due to say that the re-appearance on this occasion of the Blackpool Glee and Madrigal Noczety was an event in which considerable anticipatory interest had been manifested. 'Nor did the singing 'of this famous body of vocalists . belie the ever-growing eulogiuzus that are passed upon it wherever it has appeared in the interval. Much of the I music sung by the mixed choir was heard for the first time in 'Warrington, and that many of these novelties made a profound impression upon the audience was shown in frequent en- cores and in outbursts of enthusiastic ap- plause. In some of the grouped pieces there were contrasts of style, which a performance in ' the chronological order of their composition made doubly interesting. Thus, following upon the quaint 16th century "Ladye, your eie," of Thomas Weelkes, one listened, in succession, to Mendelssohnian suavity in "The Nightingale," to the graceful gaiety of Parry in "Come, pretty wag," to Cornelius's deep emotionalism in "Surrender of the soul," and lastly to the formal prettiness of our latter-day Sullivan in the madrigal " When love and beauty." All of these were sung with that impeccable taste and precision which has brought high distinc- tion upon the Blackpool Society, whose equip- ment, in the qualities that go to making of an ideal choir, Mr. Herbert Whittaker has done so much to perfect.</p>
        <p>Of the seven other items in the programme, sung by the mixed choir, two stood well for- ward from the rest by reason of their vivid pic- turesqueness- These were the choral ballad, "The Mad Fire-rider" (Wolf) and the choral song in eight parts "The Leprehaun " (Gran- ville Bantock), in which dramatic intensity in the one vied with. a spirit of sprightly playful- ness in the other, to hold the listener entranced. Amongst a mass of modern music there was only one item by the late Coleridge-Taylor--an eight part song, "Dead in the Sierras"—but it proved a host in itself in the depth of its appeal and in its convincing soulfulness. Moreover, it was sung with a conscientious regard for its in- ward significance which is deserving of all praise.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Male Choral Union had its ranks re-inforced by the male singers of the Blackpool Choir for the performance of the Bralams Alto Rhapsody, and, with Mde. Clara Butchers in the solo part, a fine rendering of I this important work was given. Under Mr." Nesbitt's masterly guidance, and possibly I stimulated by the presence of the Blackpool Choir, the members of the local Choral Union were quite at their best, their singing being characterised by an abounding verve, which sometimes, as in "United are we" and "The Franklyn's dogge," threatened the unqualified success of these pieces owing to exaggerated emphasis and the overdoing of expression marks. But, on the other hand, a quite ad- mirable restraint, as well as a fine sense of climax, was evinced by members in Elgar's "Feasting, I watch," just as there was also a most expressive feeling of tranquility pervad- ing "It's oh! to be a wild wind," by the same composer—two examples of quite ideal choral singing, which do infinite credit to the Male Choral Union. It should be mentioned that numerous luminous and informative pro- gramme notes, from Mr. Nesbitt's pen, added greatly to the pleasure of listening to the various choral pieces. Mde. Clara Butchers and Mr. W. B. Walton (members of the Blackpool Society), contributed interesting and well-rendered songs, and Mr. Bradford Barton appeared in the capzeity of solo violinist, without, as it seemed 'to ns, doing himself anything like justice either in his choice of‘ pieces or in the manner in which he performed them. Mr. E. Bennett North made a most efficient accompanist, and Mr. F. I Atkinson conducted, in an entirely capable way, the later numbers in the programme sung by the Male Choral Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>JUST IN TIME.</head>
        <p>JUST IN TIME.</p>
        <p>_ The delightful suits that are being tuned out by our local firm, " Suits, Ltd.," at 12/11 has talking of what this enterprising is gfirmeSt3:v simplyewl bb°ed:d; laying g next. down a deposit of 2/11 it possible to obtain a first-class suit made to measure, London cut, from cloths which will wear well, and the garment need not even be settled for until it is quite convenient to the purchaser himself. It is little wonder that Suits, Ltd., are inun- dated with orders, whilst other tailors are grumbling about business being slow. Last Saturday, it was almost impossible for cus- tomers to get served, at Suits, Ltd., during certain hours of the day. Don't miss the opportunity now offered you of getting an extra suit at a very much lower figure than is usually charged. The sale fin- ishes in a few days. It only requires a deposit of 2/11 to secure you a suit, which can be worn on many occasions, or saved for the holidays, but you must call at once or it will be too late. Suits, Ltd.'s, address is: 17, Ilorsemarket-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>ARROL JOHNSTON CARS are excellent value; 4-seater complete £281.---Sole agent for War- rington and district, J. S. °ARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-etreet, Warrivgrom</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <p>7</p>
        <p>THE DEPUTATION.</p>
        <p>Trade Unionists Maur Home hie and Condemn Bigotrg.</p>
        <p>BELFAST SLUMS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Occupied bp Nationalists bat Wet "Coal " Unionists.</head>
        <p>Occupied bp Nationalists bat Wet "Coal " Unionists.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner." _</p>
        <p>Sir,—l must confess to a feeling-OfiLa.pmeint- ment when I looked through the columns of to- day's "Examiner" and found no response fittem our friend, Mr. Ravenscroft, to the questions I put to him, disputing the accuracy of the in. formation which prompted the statement in his ,telegram, "All trade unionists here unite im condemning Home Rule." But possibly Mr. Ravenscroft may be: like myself, a creature or -circumstances, and cannot make his opportuni- ties, but has to await them, and we may hear from him later. In the meantime I would like to add: I have before me a copy of "The Irish News and Bel- fast Morning News," dated Monday, February 23rd, 1914, in which is a report of the bi-monthly meeting of the Belfast Trades and Labour Coun- cil, held Saturday evening, February 21st, 1914, in the Engineers' Hall, College-street, Belfast. D. R. Campbell presided. Mr. Mercer (Protestant) proposed: "That in view of a prob- able change in the government of Ireland, in the opinion of this Council, the time is now opportune for a conference of the Trades Coun- cils of Ireland, branches of trade unions, 1.L.P., and co-operative societies, to consider the ques- tion of Labour representation in Ireland." This was seconded by Mr. Bush (ex-Orangeman), the sentiment being supported by Messrs. D. Hous- ton, J. Murphy and J. Johnson, and corruil unanimously. _</p>
        <p>Will. you allow me to give you a few quotations from the speech of Mr. Mercer? They are "The Labour party in Ireland had been divided, owing to the great religious difficulties that had been for so long the curse of the 'coun- try."</p>
        <p>"The workers of Ireland were deluded in thinking, that Home Rule was a religious ques- tion."</p>
        <p>"The leaders of the Unionist party care as much about the Protestant religion as they did about the Hindoo religion." "He did not fear the aggression of Itomanism any more than he feared the aggression of Pres- byterianism."</p>
        <p>"The day of the power of the clerics ind of the priesthood was over."</p>
        <p>"With regard to the exclusion of Ulster, he hoped Labour would protest with no uncertain voice against any such legislation. It must he all Ireland under the Union, or all Ireland under Home Rule."</p>
        <p>"Ireland was a poor man's country, and the question was one for the workers. The exclu- sion of Ulster could not be tolerated for one moment."'</p>
        <p>UNIONIST SLUMS!</p>
        <p>I don't think I will labour this portion of the subject further, sufficient to show you the spirit of the gathering, which hardly harmonises with the statement, " All trade unionists unite in condemning Home Rule." If you will allow me again to refer to the visit of the deputation to Belfast, I would like- to quote the remarks of a friend,.a native of Belfast, who is a Protestant, and the son of a gentleman who has pretty con- tinuously held high office in the Orange Order, and all of whose relatives are members of Orange ledges. This friend is also a promi- nent executive official of the Trades and Labour Council. He writes:</p>
        <p>" With regard to Warrington deputation, I was then away at Labour party Conference in Glasgow, and the first I heard about it was when I read the report in the Press. I have made inquiries, and have been unable_to find a single trade unionist, secretary, delegate, organiser, or officinl of any kind who saw this deputation. We have two deputations here at present, one from Yorkshire, one from Lincolnshire, profit- ing by this Warrington deputation fiasco. We had some of them present during the whole of our council meeting. • They have been toured round Dublin and the South, and have been given a good survey of the Dublin slums, and are now up North for the purpose of contrast. No effort is being spared to show them ours. A few of us undertook that duty, and on Sunday morning we let them have a good inspection of Nationalist and Unionist slum quarters. They had been informed by their Unionist members that the slums in this city were to be found ex- clusively in the Nationalist quarters, but we very quickly disproved that. The slums in the Nationalist quarters are, of course, owned by intensely loyal Unionists."</p>
        <p>_ If you will favour me with space in your columns / would like to add more from the Labour men of Belfast later, as I am sure your readers would appreciate another view of the case as presented by some of the members of the de.putation.—yours very truly, PATRICK O'BRIEN. 123, Wilderspool-road, February 28th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>HIPPODRONIE.</head>
        <p>THE HIPPODRONIE.</p>
        <p>George Newburn a Great Mimic. An "all star" programme is submitted at the Hippodrome this week, and large crowds are the nightly order. George Newborn, the in- imitable mimic artiste, is always a good draw, and he captured the audience with excellent impersonations of Mark Sheridan, Tom Foy, George Lashwood and Harry Lauder, and the audiences will not let him go until he has given. "George Formby." In word and mannerisms he is perfect. Infectious mirth and quaint humour are introduced admirably by Mr. George Lill in the sketch "Her Husband." Lill is an absolute personification of humour, and in every situation he scores a success. Mr. Barton Rosvner gives a clever delineation of the character -William Huntley," and his dramatic utterances, contrasting so much from the "meekness" of Lill as the burglar, contain just sufficient of the dramatic element necessary. The other items are in equalry capable hands. Rosa Hamel is a clever entertainer in ampidex- trous crayon impressions, in connection with Which she sings, in a pleasing soprano voice, several popular songs. Julian Ross has got the exact breezy style for a light comedian, and as an actor vocalist he is also a success. The Wolken Trio give a comedy aerial trapeze act in a daring manner, whilst the Payolas merit the applause they receive in their athletic novelty and classical posing. Arthur Clifton delights patrons with his light comedy, and Maudie Vera, the dainty comedienne, sings and dances well. "Never Late," a humorous film, is shown on the hioscope.</p>
        <p>Big Draws Next Week. Wonders of canine sagacity are to visit the Hippodrome next week, when a return visit is paid by Dunean's Royal Scotch Collies. Other turns will be Yamamoto and Miss Koyoshi Troupe, Japanese equilibrists and entertainers; "The Ace of Hearts," sketch by James Wil- lard's company; The Lascelles in "The Avia- tors," comedy; Dorothy teigh, Manchester pantomime favourite; The Four Wards, in har- mony and comedy; and the Musical McGregors. musicians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>- - - -- "The Star of India," a magnificent drama of Indian life, is the star film for Monday, Tues- day and Wednesday, and for the remainder of the week "Count Zarka," a dramatic photo play of Court intrigue, occupies premier place. Other films of exceptional interest will also be shown.</p>
