      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>LYONS INTERNATIONAL EXHIBITION 1914.</head>
        <p>LYONS INTERNATIONAL EXHIBI- TION 1914.</p>
        <p>An International Exhibition of very great importance to British manufacturers in general is to be held during the summer months in the town of Lyons, the third city in France, and one of the richest industrial centres of that country. Situated at the junction of the Saone And the Rhone, in the midst of the fairest val- leys of France, Lyons is always, exhibition or no exhibition, one of the best markets for British goods. The British merchant or manu- facturer who is represented at this forthcoming exhibition will, therefore, be certain of a con- siderable increase in his trade—certain of reap- ing a rich harvest, where every Continental buyer is on the qui vive for high-class products. The importance of the enormous advertising afforded by such an exhibition can hardly be realised. But it is more than advertising, it is salesmanship of the highest class, for it not only advertises the goods that are for sale, but it brings them to the buyer himself, to his very doors, as it were. In the case of goods of w bulky description, this is of an inestimable ser- vice, which accounts for the popularity and utility of these big exhibitions. There is scarcely any other way in which consumers can be reached so effectively. The Lyons Exhibition will be one of the most important Industrial and Commercial Exhibi- tions ever held in the history of business. Opening on the let May, it will be both Inter- national and Urban. It will bring together every keen business man, not only from the Continent, but from countries in every part of the world.</p>
        <p>At the Lyons Exhibition special features will be made of all important branches of industry. Special attention will, of course, be paid to the silk trade, of which Lyons is the centre in France. It is a well-known fact that these fac- tories, as well as those in all the other textile industries in France, are run for the major part by British-made machinery. It is evi- dent, therefore, that a machinery exhibit worthy of the reputation our home manufac- turers have acquired, cannot but serve to strengthen our grip on this productive market, and prevent foreign competition benefiting at our expense. Nearly everything produced in the British Isles can be put on sale in this rich market by means of space taken in this exhibition. ' CHARLES KNOTT.</p>
        <p>THE TRANSFORMATION OF THE INN. The Saxon word "inn," taken in its present sense, was probably in use before the Conquest, whereas the purely French word "hotel," as generally applied to an "inn of style and pre- tension," dates only from about the time when officers of the British Army of Occupation re_ I turned from Paris with enthusiastic accounts of I Meurice's and a dozen other hostelries of the Rue de Rivoli and the Rue St. Honore, which quite threw the humbler accommodation offered by the average British inn of that period into the shade. At the commencement of the nine- teenth century Frenchmen taught the London inn-keeper the science of.. hotel-keeping; before the beginning of the twentieth the Englishman had begun to return the compliment, and now the most luxurious and well appointed Parisian hotels owe their origin to British enterprise.— "Country Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
