      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>EXA</head>
        <p>EXA</p>
        <p>HOUSES FOR WORKERS.</p>
        <p>The Subject is Deferred for Six Months.</p>
        <p>NOT PREPARED TO BIND THEMSELVES.</p>
        <p>The question of providing working men's dwellings in the village has been deferred for. six months. This decision was arrived at when the Parochial Committee met in the Hall Cafe on Tuesday evening. A lengthy discussion arose when the Clerk read the report and recommendations of the sub-committee, which met to consider the ques- tion on February `2Bth. They ,reported that after consideration of the various plans pre- pared by the Surveyor it be a recommendation to the Parochial Committee that Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., and, the agent of the Arley estate, be-asked the price for the sale or lease of two acres of land in the parish for the erection of 22 or 24 workmen's dwellings. (2) Subject to the land being obtainable at a reasonable price, and in a suitable situation, the erection of such dwellings be further considered.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said the sub-committee were not prepared to bind themselves to that recommend- ation, but were prepared to consider the matter further. Councillor Hallows said that wherever the dwellings had been tried they had been a failure.</p>
        <p>IS THERE URGENT NEED? Councillor Warburton thought if the land could be bought it would be far better than leasing it. Councillor Pearson: What will the rent be for the lowest scheme?</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Seven shillings, inclusive of rates. Councillo? Warburton: The tenants will pay their own rates.</p>
        <p>Councillor MeDermid : The amount of rent and that of rates will be-specilled separately. Councillor Pearson: Has the rate collector to go round every week? Councillor Warburton: I don't know about that. The Chairman (Councillor Pilling): That matter will come forward when the question is considered again. Councillor Bottomley : Have you heard of any urgent need? The Chairman: I have not heard of any. Councillor McDermid : There are others be- sides those of Stockton Heath. Councillor Clarke: Assuming you build these houses, you will have to borrow the money, and that will have to be paid back within a certain number of years. Will the rent cover the in- terest on the loan and the repayment of the principal? The Clerk: That is the idea.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR CLARKE'S OBJECTIONS. Councillor Clarke: If that is so, first of all I should object to these houses being built at the expense of the ratepayers. If the rent will only partly pay for the loan, and the remainder has to come out of the ratepayers' pockets, I shall object. Further, if the interest and principal is going to come out of the rent, and the prin- cipal is repaid at the end of 30 years, then the tenants will have paid for their own houses, and still they won't be their own. (Hear, hear.) It is not fair to take money out of a tenant's pocket for a house an' then at the end of 30 years it won't be his.</p>
        <p>Councillor Westbrook: This is not the same as the co-operative principle. Councillor Clarke: They do become the ten- ants and then owners, but in this case the tenants will not possess them after they have paid for them. Councillor Westbrook: Is that not the case with any tenant? Councillor Clarke: No; because the man who expends the money—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>"NOT A COMMERCIAL MAN."</head>
        <p>"NOT A COMMERCIAL MAN."</p>
        <p>Councillor Westbrook made some inaudible remark, to which Councillor Clarke replied: You are not a eom- mercial man.</p>
        <p>Councillor Westbrook: Are your views those of a commercial man? If so, I think I am in a position to congratulate myself on not being one. Councillor Clarke: The co-operative principle is different entirely. They wipe the capital ex- pended off with the profits. The ordinary com- mercial business— Councillor Westbrook: Is this commercial business?</p>
        <p>Councilor Clarke: If the tenants of the Coun- cil cottages pay for the erection, and there is no charge on the rates, the cottages ought to be given to the tenants. Councillor Westbrook: I don't agree with that. The District Council will be landlords of that property. It seems to be a similar argument for any tenant who pays rent. After a certain number of years he has bought the house. . . _</p>
        <p>--. Councillor Clarke; The rent is income from it.</p>
        <p>RATE OF INTEREST. • Councillor Westbrook: I am told in this case —I don't say they are right—they have put the interest at 5 per cent. and they are going to borrow it at 31 per cent., and the interest is going to go towards paying the capital. I don't say that because a man has paid rent for a number of years that he has a right to call it his house, in this case more than any other. Councillor Clarke: If we borrowed the money we should probably have to pay 5 per cent, and not get it at 3?,i per cent. Councillor Bottomley : Not 5 per cent. In the co-operative principle if you put .130 down they will advance you the rest of the money, and the interest will be 4 per cent. Councillor Clarke: At the end of that time is it the tenant's own house? Councillor Bottomley : Exactly. Councillor Clarke: I approve of that. Councillor Bottomley : I should like to know if there is any urgent need for these houses in the village. The Chairman: It has not been proved there is. A DIFFICULT MATTER.</p>
        <p>Councillor Bottomley : Is it so that if a person wanted a house it is a difficult matter to get one?</p>
        <p>Voices: No, it is not. Councillor McDermid: Only there has been a difficulty in obtaining a certain class of house. Councillor Bottomley: Has it got to the pitch that something must be done? Councillor McDermid: It has come to this, that private enterprise is not providing in the ordinary way to supply the demand, and the question comes for us to take it up. Councillor Bottoruley : Have we got to the , Position whereby something must be done? Is it a question of Blackburn? A Voice: No. Councillor Clarke: It is not for Stockton Heath people, but for those coming in. Councillor McDermid : It is a queStion if yen are prepared to rest at your present state of de- velopment in Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>DELAY IS MOVED. • Councillor Bottomley : I move, "That having been informed it is not necessary that we should immediately commence building operations that the matter be deferred for six months." He thought the time when a public ,body should begin to build, and that waa to inter- fere with private enterprise, was when they were absolutely compelled to do it, and if they were not in that position it was too soon to com- mence. Councillor Westbrook said it was not fair to use the rates of a particular district for the purpose of providing houses unless there was a natural increase of those in the district. Was I there overcrowding, or was there anyone pres- ent who wanted to get married and could not I because there were no houses? (Laughter.) He objected to spending the ratepayers' money in order to provide accommodation for people who came in from outside. If the private builder liked to do it. well and good, it was his own I enterprise. .(Hear, hear.) He seconded Conn- ' cillor Bottomley's resolution. Councillor Bottomley : I strongly support the building by a public authority when the neces-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <p>L</p>
        <p>"FAIRLY AND SQUARELY."</p>
        <p>Councillor McDermid said they ought to con- sider the matter fairly and squarely. It had been put by Councillors Clarke and Westbrook that they wished to build at the expense of the ratepayers. That was not put squarely, because they were not going to build at the expense of the ratepayers, but on the security of the rate- payers. In regard to building in competition with private enterprise he might say that there was a state of affairs in which private enter- prise was practically stifled. It then followed as to whether it was the function of the local body to supply or not. The village had not developed from inside, but outside, and they ought to con- sider in that connection that they as a public body were in a position to borrow money on more favourable terms than private individuals. They ought to consider the matter fully, and give it fair consideration, and not dismiss it in a simple 05-hand manner or postpone it. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Westbrook said if they built houses the district was liable to repay the loan. Supposing the houses came empty on account of a Councillor McDermid : That is improbable. Houses of this class very seldom become a bur- den on the owners. On a vote being taken the following resolu- tion was carried: " That no evidence having been submitted as to the necessity of erecting these houses by the District Council, the matter be deferred for six months."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</head>
        <p>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>A fast running man, A policeman running still faster, A crowd of men racing in their rear. These threw Heathens into excitement. It was all over the strike at certain works. The man was a non-striker and the Policeman was his protector, and the Crowd were pickets seeking a capture. And the onlookers got the best of the fun. Tar-paved roads, said Alderman Bolton, at The meeting of Warrington Council, are good enough For an out-lying district like Stockton Heath. But all the same we've better roails than The ancient borough of Warrington possesses. The Parochial Committee have found still Another grievance at the Swing Bridge. They think it needs a lamp in the Centre to light it more adequately. And truth it does, so the Ship Canal Co. Are to be told to provide a light forthwith.</p>
        <p>House Sold for £Boo.—Messrs. A. H. Clive and Co., auctioneers, Warrington, at a public auction at the Blue Bell Hotel, Warrington, on Wednesday, sold Brook House, Stockton Heath, for £BOO.</p>
        <p>Girls' Friendly Society.—Miss Whiteley, an organiser of the Girls' Friendly Society, gave an address on Monday evening in the Church Room. Miss Whiteley- dealt with the movement and the progress of the work that is being car- ried on in different parts of the world.</p>
        <p>Twenty Steps Bridge.—ln regard to the erec- tion of a public convenience at Twenty Steps Bridge, the Clerk reported at the Parochial Committee meeting on Tuesday that the Ship Canal Company did not object to its erection, subject to certain conditions.</p>
        <p>What Did It Mean?—The question of conver- sion at houses in the village was again raised at the Parochial Committee meeting on Tues- day. One estimate was for £9 10s. per house in Gaskell-street. Councillor Clarke: He means building the house as well. (Laughter.)—The Surveyor was instructed to prepare specifica- tions and get estimates, stating that 800 houses would be treated.</p>
        <p>Prompt Fire Fighters.—The Fire Brigade dis- tinguished themselves at the recent outbreak near London Bridge, and giving a report before the Parochial Committee on Tuesday, Council- lor McDermid said the brigade were on the spot in seven minutes, and the damage was only about .2.50. Councillors Hallows and Mclntyre were with the men. The Brewery could be called at any time, and the conditions obtain- ing now in case of an outbreak were satisfac- tory.—Councillor Clarke stated that in two months the Brewery would have a new motor engine with a capacity of 300 gallons per min- ute. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Oh! That Bridge !—Members of the Parochial Committee have another grievance now, and it was discussed on Tuesday in the Hall Cafe.— Councillor Bottomley said a lamp was needed in the middle of the bridge, as there was sure to be an accident if•there were not one erected. He thought they could not rap at the Canal Company too often.—Councillor Whitley pointed out that they had two lamps on this,side of the bridge, and there was only one on the Warring- ton side.—Doubts were expressed as to the pos- sibility of erecting a lamp account of the bridge having to turn, but it was stated that an electric lamp would meet the case. in the middle on</p>
        <p>Walton-road Dangers.—The fatal accident in Walton New-road was mentioned at the Paro- chial Committee meeting on Tuesday, when Councillor Westbrook said that the attention of the County Council should be directed to the urgent necessity of widening Walton New-road, opposite the Toll Bar Cottagesi They had, he said—referring to a previous discussion on the housing question—been studying the natural in- crease, but the fatal accident was a case of the unnatural decrease of the inhabitants.—The general opinion was that it was extremely neces- sary to have the road altered, and it is hoped steps will be taken in the near future.—The Clerk stated that the County Council were moving in that direction, and that resolution from the Committee might help them forward. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Funeral of Motor Victim.—Deep and wide- spread sympathy was revealed at the funeral of Joseph Carter, whose tragic death from a motor accident was reported in our last issue. The funeral took place on Monday afternoon at St. Thomas' Churchyard, and large numbers at- tended. The service was conducted by the Vicar, the Rev. J. Collins, and Mr. A. Moun- field read the lesson. Floral tributes were re- ceived from: "The Family and Aunt Alice," "His Aunts and Uncles," "Uncle John and Aunt Pricilla," Mrs. Payton, The Council School, "Some Friends from the Independent Methodist Church," "The Junior C.E. Society of the Independent Methodist Church," and "The Wesleyan Sunday School." A handsome permanent wreath with glass cover was also sent, subscribed for by friends and neighbours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>N GT 1.141, t. 444442:64</head>
        <p>N GT 1.141, t. 444442:64</p>
        <p>(Regd. Trade 11ar.L.,1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>COFFEE ESSENCE</head>
        <p>COFFEE ESSENCE</p>
        <p>The Coffee Maid, of SyminVon's, says:</p>
        <p>"Sing a song of Symington's, the kettle's on the slit:, " - A little drop of Essence, makes Coffee for a kin.' THOS. SYMINCTON 451 CO.. Edinburiaz. and Landon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <p>ER</p>
        <p>SATURDAYP MA4CI-1 7,1914.</p>
        <p>LAYMIVI.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR GOULD'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Accidental Fall From An Express Train</p>
        <p>IMPRESSIVE FUNERAL SERVICE.</p>
        <p>The tragic death of Councillor W. Gould, who I fell from a Midland express near Kettering, created a great sensation in I yram during the week-end. There was an impressive funeral on Monday afternoon, the attendance probably being the largest seen at Lymm for many years. All classes and creeds assembled to pay the last respects to one who was honoured and most -highly .esteemed in the district. A short service was held at Councillor Gould's residence, and the cortege proceeded to Higher- lane Baptist Chapel, where the Rev. F. E. Lovell conducted the funeral service. The at- tendance ;was so great that there was only suffi- cient room in the church for the more immedi- ate friends of the family.</p>
        <p>The hymn "Abide with me" was sung with great feeling, Miss H. Brundrett presiding at the organ. The scene at the interment in the adjoining graveyard was very impressive. There were no floral tributes by special request of the family.</p>
        <p>The chief mourners present were: Mr. James Gould and Mr. S. W. Gould (brothers), Mr. W. C. Moore (father-in-law), Messrs. G. H. Clarke, Harry Moore, Sydney Moore, Wilfred C. Moore and W. H. Wrigg (brothers-in-law), Messrs. James Gould, jun., Wiliam Gould, jun., James Clarke and Norman Clarke (nephews). Messrs. H. Daniels, P. Ingham, W. Mill, J. Sutton, J. Whitfield and Newton Wortillington were the bearers.</p>
        <p>The following members and officers of the Lymm Urban District Council attended: Mr. S. Davies (chairman), Mr. R. H. Gotobed (vice- chairman), Rev. G. B. ,Thurston, and Messrs. T. F. W. Dixon, W. G. Groves, J. H. Holt, F. Lockyer, 0. G. Smith, G. L. Welford, W. Mul- lard (clerk), D. Mort (surveyor), W. L. Donald- son (gas manager), C. I. Barnes (water man- ager), Dr. E. M. de Jong (medical officer), H. Simpson (Fire Brigade), W. M. Beckett and W. Mort.</p>
        <p>A POPULAR PERSONAGE. The deceased gentleman, who was 44 years of age, was the youngest son of the late Mr. Gould, farmer, of Bollington, near Altrincham. HiS two brothers and sister are well-known local in- habitants : Mr. James Gould, the noted Shire horse breeder, of Crouchley Farm, Lymm:•Ml4• Siunuel Walker Gould, retired farmer, of Bridge House, Oughtrington, and Mrs. G. H. Clarke, of Field House, Higher-lane, Lymm. In business Mr. W. Gould was a grey cloth mer- chant, in Manchester. He was the popular representative of the Lymm Ward on the Lymm Urban District Council, where he has performed great service to the locality, one of his last duties being that of geblk On a tour of inspection with -Council- lors Davies and Thurston to various sewage farms, for the purpose of obtaining first-hand information before the laying out of the new Lymm sewage farm. He was a strong advocate for the Burford-lane lighting scheme. Though a Nonconformist and a Liberal', his genial and generous character won for him a wide circle of friends amongst persons of all creeds and parties. He took an active interest in the Broom Edge and District Horticultural Society; had a keen partiality for racing plgeons; was a generous supporter of local ath- letic clubs, and a thorough sportsman. He leaves a widow, a daughter of Mr. W. C .Moore, late of Lymm, and a little daughter, for whom great sympathy is felt in their sad bereave- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>"ACCIDENTAL DEATH."</head>
        <p>"ACCIDENTAL DEATH."</p>
        <p>---•—•-•--- —• ••• The inquest on the body was held at Ketter- ing, when a verdict of "Accidental death was returned. Mr. James Gould, a brother, Crouch- ley Farm, identified the body. , Dr. Dryland said the injuries consisted of a compound fracture of the skull, a large portion of which was entirely smashed. There was also dislocation' of the left shoulder. A portion of the left foot was missing. The injury to the head alone would cause instantaneous death. Richard Daniels. Rose Lea, Heatley, cotton handkerchief manufacturer, said he and Mr. Mills went with Mr. Gould to London on Mon- day, and returned by the 2.40 Manchester ex- press on Friday morning. Witness and Mr. Gould occupied a third-class compartment. They lay down and both went to sleep. He remembered waking feeling very cold, and dis- covered Mr. Gould missing, and the outer door of the carriage in the corridor open. He arouse 4 Mr. Mills, who was asleep, and they searched the corridor in vain. They pulled the communication cord and stopped the train. The fireman and guard came, and witness explained what had happened. With the guard they walked along the line, and were informed that the body had been found. In reply to the Coroner, witness said they had been to Sandown Park Races on Thursday, re- turning td London at 7.30. Mr. Gould was quite all right, sober, and clear, but very tried. The carriage was in darkness, as they put the green shades over the lights. Mr. Walter Mills, Riversdale. Heatley, cotton designer thought Mr. Gould must, when half asleep, have opened the wrong door. The Coroner said that doubtless, whilst very tired, Mr. Gould got up suddenly in his sleep and opened the wrong door. pressed sy The jury ex mpathy with the relatives.</p>
        <p>OTHER REPRESENTATIVES. d b Other Persons present included Mr. Nield, the manager a warehouse: the late Mr. Gould's Manchester represente T. ill ' ---Ym Tradesmen's Association, W. E. Hillier (hon. y Mr. D. Daniels (I)resident), Mr. secretary), and Mr. A. B. Richardson (hon. treasurer); Lymm Liberal Association, represented by Mr. T. G. Bate and 1 Mr. F. A. Morris; Higher-lane Mission, repre- sented by Mr. G. Bennett; Lynam Subscription Band, represented by, Mr. W. H. Daniels; the following, Council employes: Messrs. T. Blain, J. Broadey, W. Challinor, Daniels, J. W. Davies, H. Diamond, Downward, H. Drink- water, E. Gilberts, J. Griffiths, E. Hazelhurst, H. Marsh, D. Ratcliffe, W. Ratcliffe, S. Shaw, G. Simpson and J. Southern; Manchester Fly- ing Club, represented by Messrs. Wolstenholme (president), Barcroft, Bruce, Burrows, Cun- liffe, Darbyshire, Dixon, Duckworth, Green- ough, Griffiths T ' I --egg, Lord, Lowe, Miller, Nel- son, Lancashire Saturday Federation of Flying and Peters. y Messrs. PaehleY, Sinclair, Walker and Yates; West Clubs, represented b - - Moston, Newton •...</p>
        <p>Messrs. C. E. Ardern, J. BrearleY, Amongst local gentlemen present were: P. R. Brazendale, J. E. Buzzard, H. G.' Carr, Clarke, R. Clarke, S. G. Collins, G. A. Cook (who organised the cortege), S. Critchley, S. 11. Davies, F. E. Evans, Hans Frege, C. Gleave, T. Griffiths, T. C. Heaven, R. Hinde, S. Hinde, T. S. Hoblyn, 11. Holt,,J. Kemp, W. IC. H. S. Brown, Kershaw, E. Landless, M. Lea, A. Leather, J. Legh, A. New- kin, P. Peter,s, T. Peters, New- Mclntyre, W. Mclntyre, D. iNfolneux, T. Moore, E. Morris, Moss, 11. Munroe, J. R. ton, T. Newton, J. Palmer, J. Pearson, A. Per- Saville, 11. S. Saville, jun , T. Saville, W. Shakesbaft, J. Sheppard, E. S.impson, G. Smith, E. Thomason, H. 'llomasori, L. Thomason. J. Travis, K. N. Tweedale, P. Warburton. L. War- burton, J. 11. Warham, E. Whitlow, Willis, F. 'Wilson and J. Winstanley. Many other gen. tlemen from Manchester district were in attend- ance. . H. Rhodes, H. S.</p>
        <p>Service of Song.—A service of song to extol the coming of the angels was rendered by the choir of the Congregational Church, assisted by the members of the Bucklow Hill Church, on Sunday afternoon. The solo was taken by Mrs. Lewis. The duet was beautifully rendered by Mr. E. Leather and Miss Annie Miller, and the quartet, "There came a little child to earth," was nicely rendered by the children. The descriptive reading by Mrs. Willan was listened to very attentively. The tenor solo was excellently rendered by Mr. T. Hewitt. There was a good attendance and a good collec- tion for the choir fund was taken.</p>
        <p>United Service.—The monthly united service under the auspices of the Lymm Free Church Council was held at Broom Edge Primitive Methodist Chapel on Wednesday night, when the Rev. W. Daw was the preacher. Taking as his text Ephesians iv. 13 (revised version), Mr. Daw spoke of the unity which comes from faith and said that a more precise knowledge of God would bring people closer together. He re- quested his congregation to study Christ anew and get a fuller knowledge of Him, and they would be more charmed than ever with His life and would become a centre for attracting men to Him.</p>
        <p>R.A.0.8. Lodge.—There was another large gathering at the above lodge on Monday, the occasion being the raising to Second Degree of Bro. J. Atherton, C.S., and also a presentation of the jewel to Bro. F. A. Morris and a Knight's Certificate to Bro. J. Wilcock, K.O.M. Amongst those present, in addition to a large number of P.G.L. officers, were the P.G.P., Manchester Province, Bro. C. T. Wilson, K.O.M. (who per- formed the ceremony); Bro. F. Earley, K.0.M., Deputy Grand Primo of All England. The pro- ceedings were carried out in a most able man- ner. 'The D.G.P. of England presented the cer- tificate to Bro. Wilcock, K. 0.111., and the D.G.F. of Manchester, Bro. Davies, K.0.M., presented the jewel to Bro. Morris. The usual toasts were duly honoured and the various re- cipients responded. Visits were paid from the Knights Chapter, Manchester; "The Golden Fleece," "P. Bonnell," "Duke of Lancaster" and "Harmonic Hope" (Rochdale) Lodges. Harmony was, rendered by Bros. Thoma- son and Ditclifield, " Sir " J. Howarth, and others, in a creditable manner.</p>
        <p>Temperance Women.—The members of the Lymm branch of the National British Women's Temperance Association partook of a very en- joyable tea in the Eagle Brow Schoolroom on Tuesday evening, after which the first annual ineeting was held. Mrs. G. L. Welford, the president, occupied the chair, and the treasur- er's and secretary's annual reports were given by Mesdames J: Edwards and F. P. 'Brown re- spectively. Steady progress during the year was reported. Mrs. Welford gave a short but ' encouraging address. The members showed ' their confidence in the officers by according them a hearty vote of thanks for past services and re-electing them for another year. Miss Bate was elected as assistant secretary. At the conclusion of the business meeting a con- cert was given. Miss Doris Scrag contributed two pianoforte solos, " Idylle" and "Madeline," which were beautifully rendered, whilst Miss Bate's songs, "Come unto Me" and "Thoughts have wings," were also well received. Miss Armstrong added to her reputation as an elocu- tionist with the items "Lady Clare" and "The bachelor's sale." Miss llolt was the accom- panist.</p>
        <p>A Nurse Girl's Claim.—An echo of the inter- esting case, heard last November, in which Miss Margaret Ann Marsh, Booths-lane, Lymm, was awarded £3 for wages due for nursing the late Mr. Richards, billposter, Lymm, under whose will Mr. D. Daniels, confectioner, Lymm, was the executor, was heard at the Warrington County Court on Thursday, when Miss Marsh applied for an execution to be issued for .23 against the executor.—Mr. A. Browne, for ap- plicant described the case as out of the ordin- ary. His Honour had given judgment against defendant, who was the executor under the will of the late Mr. Richards, and found that .23 of the estate had not been expended. The costs were taxed, and defendant had paid them, but nothing of the judgment. Defendant was carry- ing on the business, and was wilfully refusing to pay the money, which was wages due to the girl.—Mr. Steel said defendant was willing to pay half the amount to save further litigation ..proceedings.—Mr. Browne: I would sooner pay it myself than accept that for her.—His Honour: She would get the whole amount.then, Mr. Browne.—After sonic: argument as to the amount to be paid, his Honour awarded appli- cant £1 155., which Mr. Steel promised would be paid.</p>
        <p>• May Queen Festival—lt has been stated that there was to be no May Queen Festival this year. A further meeting is to be held to con- sider the matter, and a full report as to the financial position of the festival will be pre- sented. This meeting, the secretary informs us, will be held at an early date. Carter's Tragic End.—The tragic story of the death of William Neild, of Radley Hill, Heat- ley, a carter in the employ of Messrs. James Thornley and Sons, millers, of Warburton; was unfolded at the inquest held by Mr. H. C. Yates at Altrincham on Thursday. Nei.id was returning on Monday evening from Manchester with a lurry laden with bags. Ile and two other carters were proceeding along Sinderland- road, Dunham Massey, and stopped to light their lamps. When the three vehicles started the carter in charge of the rear luny- saw. Some little distance ahead an object lying in the road, which he thought was a log or bag, but it was discovered to be Neild's dead body. He had evidently fallen under the wheel of the hurry, which had a load of 31 tons, and the wheel had passed over his head. He was stated to be perfectly sober.—ln answer to the Coroner, one of the men said there had been no larking among them.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death."—Neild, who was 35 years of age, leaves a widow and several chil- dren. The funeral takes place to-day (Satur- -1 a 3)•</p>
        <p>NOWSPLIVERING GLASS. A t•ery ingenious process for producing glass which will not splinter and fly about when broken has been devised, according to "La Nature," by a French inventor. One side of each of two glass plates is covered with a thin coating of gelatine, a thin clear plate of celluloid is laid between these two surfaces, and glass and celluloid are then united by strong pressure into a single pane. The ." triplex pane" thus produced is said to lose little in transparency, while it gains the valuable quality of not splin- tering when struck or pressed against. ,While it does not become unbreakable the fragments do not fly, being held by the gelatine layer, and hence no damage is done to bystanders from fly- ing shards. Tests showed that the most violent blows with a hammer merely caused radiating cracks and concentric rings of cleavage.</p>
        <p>GRAPPEN HALL.</p>
        <p>Confirmation.—A confirmation service was held at the Parish Church on Thursday after- noon by the Bishop of Chester. It was a most impressive service. The Rev. H. V. Pigot con- ducted the service, and the Rev. G. C. Briggs carried the pastoral staff. Other clergy present were the Revs. H. Gray, L. J 7 T...._Darwall, W.</p>
        <p>_ Hendy Cock, F. E. Evans, And J. Holden. There were 60 candidates presented, as follows: Grap- penhall, 27; Appleton, 18; Christ Church, 8; Walton, 6; Antrobus, 1.</p>
        <p>Parish Meeting.7-The 'annual parish meeting was held at the conclusiOn of the Council meet- ing, Mr. A. L. Kaye presiding. Mr. F. L. Gar- side asked how the district stood in relation to the sewerage scheme, Mr. A. L. Kaye stated that he had done his best to modify the scheme Without avail, and, as it was now, practically settled, they could do no more in the matter.— Mr. F. Leach called attention to", the disreput- able state of the footpath leading from Knuts-: ford-road to Chester-road, stating that it was almost impassable, and moved that it be a recommendation to the Parish Council to have it put in order.—This was carried.</p>
      </div>
