      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>PEN K ETH.</head>
        <p>PEN K ETH.</p>
        <p>Accident at Penketh Tannery.—On Thursday afternoon Mr. E. Bond, Widnes-road, Penketh, had an accidcnt which caused injury to his hand while working at Penketh Tannery. Dr. Murray attended to it, and the injured man was later conveyed to the Warrington Infirm- ary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>GREAT SAN K EY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SAN K EY.</p>
        <p>Footpath and Bridge.—The question of the bad state of the road leading to Buttermilk Bridge was._again_ discussed at the Rural Dis-</p>
        <p>trict-Council Meeting on Wednesday, and Coun- cillor Furness said the footpath was one of the most frequently used in the district and needed attending to. (Hear, hear.)—The Clerk and the Surveyor were instructed to take the neces- sary steps to get the matter rectified.—Council- lor Furness moved that the Clerk write tho</p>
        <p>County Council in regard to laying down the bridge, for which they had money from the Government. The Parish- Council, said Coun- cillor Furness, had widened the road, and there should be nothing to stop the matter from going on. ([tear, hear.)</p>
        <p>1 . , Miss M. A. Haslam's Coming of Age.—Mr- and Mrs. E. Haslam, of Belmont, Great Sankelr• invited their relatives and friends on Satiirda:f , to an enjoyable evening at the Willmar- leigh Cafe to celebrate the coming of age of their daughter, Miss M. A. Haslam. The coal- o pany numbered over 100. A very enjoyable time was -spent. Mr. F. C. Gill, of Penketb. proposed the toast of the evening, which was supported by Mr. A. Grime, of Ramsbottom. Mr. Haslam suitably responded. Danctng took place, the music, being supplied by Messrs. Hall Bros. Mr. F. ITaslam was the M.C. Songs were rendered by Messrs. S. Aukland, Liverpool (humorist); W. Tattersall, Littleborongh; 'l'. Holt, Bury; H. Haslam, Ramsbottom; F. Has iall', o m tt, - Warrington. Master W. Holt, Bury, sup- plieda recitation, : and Mr. F. Russell, Rams- trombone solos. Mr. C. Hall, Barns- bottom, was the accompanist. The guests after- wards had a game of whist. Mr. Gill was the M.C. The lady's prize was won by Mrs. Car- rington, Penketh, and the gentleman's. by Mr. F. Russell.</p>
        <p>LIFTING-POWER IN GROWING PLANTS. The rapidity with which drooping leaves re- vive under a shower shows the extraordinary quickness with which plant roots take up water and drive it through the stem to the uppermost twigs and leaves. A simple way of discovering how rapidly this takes place is by watering a white iris with a blue solution. In a few hours its white flowers will be streaked with blue. The power exerted 17 the roots and stems of growing trees is almost .incredible. The roots of a young oak are known to have lifted a brock of granite weighing 6i tons. The lifting began in the fifth year of the tree's growth, and within ,tlie next seven years the stone was raised more than eight inches. This remarkable feat has been exceeded by the American swamp cypress, which throws out immense buttresses around its stem. A barge loaded with bricks was wrecked on one of these buttresses in the Oklawaha river. and six years later the growth of the tree had lifted the wreck above water.</p>
