      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>PARISH CC"._ NCIL MATTERS. The Question of Boundary Alteration</head>
        <p>PARISH CC"._ NCIL MATTERS. The Question of Boundary Alteration</p>
        <p>The Parish Council meeting was held in the Council Saw'ls On Thursday night, Mr. A. L. Kaye presiding. Other councillors present were: Messrs. P. M. Appleton, E. Allen, W. Winstanley, R. W. Wilde, W. Lomas, J. Brad- ford, with M. C. 11. Hoult, clerk. The accounts showed an income of .816 is. Bd., expenditure .616 3s. 5d., including an election account of .612 10s. 6d., leaving a deficit of is. 9d.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Bradford proposed that a further reso- lution be sent to the Cheshire County Council re the dangerous concealed turning at the Cartridge-lane end, and requesting them to see into the matter.</p>
        <p>ALTERATION OF BOUNDARIES. The District Council Committee requested that the Parish Council should send a representative in opposition to the proposed alterations of the Lancashire and Cheshire boundaries. A meetilig is to take place on April Ist at a place to be settled later. Mr. W. A. Wooding proposed that Mr. A. L. Kaye be deputed to attend. Mr. Kaye remarked that if the scheme was carried it would take away about ot 3,000 from the rateable value of Gimp- penhall. POSTAL FACILITIES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Postmaster of Warrington as follows: "Referring to your letter of the 2nd of October and your further letter of the 4th inst., respecting an extension of the postal facilities at Grappenhall, I have pleasure in informing you that sanction has been obtained for a day mail delivery to be made at all places in Grappenhall. as far as Flowshoots, and the erection of a letter-box there. It is regretted that the day mail cannot be extended to Cherry-lane and Bradley Brook, and it will also be necessary to transfer these places to the Lymm district for the morning service; the alteration to take effect as soon as possible." The Clerk was instructed to reply to the Postmaster, thanking him for the inter- est he has displayed in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>APPLETON.</head>
        <p>APPLETON.</p>
        <p>Rifle Club.—The February competitions were completed on Saturday evening, Results: H "A" Division—A. Shepherd, Ist . prize and "Daily Telegraph" Certificate, 198; F. Gm: cott, 2nd prize, 194; H. Baines. 3rd prize, 193. "B" Division-1, C. Shepley, 199; 2, arri p, son, 192; 3, J. W. Elliott, 188.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>HATTON.</head>
        <p>HATTON.</p>
        <p>Billiards.—A billiard handicap in connection with the Parish Room terminated on Tuesday evening. The winners were: 1, .3'. Houghton; 2, T. Butter; 3, P. Cliffe. The final between Rutter and Houghton proved very exciting. Butter looked all over a winner in the first hun- dred, but in the second he was severely handi- capped for position, and Houghton, being favoured with good positions, ran out an easy winner.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. J. Blackshaw.—One Of the oldest inhabitants of Hatton passed peacefully away on Saturday Aiming, in the person of Mrs. J. Blackshaw, widoW of ,the late Mr. John Black- shaw, Blue Coat Farm. The deceased lady came of a good old stock of yeoman farmers. Her father and mother were both over 90 years of age when they died, and Mrs. Blackshaw was in her 83rd year. The interment took place at Daresbury Church on Wednesday, the Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones performing the last rites. Many beautiful wreaths testified to the respect in which the departed lady was held. Her whole life was marked by her intense kindness to both man and beast.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>Guild Festival.—This interesting function was held on Sunday and, Monday in the Wesleyan Church. On Sunday sermons were preached by the Rev. T. Hartley, of Liverpool, and special music was rendered by the choir. In the school- room on Monday evening Mr. Hartley gave an interesting lecture on "Oliver Wendell Holmes." The chairman was Mr. J. B. Isaac, of Prod- All the services were well attended, and the collections showed an increase on the pre- vious year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>Who is at Fault?—At the meeting bf the War- rington Rural District Council on Wednesday, a lengthy discussion took place on the allega- tion of the Asylum that the Council were let- ting crude matter get on to the land at the Asylum. Samples of water had been taken at the Asylum, and Mr. Newcombe (surveyor) read the analyst's report, which, according to several councillors, proved that the Asylum were at fault in the matter.—lt was decided to write the Asylum authorities pointing out that their statements were erroneous and asking them to put in order anything that might be found wrong by the Inspector and the Surveyor. ,</p>
      </div>
