      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>AMalgamation. This, like the castles some are so fond of building, is at present very much in the air. According to an " Examiner" paragraph, Lud- deaden Foot has declined to be gobbled U by Sowerby Bridge, the voting being 76 for and 166 against. I know the district in question • thor- oughly. and I infer from the figures that a com- paratively small percentage of the inhabitants took the trouble to express an •opinion, so that the majority appear to be indifferent. I know of several other schemes of the kind, and many of those similarly affected hotly resent the pro - Posed amalgamation. Readers do not need to be reminded of a local instance.</p>
        <p>Lud.&amp;e., ViThen. I lived in that part of the country I once tried to get at the origin of that odd place- name Luddenden. Through the village flows a treaan called the Lad, and I was told that in lariner times the place was simply called Lad- lean Subsequently it became Ludden-dean, ,a.nd hence Luddenden. But there is actually in tne vicinity a Luddenden-dean, so what it will ultirnately become who shall say? Already in Pronouncing it one might by a stranger be sus- pected of stammering. Those who do me the "flour to glance over this column week by week Ina „ Y remember it was at Luddenden Foot Rail- .;?Y Station early in the history of the L. and elerk C.orapanY that the brilliant but reckless ranwell Bronte served for a while as booking l Of course, the porters who daily call the Lial ef the station for the information of the "arellers mostly make short work of it after Manner of their kind, "Foot" being the 17• 1114. est they will concede; and I recollect that a little amusement was caused when, away I°' the bygones, there was appointed on the staff boi extremely conscientious young fellow who, 01,.'11.Y. defying tradition and convention, habita- -44 tlaversed the length of a standing passenger n announcing with great deliberation, heeed-dane-dane-foot." I need hardly add that was rara avis. Ido not expect ever to look IlPon his like again. Time !</p>
        <p>• This. reference to the railway somehow re- Minds me of Mrs. Crosfield's funny story of the °ld Welsh peasant woman, and that in turn recalls the good old wheeze of the fidgety dame , travelling in that direction, was most 11„uxious as to the whereabouts of Llanfairfechan. • Gulled, guard," she began in good time on the 'eurrie:e. "is this Llanfairfechan Station?" 'Oh, dear, no, madam," was the reply,," you're guide a long way from Llanfairfechan Yet." But St the next stopping-place, and the next after that, the old lady repeated her question with "ident concern; so the good-natured official in Charge of the train assured her that he would let her know when she reached her destination, and Prevailed upon her to settle comfortably\ down and worry no more about it. Llanfairfeclian reached, he flung open the door of the old lady's compartment and exclaimed Now, ma'am!" "Yes, what is it, guard?" she returned, betray- ing but languid interest as compared with her former perturbation. "Llanfairfechan," said "come, hurry up, please, lady." "Thank Yen, guard," quietly responded she, "I don't Want to get out here. I've a long way to go yet, but you see my doctor told me that when the train got to Llanfairfechan it would be time for Me to take this medicine!" .</p>
        <p>4 Was at the same crowded meeting addressed 17 Mrs. Crosfield that a lady in the audience,: it'reBll.l:oably a member of the Women's 'Liberal interposed with a suggestion that stmeq-called Unionists might appropriately be Of " The Bow-Wow Party." I was reminded the- !After rain young fellow who once joined ti-.l„Bufff of a paper on which I was working. He what nowadays we should term a toe ead an amazing taste in neckties, and seemed W 1,410 to have an endless variety of changes. "ell I'o be appeared at the A fresh tie, he , s stand before the glass admiring himself, tl.audibly repeating '7 Doggie, Williams; °ggie. doggie." This was how .0110 colleague 11-11 0 lad little patience with this amiable weak- Z.ese came to speak pf Williams as " that 1)0W Party."</p>
        <p>A Big Spider. -"le discovery of a live snake among bananas in the Warrington Market set me thinking of °ther incidents of the sort, some of which I may „.llllsinhlY have mentioned before—one needs a •°°(l memory indeed to avoid repetition in a WeeklY column consisting largely of reminis- ' ~elle", The one most like last week's occurred while the employes of a wholesaler were open- ing a crate on a market ground, when out popped a tarantula spider. It was captured and added ti° the collection in, the local museum. I had Vl' Years a locust which came in a bale of •EllYptian cotton, and I have had my attention ealled to many curious oddments among foreign Wheats. There are two things in this connec- ,tion that have puzzled me and have never been explained to my satisfaction. I, once discovered illlnY cellar, among coals shot into the place several weeks before, a very fine newt, not merely alive but literally kicking. Close by the Fame house, while -opening the road to repair a leaking main, some men found in the ground a live death's head moth. ' Colour Cure. •</p>
        <p>_ The w titer on " 'Womenls Questions" for this Paper had a paragraph on " Colours asis stimU 'ant to growth," starting an interesting theory Which it Nvould be well to have ",established on scientific authority." While of course Unable to help in that direction. I may state that nearly 30 years ago I interviewed for the Press an indi- vidual who claimed to be aide to cure many of the ills that flesh is heir to after the manner now question. lie had it number of glass vessels such aS we used to see in the windows of chem- ist , SItUpS, only eatment for a given disease had simply to gaze • l'ug upon a colour specially indicated as the vltlllklire agency in that particular complaint. ' iill4,s• few instances the result seemed highly tiZaktory, but the enterprising quack did not t6-4,8a success of it on the whole. However, ge;;lfilere is something in the idea is now, pretty cobletalty recognised. There are people who "d not long remain in a room with a red wall 11Per; it would excite them almost to frenzy, eas a blue paper would inamediately have • soothing effect.</p>
        <p>kJ% ,Titoman ia. i,„4,11e ease of Nurse Millicent Thorpe, reported :'ast week's Borough Police Court news, would '"d'Pear to be one 'of kleptomania, though to be of all that I ever met with, suchla sugges- tion has never availed where the accused has been °r and undefended. The plea has succeeded 'flat least two in my experience. One was that elderly married woman who systematic- -"Y robbed market stalls over a considerable 11 LenriTl• When at last she was caught red- we- say, and her house 'came to be o—,arched by the police, there was quite a load : Stolen goods to remove.. One of. her sons in- 15ited this unfortunate tendency, and it cost. 41! a good situation. The other case was that 41'4 !wife of a distinguished and honoured e;d4efl. and in her behalf it was successfully 'eaded before .the magistrates that the peculiar istote of her health at the time was sufficient In nk,count for the offence. We were all tilled with sZillpath.V, and for some time thereafter dis- `-aYed great solicitude respecting the -lady's elfare. It was all very well, but one had an 111%83, feeling that if she had been a friendless taioloicure person without the means of re- f_,orling a clever lawyer and expert witnesses her 17: would have been far otherwise. It Used to wh:aid that " he uot steals what isn't his'n, I s 0 he's eotelted must go to Innen," but that It ; 110 Means invariably the case nowadays. 114 depends.</p>
        <p>LODIK OLD—Keep the rave ges of time at bay by tig the grey streaks withLocarEn' SULP Min IT AM t 0 ' QilltPt which, deepens to s..lnaturalcolourin a few days. e.Yere maintains a youthful appearance. Large hots. ,erTivhere.r oat free/0 Beat or 4 Laboratory,LoncionAr,</p>
