      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1914.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. "COMING OF AGE."</p>
        <p>The Class Started 21 Years Ago in an Amazing</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>and Striking Manner.</head>
        <p>and Striking Manner.</p>
        <p>THE REV. JOHN YONGE'S REMINISCENCES.</p>
        <p>There were great rejoicings at the Wycliffe P.S.A " coming of age" meetings on Sunday and Monday. Speeches, • pointed, fascinatimr yet, brief, bright and brotherly, were made; and each ..had some characteristic story to tell or some sound moral to teach. At the Monday night gathering the Rev. John Yonge,• one of the . founders aad the. first president of the P.S.A., told of the anxiety displayed over the start, how Warrington was placarded with in- vitations to .men to attend, and yet a Sew min- utes to the hour of start only 50 persons Were present. Then happened what be described as a strik- ing and amazing thing. Men began to pour into the church in a continuous stream, until the edifice was crowded, and the good ship Wycliffe P.S.A. was enthusiastically launched upon the course it has steered during the past 21&lt; years. At Sunday's gathering Councillor H. Roberts presided, and supporting him were the Rev. T. 'l'. James and Mr. 1L Lewis. Great disappoint- ment was felt that Mr. J. Seddon, ex-3+l.P. for the Newton Division, was ,unable to attend. Mr. W. H. Wallington was the vocal soloist, and interest centred round him because be sang at the first meeting. 'Mr.- W. Rutter is a capable cornet player, and his selections were much enjoyed. Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ, a position he filled at the birth of the society.</p>
        <p>NEVER LOOKED.OLD, The Rev. T. T. TAmss said it was his duty as president of that P.S.A. to welcome all the old friends, for they were glad to see them, as also</p>
        <p>battle, they would get a hearty response, for that would be showing the spirit of brotherhood. There was a great enthusiasm generated imme- diately. They could speak to an audience of intellectual people upon any subject they liked, including the sciences and arts, but they could get greater enthusiasm if they spoke about the man Who lived 1900 years ago. (A pplause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>PAST AND THE FUTURE.</head>
        <p>THE PAST AND THE FUTURE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JO/IN Y ONGE, who bad a hearty re- ception; said they had had glorious times in the past, and he hoped they would have better times in the future. In Mr. James they would find a splendid leader, and one they should follow loyally and heartily in the days to come. Mr: B. FARRAR announced that he had re- ceived the following message.from the Warring- ton P.S.A. :7-" The Warrington P.S.A. meeting to-day sends their. hearty congratulations to the Wycliffe Brotherhood on the attainment of their 21st anniversary, and trust that they will have a successful future.—WILLIAM CLEGG, President."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>REMINISCENCE NIGHT.</head>
        <p>REMINISCENCE NIGHT.</p>
        <p>Rev. J. Yonge Tells of a Striking awl Amazing incident.</p>
        <p>The celebration was continued on Monday evening, When a tea party and concert was held in the Wycliffe Memorial nail. At-,the con- cert the Rev. T. T. James presided, and SllP- porting him were the Revs. John Yonge and J.</p>
      </div>
