      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>A, FOUNDER'S STORY.</head>
        <p>A, FOUNDER'S STORY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YON6E said he had become un- accustomed'to public speaking, and was out of practice—(laughter)—but he felt he must speak on :in occasion like that. Ile could not resist it —(bear, hear)—for having been in at the birth, lie must be in at the majority, and as he hap- pened to be 'still living in the neighbourhood, it would have been treason to his sentiments of the P.S.A. Brotherhood if lie had not been with them.</p>
        <p>The fifth day of March, 1893, was a Sunday which those who were the initiators of the P.S.A. movement were never likely to forget, and who had expended a large amount of anxious thought and, earnest effort to make the thing a success from its introduction. He could not tell them the number of committee medtings they held, and the counsels they sought. There were tew movements of the kind in Warrington when Wycliffe started its P.S.A. However, there was</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. " COMING OF AGE ~_ continued.</p>
