      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>A use has been found for the cocoa shells which are a by-product of the chocolate industry in France, as fodder for cattle. The cows like</p>
        <p>- . it, and it nouriihing4 and fattening, having much the character of wheat bran, but it is thought by some authorities to lessen the milk yield, though not lowering (but even increas- ing) its fatty content. A report on the subject has been made by an agronomic engineer named Lucas- to the National Society of Agriculture of France. The question is to be further studied, and is of importance, as this product consists of no less than three to five million kilogrammes (5,000 tons) yearly, according to "Les Annales."</p>
        <p>' Seed is Their Currency. In his report to the Governor of New York, Joseph N. Francolini, one of the commission sent to Europe a few months ago to study the workings of agricultural banks, calls attention to an ancient system of banking which is still in active and successful operation in Southern Italy. This is that of the Monti Frumentari, or grain storage warehouses. In these are stored large quantities of seed, which is given out to farmers upon demand without payment. After the harvest .the farmers return to the vare- houses the amount of seed they have received, plus interest in seed. Thus do the farmers be- come established agriculturists without the necessity of borrowing money.</p>
        <p>Miraculous Escape. lii the Rue Petit, Paris, an Italian bricklayer named Valenti liigault was at work on the sixth floor of a scaffold When he fell on to the pave- ment below. People rushed up xpecting to find a dead man, but to their surprise Rigault got tip, put on his cap, which was lying near him, and as he brushed himself down, exclaimed, " That was a nasty bump. Where is the near- est place to get a drink?" The police insisted on removing him to the hospital, in spite of his protests that he was all right. The doctors ex- amined him, but could find no injury except a scratch on his elbow, and a few slight bruises 010 one side. Rigault now threatens to claim damages from the hospital for causing him to lose half a day's Work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>Rest Cure.</head>
        <p>The Rest Cure.</p>
        <p>The value of rest in -ti-fe medical management of acute inflammatory and infectious diseases is not.f ally appreciated. Its effect on the eircula-</p>
        <p>tion,is significant. The average daily output of energy by the heart is 400,000 foot-pounds. By simple restiin bed it is possible to save the heart a daily expenditure of 50,000 foot-pounds of energy. The taster the heart beats the less time it has fot rest; so that decreasing the pulse-rate saves tha heart (says the "Family Doctor "). Again in the recumbent position this organ is saved the labour of elevating that part of the</p>
        <p>tood which goes to parts above its own level.</p>
        <p>. - Nest of the voluntary muscles is still more portunt. An immense amount of energy evolved iii muscle movement is conserved by rest in bed.</p>
        <p>Hot Water for Children.</p>
        <p>In eases where there is non-assimilation of food, accompanied by vomiting, purging, and even convulsions, a well-known doctor orders all</p>
        <p>the hot water the child will take given through a nursing bottle. In one most severe ease where no kind of nourishment could be retained, and medicines did no good, The hot-water treatment was tried. The first bottle of water was imme- diately vomited, but the second was retained. 4111(1 a most peaceful sleep followed. The condi- tion of the bowels gradually improved, and vomiting gave very little trouble. One will be surprised to see how greedily the children drink it, and it will producti,a .quiet, peaceful sleep, and the extremities that were previously cold and clammy will become warm and .natural.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>To Reach the Men.</head>
        <p>To Reach the Men.</p>
        <p>lie •never dreamt of it being so, and never expected it. So far as he was -concerned, his only desire was to reach the men of Warrington with the members a society, and he agreed that the suc- cess of a P.S.A. did not rest upon the minister, them , t , e and make)el m' to look for them and bring the downtrodden respected but upon the members. (Hear, hear.) They should not fancy .they were in ,an armchair for an hour, but should strive to bring in someone else. .lle -hoped the 21st birthday wourii 'be a wonderful reinspiratiou to the whole of the Brotherhood for a 1 ,arger and wider service in the days that were to come. (Hear, bear.) fie hoped they would hare a larger membership, deeper fevii„gs. a ,trooFer to amid that they would he 1y ml 10 the instil °Hon mid curry it DA year by yea r w ith i ncreasing interest and enthusiasm. (I,oud app' au se.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>HINDERED BY SMALLPDX.</head>
        <p>HINDERED BY SMALLPDX.</p>
        <p>- - -- Mr. B. FAttuAn said that 'die- first movers in that Broth erh cod ere the Wycliffe Church. hey -termed a council in 1890, and had it imt been for the smallpox epidemic of 1392 their P.S.A, WO uld have been termed earlier than it was. Re had been think- ing that if they bad bad a similar army of workers.to those they had fit the commencement Wycliffe Church would not have been able to accommodate all. When Mr. Your persuaded him to take up the secretaryship 14 years ago they had hard work before. them. Their membership had gone down year by year until the last year or two, when they had sustained their strength. He was pleased to see the May with them, who . was made a Member of their Brotherhood when Mr. Gascoigne was the speaker in their clime]] some time ago.. (Applause.) In. the Inter- national Federation they had now threequarters of a million members. (Applause) The Rev. J. Goicmoss, the ex-president of the Lancashire P.S.A. Societies, had a cordial welcome. He was glad that the Wycliffe P.S.A. was as eager as it was 21 years ago, but a great change had taken place since then. In the early days .each Brotherhood worked inde- pendently, and had no connection whatever with its brother societies in other towns, where- as now they were all happily linked up into one united whole with a membership of over half a million. They were opening out branches on the Continent and in Our Colonies, which he thought would be a great agency for peace. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>MAYOR BRIEF, BRIGHT AND</head>
        <p>MAYOR BRIEF, BRIGHT AND</p>
        <p>BROTHERLY.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in moving a vote of thanks to the chairman and speakers, said he believed that the B.S.A. and Adult Brotherhoods were doing it tremendous amount Of good, and he had given a lot of attention to them. lie was it schoolboy at Wycliffe, and be was proud of the fact that he was the first Wycliffe (lay schoolboy to become Mayor. (Applause.) Ife was proud of that association with the school, which led one to think that there was nothing impossible in this life if they struck high. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>ANOTHER GOOD YEAR.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER GOOD YEAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ostantste, the treasurer, said they had bad a very 'good year, .and had 'given grants to the Health Committee, Band of Hope Union, and two grants to the Dublin strike , and to Chil- dren's Homes in Joiidon. After the year's working they had a balance in baud of .C2O, and altogether they were worth £26- (AP- plause.) A pleasing concert was given, when the follow- ing were the artistes: Miss Jones, Mrs. Small- shaw, and Messrs. W. H. Wallington, W. Booth, A. Davies, and H. M. Ashton (humorist). Mr. W. 11. Yeytth% was the aveoniimziot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <p>,w),ANveo.2,cweo2kv(Ngiq,l CHILDREN'S CORNER.</p>
        <p>Conducted by UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— Several have made a request for another competition, and this I have decided' to hold. To choose a subject, however, is not so easy a matter, and this puzzled me for a time. Then I discovered that your Easter holidays would be coming soon, so I thought we should devote our next competition to what you would like to do during the holidays. All of you must have thought what you would like to do, so just tell me in your own way what you have thought about, and give your reason why.</p>
        <p>_ The, competition will close on 28th March, so that leaves you ample time to write your short essay. I hope that many will take part and that we shall have a large and interesting com- petition.</p>
        <p>Below I am giving two letters from con- tributors.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>UNCLE, JACK.</p>
        <p>SOME RIDDLES. Dear Uncle Jack,—l know two nice little riddles for " The Examiner." When is a cow not a cow? When it is turned into a field. When does a hen want to cross the road? When it wants to get to the other side. JAMES CIINNINGTON, 32, Longford-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>NEW STORY.</head>
        <p>A NEW STORY.</p>
        <p>Dear. Uncle Jack,—Will you please put this joke in the "Children's Corner"? Wife: "My husband treats me scandalous. Why, only yesterday, he threw a cake at me, and one that I had made myself." Ma-in-Law: "Shameful! have killed you." FEED SHELLEY, 85, Winwick-roal.</p>
        <p>Why, he might</p>
        <p>DAWN. Arise ! for the sun is shining. The dewdrops' sparkle is bright, The darkness has silently vanished, And now has come the light.</p>
        <p>The birds are astir in the morning, As they bathe themselves in dew ; Aiise and come out to the beauty, , For the dawn is calling you.</p>
        <p>THE SCISSORS-GRINDER. Klink-klank ! klink-klank ! Along the sunny street, atznoon, . The scissors-grinder, Tim Muldoon, Comes trundling leisurely along. "Scissors to grind!" is all his song, ! klink ! • " Come, children, bring your mothers' shears, I'll grind them sharp, my little dears." Klink-klank! klink!</p>
        <p>The klink has stopped, the klank is still. The wheel goes round with right good-will,</p>
        <p>Szz ! szt ! szz! It makes a busy, merry sound, To please the children standing round, Szz! szz! szz!</p>
        <p>When all the steel is sharp and bright, Tim tests his wheel to see it's right, Klink-klank ! klink ! Then off lie goes with gay " Good-bye !" While " Come again !" the children cry. Klink-klank ! kli nk !</p>
        <p>MARTIN LUTHER.</p>
        <p>As it is a little more than 350 years ago since the great German reformer died, it may interest you to hear something about him. • Martin Luther was born at Eisleben, in the county of Mansfield, in Thuringia, on the 10th of November, 1483. His father, Hans Luther, was a slate-cutter, and six months after Luther's birth he set up a forge, the proceeds of which enabled him to send his son to the Latin school. There the boy got on so well that his father determined to make .him a lawyer, and sent him to the Franciscan school at Magdeburg, and then to Eisenach. While there, Luther sang in the streets for alms. and his polite manner and good voice attracted the attention of the burgo- master's wife, who took care of him. When he was eighteen, he went to the High School at Erfurt. where his favourite master was the great humanist, Trutwetter. A dangerous illness, the death of a near friend, and disturbing thoughts about religion so preyed on his sensitive nature that he became a monk. He found a Bible, and read it diligently, but it did not bring him peace. As was the custom with all good Catholics, he made a pilgrimage to Rome, and what he saw there so shocked him that he returned to Ger- many " a changed man," with all his reverence for Rome gone. One thing in particular an- noyed him; that was ,the sale of pardons for money. "The Pope has no right to sell par- dons; no one can pardon sin but God," were his words. Ile wrote ninety-five reasons against it, and nailed the paper to the church door at Wittenberg. When the Pope heard of this he issued a bun condemning Luther and his works. On 10th December 1520 Luther publicly burned the bull, along with a copy of the Canon Law. On 16th April, 1521, Luther was summoned before a " Piet of Worms." He spoke with such earnestness that the Emperor :felt moved, but the papal party was irritated. The Emperor deliberatNt for a day, and then announced that he would allow Luther to go free, but he con- demned his opinions, ;rod threatened to punish those who clung to Luther. Some, however, fol- lowed blather in spite of the severe penalty. In 1534 Luther published a Bible for tite use ot the people, and in the following year a new di (ion of the Wittenberg hymn-book appeared. the, " Diet of Spires," in 1514, it was an-: oouneed that the people were free to follow Luther until the Emperor returned from -war. This hope inspired what was called " The Wit- tenberg Reformation." - Luther had been suffering greatly during the last few years, and in the month of January, 1546, he returned to Eisleben. While he was there he caught a violent cold, but still con- tinued preaching. At four in the afternoon of February 18, 1546, the great Reformer passed away at Eisleben.</p>
        <p>"BOW-WOW !" DEAR UNCLE,—I'm writing a letter,</p>
        <p>For no rhyme or reason. in rhyme; And-Amow from the first you had better— I'm a dog, with unlimited time. I'm Nigel by name—black retriever, Past puppyhood now (I can snarl!) And it always puts me in a fever</p>
        <p>_ When ladies Siy "Fido " or " Karl!" Last evening I chased a grey kitten, Fought a mix in the city to-day, , And though quite severely I'm bitten, Victorious I came from the fray.</p>
        <p>These humans are unsympathetic! - Last night 1 sang songs (I've the knack). They cried " Drown. the beast !" " Antesthetic !" And douched water—cold !—on 'my back.</p>
        <p>When cats caterwaul, then it's awful! They have kept me awake with their 'meows; But I do think it should be unlawful Dog singers with water to souse.</p>
        <p>If you will look into this matter.— This doggerel I'll hasten to end— Ifere's cook with a dainty-piled platter— And remain your aggrieved canine friend.— !NIGEL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <p>were they pleased to welcome any new mem- bers, That meeting was of particular interest, because it was the 21st anniversary, and he was speaking to some members who had been with the movement since the formation at Wycliffe. These ~old members, he was glad to. say, were looking young—the people of the I).S.A. never grew old. (Laughter and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>BROUGHT GLADNESS TO MANY.</head>
        <p>BROUGHT GLADNESS TO MANY.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN, Councillor Roberts; said they were sorry that .Mr. Seddon was unable to be with them, and he was expressing all iheir feel; ings when he said it was a great disappoint- ment, but under the circumstances they were not surprised, !because Mr. Seddon had been busily engaged during the Week in London. However; a capable substitute had been sent in Mr. Lewis, who 14ad accepted at very' short notice the opportunity of speaking to them. lie (the Chairman) remembered very well the establishment of that Brotherhood 21 years ago —he wished he was celebrating his 21st birth- day. (Laughter.) The circumstances which led to Hie inception and the history since that period had been of such a nature as to give gladness to many people. Through their/ Asso- ciation they had done something of perrnanent value to the town.</p>
        <p>Character and Duty. Tie would say a few words about character and duty. Of course; as they were Aware, if a man applied for Work he should have a character,</p>
        <p>G. Gascoigne, nid Messrs. 13. Farrar,. hon. secre- tary, and 11. Osborne, bon. treasurer. The Mayor and Mayoress attended the concert. The llev.•T. T. JAMES said his first word must be one of congratulation to their P.S.A. BrotherhoOd, lie felt he could say that specially, because he had only been connected with them for a comparatively short time.. They were very pleased to. find that the Mayor and Mayoress had graced and honoured their •pro- ceedings that night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <p>• . • • • • • • • .• • ' •• • • .. • . .</p>
        <p>MR. B. FARRAR, • Box. SECRETARY FOR, SEVENTEEN YEARS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <p>which amounted to the points that he was sober and generally well behaved. But that was not everything. A man's Character was not that, it did not consist of what he said, but what be did; not/ hi S thoughts, but the actual results of his thoughts. They heard of people talking about the " claims" of their family—the question of " claim" did not come in at all, for it was an important duty. They had their duty to the district in which .they rived, and he had to con- fess that some of them did not exercise it as they ought to do. ,In their local affairs they had no authority to make laws, they had power to administer them; in I act, that was the sum and substance and the reason of their existence as an authority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>Best Administrators Wanted.</head>
        <p>Best Administrators Wanted.</p>
        <p>- Therefore they should see that they got the best adniinistrators possible, for the local authority was the expressed opinions of the town. They wanted to put in authority men of character. Then thieve as the dutS= to the State, and there was the question of education. There was a great deal of unrest in the world of labour, and be was not surprised and altogether sorry. They were now beginning to reap the advantages or the disadvantages, as perhaps some would call them, of the smattering of education which had been given to the bulk of the people. The un- rest was the natural sequence of the education which had been given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>CHRISTIANITY'S POWER.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIANITY'S POWER.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. LEWIS said that Christianity could. awaken greater interest than anything else. They could take the theatre with all its. faults,] and see that they were trying to stage something of the spirit of .the! early Christians. They could take a meeting of 2,000 strikers and speak to them of political economy, but they did not want that, but if they spoke to the men, telling them that they wqe prepared to fight their</p>
        <p>a large amount of money spent -upon it in print- ing .and advertising—perhaps they put some of the most striking placards upon "the. walls that Warrington ever saw.</p>
        <p>Awaiting the Guests. He could well remember the anxiety, upon that Sunday afternoon, especially when the doors were opened at 2.30. At ten minutes to three he did not think there were 50 people ill the building, and they began to wonder where all their money and their labours had gone. Then happened a most striking, and amazing thing, for between ten minutes to three and three o'clock five or six hundred men went into Wycliffe Church as though they were running to see a fire. (Laughter.) It was a sight they would never forget.</p>
        <p>They enrolled-300-men- that day. for 'it was a mews Meeting; there were none of the fah- sex' there; they kept them, out. (Laughter.) liv the end of the second month they had 527 mem- bers, and at the first quarterly Sunday they had an audience of Pm men and women, (A p_ plause.) In three months they bad enrolled 591 members and had an average attendance of 450 each Week. At the end of the first twelve months they had 1,150 members; at the end of the seemid year, 1,203; and in time third year they had 1,118 members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS.</p>
        <p>New Books.</p>
        <p>HOME UNIVERSITY LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>.Problems of Village Life. By E. N. Bennett, M.A. Unemployment. By A. C. Pigon, M.A. Literature of the Old Testament. By Professor George F. Moore, D.D., LL.D. Pre-historic Britain. By Robert Munro, M.A. M.D., LL.D., F.R.S.E. London: Williams and Norgate. is. per volume.</p>
        <p>The editors of this series continue to place the reading public under a debt of gratitude. The variety of the field covered already by the Home University Library, the treatment given to the selected subjects by specialists and experts, and the attractive form in which the books are pro- duced, all go to ensure an increasing welcome for these publications. Of the four volumes now before us two deal with aspects of the social problem, whilst the remaining two present different and unrelated subjects. In " Problems of Village Life" Mr. E. N. Bennett has brought to his task a thorough knowledge. Intimate acquaintance with our country life, not as a mere spectator, but from ' within, gives to his work a peculiar and often a convincing note. An opportune moment has ' been seized for the appearance of this book, ; and at a time when the land question, with all the related problems attaching to it, is becom- ing a matter of practical political interest, and when political discussion appears likely to be more and mord centred upon it in the imme- diatefuture, the treatment accorded it in these pages will prove to many readers both valuable and illuminating. The short chapter entitled " The story of the English Land" is a good piece of writing in which a long stretch of history has been stated in striking generalisa- tions with unusual success No one v-ill be sur- prised that considerable space has been devoted to the serious .outlook presented by what the writer terms the " Rural Exodus." The con- tinuous impoverishment of the countryside of its young men and women, and the grave import of its decreasing population, are things not only described here in sentences which make vivid impression, but the facts of the ease are illus- trated by statistics arresting in their nature. Amongst the causes of this constant depopula- tion a prominent place is given to the poverty of the agricultural labourer. An analysis of the rates of wages paid to workers on the land reveals a lamentable story, in which long hours ' of labour meet with scant and quite inadequate return. It is rightly pointed out that the sup- plementing of these rates of payment by allow- ances in kind, such as the provision of allotment ground or rent-free cottages, however well intentione(l such provision may be, yet tends . ultimately to depress wages and to condemn the labourer to a continuance of his present dis- abilities. The serious nature of the housing problem in oar villages, with a constant and oft en (wile astonishing measure -of over-crowd- , ing, and, with sanitary arrangements of the most backweA and imperfect condition, constitutes - also one of the factors which tends to deplete the tteuntry population. To this has to be added the general dulness of social life in the village, where often absolutely nothing is done to meet the desire of the young for brightness and plea- sure, and (lays have to be passed in a complete absence of recreation both indoors and out. Very convincing in its demonstration of the deadly monotony of village life is the story: in these pages of two young men who every night walked two or three miles to the point where their country road touched Hie railway line, and there watched the expresses rush past, the symbol to them of a life of fuller expression and more varied interest in the great city to which they one day hoped to go. Finally in these causes of a diminishing population the greatest weight is placed upon the uncertain and un- _attractive nature of the career open to dwellers in the countryside, the lack of any promise that life on the land will yield an adequate or antis- fving future' for the ordinary individual. Such MI analysis of the immediate problem, and the steps which may be practicable to its solution, so that the future may see tlle settlement of a contented people in the agricultural districts, make up the lines of the argument of a book 'heartily to be commended. An important addition to the Library has been made in the treatment of the subject of " Unemployment" by Mr. A. C. Pigou, Pro- fessor of Political Economy in the University of Cambridge.. Naturally the Professor ap- proaches his task .from the standpoint of an economist, and while not despising the feelings of sentiment and. pity which not infrequently lie at the root of popular treatment of the problem, it is to a scientific analysis of causes and a scientific proposal of remedies that he devotes himself. A very valuable chapter is n a that which treats of the effects of unemploy- men t. Here stress is-laid upon the actual loss of wages involved, the' variable standard of living resulting from uncertain income, and the Permanent injury often inflicted upon the inch's- a trial character of those who suffer from lack of work. The disastrousofo rundertakenee effect Under d e o (i.y lt the strain after b fruitless flii put sea u'l•litihorgraeatjb kind a man's ' morale ' may break, and he may pass, never to return, across the line whicii</p>
        <p>separates poverty from a shiftless and unworthy pauperism." Very close to the heart of modern conditions is the final assertion made in this connection that amongst the poor the precar- iousness Of employment, the fact that life has been reduced to a mere chance, and that the risk of want is always imminent, results in a haunting sense of insecurity and danger which has the most positively evil results. These pages are notable not only for certain definite ' proposals for the lessening of the gravity of one of the most appalling feature:: of our social and :ndustrial life, but also for an examination of mime popular conceptions, and here and there for the demonstration of some popular fallacies with regard to it.</p>
        <p>A very different subject is presented by the third volume under review. Professor George Moore has provided a book dealing with The Literature of the Old Testament." This is hardly food for those who are not more or less familiar with the spirit and method of Old festament criticism, but those who have such ground-work of knowledge will be both gratified and astonished by the amount that has been compressed into these short chapters. Professor Moore points out that the Old Testament con- tains the survivals of a national literature, which repays study for its own sake, apart from its value to church or synagogue, and that it will he better understood and appreciated as the reader gets a view of the times in which it was aanally written, and the historical circum- stances which gave it ,birth. He lays down the rule that the questions involved must be ap- proached ,in precisely the same way in which similar questions dealing with any other litera- ture should be approached, and that the ap- proved methods of historical criticism must apply .alike to all .writings, whether they be called sacred or profane. Those who are ac- quainted with Professor Moore's articles in the ''..Hneycloptesdia. Biblica " will not be surprised</p>
        <p>that he advances what, in mfd a fey.- quarters, will be considered extreme positions. In a limited space he has had to give a presentation of a complex and difficult subject; in many cases he can only announce a balance of pro- babilities. Even those who on some points will be ready to challenge the conclusions Arrived at, and would prefer a treatment of. the subject</p>
        <p>from a more conservative standpoint, will not fail to join in the recognition of ripe scholar- ship, fearless honesty, and stimulating fresh- ness which this hook demands.</p>
        <p>The name of Robert Munro upon the title- • page of the volume " Pre-historic Britain?' will of itself be sufficient recommendation to many readers. The product of a mind rich in learn jug, and tireless in study and research, this liook will be as good as a fairy tale to many who procure it. Here are studies of graves and caves, camps and forts, cromlechs and menhirs, ornaments and weapons. Here is the strange story of the Ice Age and of the Antiquity of Man. Here are the evidences of Palm)Ethic and? Neolithic civilisations, and descriptions of the - arts and industries of the Ages of Bronze and</p>
        <p>Iron. Of course, it is all (lone in the true scientific spirit, and with that due appreciation' of order and development which 'leaves a clear • view of the evolutionary process in the mind. Vet perhaps one of the highest words of praise which can be said about it is, that while it is a scientific handbook of, real merit, and a book of learning of the first order, the reader, as be turns its pages, becomes conscious that he has been translated to a world of romance. This ilfustrations provided enhance very definitely the value of the work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>HAVE YOU HAD INKUENZA ?</head>
