      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>MARCH 7, 1914.</head>
        <p>MARCH 7, 1914.</p>
        <p>Literary °leanings.</p>
        <p>CARUSO'S CHRONIC STAGE FRIGHT.</p>
        <p>Vocalists will doubtless read with interest Caruso's new book " How to Sing," though it gives no new royal road to focal success. But nervous people will be glad to hear that the great tenor himself and most of his fellow artistes suffer acutely from stage fright before they begin a performance; anll they may con- sole themselves with the reflection that, as Caruso says, a singer who is not nervous enough to feel anxious about his work is invariably a second-rate artiste. Some singers, he _says, try to ward off evil by crossing themselves before they go on the stage, or by getting their mothers to bless them. One lady always wears a brace- let that was given her—Caruso gallantly says "as an infant "—by Gounod. "She has grown somewhat stout of late years, and the hoop of gold has been reinforced so often that there is ' hardly any of the great composer's original gift left." But the charm still works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>WINDSOR CASTLE.</head>
        <p>WINDSOR CASTLE.</p>
        <p>The author of two volumes on " Windsor Castle" tells us that their preparation has en- gaged his leisure hours for the last twelve years. That is a long slice of a man's life to be de- voted to a monograph on a single building, but, then, Windsor Castle is no ordinary building. and the author, Mr. W. H. St. John Hope, has treated it in no ordinary way. In a very useful chapter at the end of the book the author briefly summarises the history of the Castle. It was Henry 11. who first en- larged the " King's Houses" and built walls of stone; Edward 111. replanned them on a more sumptuous scale; to Edward IV} we owe the foundation of the magnificent St. George's Chapel, which was completed by Henry VII.; Queen Elizabeth rebuilt a great deal of the upper ward, and Charles 11., added tha fine state rooms, which Gibbons and Phillipps en- riched with their superb carvings, and whose ceilings were gorgeously painted by Verrio. Queen Anne began the laYing out of the grounds, and allusion is made to the reconstruction by Wyatville, in the time of George IV. In the middle of Queen Victoria's reign considerable destruction was wrought by the removal of Den- ton's New Commons and other buildings north of St. George's Chapel, in order to clear the site. Denton's Commons were an exceedingly picturesque group of buildings, dating from 1520, in which the chantry priests we're lodged who served in the adjoining chapel. A word may be said of the Round Tower, which forms so conspicuous and familiar a feature of Windsor Castle. We learn from these pages that the first mention of a tower, or tur- ret, on the great mound occurs in an account of some repairs carried out in 1224: In the four- teenth century it contained the principal lodg- ings of the Sovereigns, but in 1406 it is spoken of as Le Doungion, or the Dungeon Tower. In 1572 it was called the Keep, and reported to be in great decay in 1577. Repaired id' Charles ll.'s time, it was considerably raised by Wyatville in the great reconstruction,</p>
        <p>THE MOTLEY MUSE. Captain Harry Graham is, perhaps, the most Popular light verse writer of the day. us merry jingles ppear serially in many news- papers, and when they are collected into a volume they find as good a public as verse can hope to find in these somewhat unpoetical days. His verse represents the beau ideal of the light Opera libretto. Take his portrait galleries of London clubs; they are both actual and critical in a breath. Here is the Athemeum : Here the Bishop, with his nether Limbs in leggings swathed demurely (Hatbrim fastened by a tether To the crown Securely), Buttonholes some friendly Duke, To discuss the Pentateuch, Or abstracts (with absent mind) All th' umbrellas he can find.</p>
        <p>And here the Garrick :--- If You come here (say) at supper-time on Satur-</p>
        <p>days, You will meet with all the patrons of the stage (Though the place is not so popular these latter days, As it was before " weekends" became the rage). Here each notable "first-nighter," Critic, journalist and writer, Sprinkles pepper on this club's especial oyster. And you hear a well-known jurist, Or some literary purist, Telling anecdotes unsuited to the cloister! • -</p>
        <p>Another characteristic' aspect of Captain Gra- ham's muse is its relation to the news of the morning. lie assun43 the function of a sort of poetic commentator upon the events of the craY, or the ebullitions of the correspondence columns. Somebody writes to the paper to say that univer- sity men, driven into a corner for a means of livelihood, are taking up the duties of the restaurant waiter. In a moment, Captain Gra- ham is ready with his witty verse-reflection : Yes, at some cafe let me wait; For though I stroked my College eight, The year they won the Ladies' Plate, How mean a triumph that is, " Compared with his who daily bears Whole stacks of Ladies' Plates downstairs. Or bumps" the backs of diners' chairs, At Evans' or Gatti's ! A " first " in "Greats" I deem no greater Than every exploit of the waiter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>NEW ST. HELENS SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>A NEW ST. HELENS SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Dowager-Lady Gamble has decided to assist the managers of the Windle Schools, St. Helens, and the Vicar of St. Mark's Church by giving a new public elementary school to accom- modate 1,200 children to be erected at Windle City on land belonging to Sir David Gamble. The managers of Windle Schools have notified the Education Committee of their intention to provide this school and also a new public ele- mentary school for about 1,200 children at Haresfinch, another outlying district in the Parish of St. Mark's.</p>
        <p>Alderman M. B. Farr,Mossley' Labour Mayor, has been initiated as an honorary mem- ber of the "John Itardman'' Tent of the Inde- pendent Order of Reehabites. s</p>
      </div>
