      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>KNUTSFORD DIVISION LIBERALS.</head>
        <p>KNUTSFORD DIVISION LIBERALS.</p>
        <p>A Good Candidate and a Hopeful</p>
        <p>Position.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Warrington side of the Knutsford Divi- sion was strongly represented at the annual meetings of the. Knutsford Liberal Association in the Manchester Reform Club on Saturday last. _ _ _</p>
        <p>---"ihe President, Lord Sheffield, occupied the chair, and accompanying his lordship on the platform were Messrs. Frank Haworth (chair- man of Executive), H. N. Bickerton and Henry Roberts (vice-chairmen), If. P. Greg (treasurer), J. R. Tomlinson and J. A. Thompson (hon. secretaries), and H. Cliffe (seretary and agent). Mr. P. M. Oliver, the prospective candidate, was also present. ,</p>
        <p>Amongst those attending from the western side of the division were: Mr. and Mrs. Henry Roberts, Captain H. G. Roberts, Mr. and Mrs. A. Albinson, Mr. and Mrs. J. W. Brayshaw, and Mr. W. H. Sherburn (Stockton Heath) ; Mr. and Mrs. J. Kenworthy and Mr. and Mrs. Frank Fox (Grappenhall); Mr. John Rose (Stretton), and other representatives from the neighbouring polling districts of the division. The meeting was one of the best ever held in the history of the Association, and speaks well for the prospects of the Liberal cause at the next election.</p>
        <p>TRIBUTE TO MR. OLIVER. The committee reported in a spirit of great hopefulness for the future. The year had been one of sustained work and propaganda. Mr. P. M. Oliver had done yeoman service in ex- plaining and expounding Liberal views and Liberal ideals throughout the length and breadth of the division. "Ever ready," pro- ceeded the report, "to give a sound reason for the faith that is in. him,- he is earning not only the affection, but the political confidence of all those with whom Ile comes in contact, and his consistent work in the division Cannot but have favourable effects at the next General Elec- tion."</p>
        <p>The Association was represented in the annual revision courts by Mr. Chile, and the result was again highly satisfactory. They were to be congratulated upon possessing such excellent and efficient service as that rendered by Mr. Cliffe. The Plural Voting Bill—now hung up by the House of Lords—should pass under the Parlia- ment Act before the next General Election. That would mean a gain of between 200 and 300 votes—not sufficient to wipe out the adverse majority, but still an improvement by no means to be despised. The officials were re-elected.</p>
        <p>TORIES AND TREASON. Lord Sheffield said they were told by their opponents that they were face to face with the possibility of civil war in Ulster. What was quite certain was that they were face to face with a declaration of intended treason, and they had the Leader of the Tory party in the House of Commons deliberately saying that he and his followers were prepared to aid and abet treasonable rebellion tli Ireland. ("Shame") "I see from "The Daily News' to-day," Lord Sheffield proceeded, "that the energetic Dean of Manchester has been saying that Home Rule may result in civil war, not only in Ulster. but in Lancashire. Well, he is a very big man, but I hope that if he goes into the streets to prac- tise civil wa someone will be found big enough to run him in." (Laughter.) He thought if the trouble came, great respon- sibility would rest upon those who made it im- possible to come to any accommodation on the question. A grave responsibility rests upon these people for ref using to try to mitigate what they did not like, but which they could not avoid." (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>Wage-earners' Rights. It was cause for rejoicing that there should now be an active demand on the part of the wage-earners for better conditions of life and labour. But their methods should be methods of law, and not of lawlessness. Working men should not forget that they had no more right than others. A working man had no more right to break the head of his neighbour than a duke had. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) "I am rather surprised," Lord Sheffield said, "that the party who call themselves the party of law and order should be the people to talk of disregarding the law and having recourse to force. It is a dangerous doctrine for a numeri- cal minority to preach in the hearing of a dis- contented and not over-comfortable majority." (Applause:)-</p>
        <p>Oil the housing question, Lord Sheffield said that voluntary activity and self-reliance, rather than Government provision and control, was, in his opinion, the bett.er way. (Applause.) Mn, OLIVER AND THREE-CORNERED CONTESTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Oliver dealt chiefly with the question of three cornered contests. Some measure like the alternative vote or the second ballot was, he said, absolutely necessary in order that they might have majority representation. Since 1910 there had been 20 by-elections, which had been three-cornered contests. In those constituen- cies in 1910 there were twenty Liberal mem- bers. • There were now fourteen Liberal and six Conservative members, and this gave to the Opposition a .turnover of twelve in the House of Commons. The representation of those con- stituencies had changed, but their political com- plexion had not. 'This kind of thing cannot . _ . _ ._</p>
        <p>go on," exclaimed Mr. Oliver, amid almilanse. "Something must be done to remove the anomaly."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>WIDNES RAILWAYMAN KILLED.</head>
        <p>WIDNES RAILWAYMAN KILLED.</p>
        <p>A distressing fatality occurred at Widnes on Saturday evening. Richard Jones (26), Harris- street, a shunter in the employ of the great Central Railway Company, was working at the points beside Moor-lane signal-box, when he was knocked down by a train going in the direction of Liverpool. Immediately after the train had passed he was found in an unconscious condi- tion. His body was covered with blood, and his skull, right leg, and arm had been broken. Dr. WCausland was sent for, but on his arrival pro- nounced life extinct. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <p>31</p>
        <p>VENUS WAIST PREVAILS.</p>
        <p>There could scarcely be a greater contrast in the lives of four sisters tin that presented by the daughters of the late Princess Alice, who married the Orand Duke of itesse. One is the Empress of Russia; 'another the wife of the orother of the German Emperor. The third sister, as the wife of Prince Louis of Battenberg, leads a retired .and as quiet a life as any Eng- lish lady, in the depths of the country; the fourth—a beautiful woman—is a nun, who con- siders the most menial work a privilege. She made a brilliant marriage, but after the tragic death of her husband, the Grand Duke Serge of Russia, she used her wealth to found a Religious house, of which the is the head.</p>
        <p>Costly Lace. The great charm of lace lies in the fact that its beauty is contained in so small a space. A piece of rare old lace, that could be carried in a small handbag, may be worth several hundred pounds, and as a work of art is capable of giving its owner as much pleasure as picture lovers derive from the most wonderful produc- tions of the Great Masters. An American woman, whose collection is rare and varied, values it at .00,000. A fichu of modern gold lace costs at least a hundred pounds, and twenty pounds a yard is not an extravagant price to pay or modern hand-made lace on a Court train. Many a great lady's lace is worth fortune. At the recent Court, the Marchioness of iSligo wore some on her train that was made for the ill-fated Marie Antoinette of France.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>there is no decrease in the length, which fre- quently reaches to the knees. Insertions of elastic, are most ingeniously let in down the hips, and these permit of freedom of ma vement, and the boning is of the slightest. Straps of baptiste keep all in place. Waists are wider than ever, almost clumsy in fact, the ideal this season being taken from the Venus of Milo. The small and tapering waist of thirty or forty years ago is now regarded as a defect. IL:m- over, it is quite possible that before the end of the year the pendulum may-swing in the other direction, and the waist And hips again be clearly defined.</p>
        <p>The Cult of the Orange Tree. - Following probably on the craze for the dwarf trees of Japan comes the cult of miniature orange trees for decorative purposes, and as the latter put forth charming blossoms, and bear golden, if not eatable, fruit, there is mucn to be said in favour of this change of taste. It is a pretty idea to provide the blossoms for the bridal wreaths of one's relatives, or friend..., or for oneself, as some brides are doing. More- over, these pretty trees make a welcome change from lilies and palms, as church and house decorations on the occasion of a wedding. A recent bride walked through quite a grove of these miniature trees on her way to the altar. They must be grown in a conservatory, but are by no means difficult to rear, and will well repay for the trouble taken with them. The Vogue for the Silhouette.</p>
        <p>The craze for collecting silhouettes has led to the revival of this interesting art, which was invented by a Frenchman, Edouard Silhouette, who flourished in the early part of the 18th century, and devised a machine which kept the sitter's head perfectly still. Then his profile was thrown on a white sheet, and the likeness cut out in various materials. The art flourished for at least a hundred years, until superseded by the Daguerreotype, which was hard and not as picturesque as the silhouette. There is a priceless collection of the latter at Knole Park, among which are many family groups. Kings and queens of Franco and England sat to sil- houette artists, as did the greater number of their subjects. The big London stores and shops are now employing artists, who are kept fully employed, for society has taken up the crazy once more.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>Remunerative Work for Women Artists.</head>
        <p>Remunerative Work for Women Artists.</p>
        <p>The book-plate is another revival—if it ever went out of fashion—which is. providing remun- erative work for women artists, with a technical knowledge of drawing, some imagination, and a talent for artistically combining incongruities; for clients frequently name a multitude of objects, very difficult to group, or to arrange successfully. It is said that the demand for these plates is increasing, and that the work. is fairly well paid, and very interesting. Bread and Milk.</p>
        <p>The majority of cooks and housewives would be highly indignant if told that they did not know how to prepare bread and milk correctly, and yet such a statement would, generally speaking, be the truth, and nothing but the truth. First the milk, with sugar to taste, should be brought to scalding point, then a sufficient amount of bread, cut into neat little squares, thrown in, the saucepan drawn on one side, and covered for about ten minutes. The bread and milk, which should be neither too thick nor too thin, should then be served in a cup or basin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>Child's Paralysis,</head>
        <p>Child's Paralysis,</p>
        <p>Left Hospital a Cripple.</p>
        <p>But made to walk by power of Dr. CASSELL'S TABLETS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cooper, of Fenn-lanes, Fenny Drayton, near Nuneaton, says: "Dr. Cassell's Tablets have worked a wonderful cure in my litt' Hilda. She was paralysed for thrr now, after a course of the Tablets, she can go about by herself. The cause of the trouble was her being knocked down by a bicycle when three years old. Some years later the Paralysis came on, and though we had good advice and .also took Hilda to hos-</p>
        <p>_ pital, where a slight opera- tion was performed, there seemed no hope. She left Hilda Coo] hospital a cripple, and it Nuneaton. was thought she could not live many months. However, I got Dr. Cassell's Tablets for her, and the improvement amazed me. Hilda now goes about without any help, and even runs errands for me." Cure after cure, even in he t most severe 'cases, have proved Dr. Cassell's Tablets to be the surest remedy ever devised for Nervous Breakdown, Antemia, Debility, Sleeplessness, Nerve Pains, Palpitation, SidneY and Stomach Disorders, Children's Weakness, Spinal and Nerve Paralysis, General Bodily Exhaustion, Brain Fag, and all run-down conditions. Send 2d. to-day to Dr. Cassell's Co., Ltd., 418, Chester- road, Manchester, for a free sample. All chem- ists sell Dr. Cassell's Tablets at 101(1., Is. and 2s. 9d.—the 2s. 9d. size being the most economical.</p>
        <p>.tle girl. tee years, and A 444r1.," kW, - -Ter,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>ST. HELENS WOMAN'S NEGLECT.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS WOMAN'S NEGLECT.</p>
        <p>Mary Ann Swift, of 8, Wolfe's-row, Fleet- lane, St. Helens, was sentenced to three months'imprisonment on Monday for neglecting her five children. Tier husband, Daniel Swift, was hound over for three years in consideration of the difficulty he had had in his* home. George Swift, aged twelve, a son of these people, was charged, along with Thomas Sarbutts, of Ash- croft-street, with stealing a pair of boots from the market, and a quantity of nuts from a shop. Sarl3 liuttsasur-aesmabnoduednd inovoerrdefie.rotaht ireae years, and Swift Tangements could be made for him to be admitted to an industrial school.</p>
        <p>Thomas Nuttall, butcher, of Hehnsliore, was fined .C 3 and costs at Haslingden for exposing for sale tuberculous meat, and Frank Abbott, farmer, of Helmshore,,Hls. and costs for assist- ing and abetting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>School Caretakers.</head>
        <p>The School Caretakers.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Shimmin said the school caretakers were having some trouble with the Education Committee with reference to extra remunera- tion for work performed by their having to attend to meetings in the schools. One or two of the Labour members on the committee were fighting for them, whilst their own delegate was opposing them on the ground that the question had nothing whatever to do with the Educa- tion Committee. He differed from the 'views of their delegate. The Education Committee em- ployed the caretakers and paid their wages. He could prove that the Education Committees in other towns had taken the matter up and laid down rates for the school managers, stating that the caretakers had to be paid for extra duties. If they could de it in other towns they could do it in Warrington. Mr. Crrealy was inclined to favour Mr. Plins- ton's views. The extra money should not come out of the rates. -</p>
        <p>Mr. Dewitt protested that the money did not come out of the rates. In towns he could men- tion people who engaged the schools had to pay the piper. In other places caretakers were paid for Sunday schools, surely all that did not come out of the rates. (Hear, hear.) A Delegate asked if the caretakers could refuse to do the work.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Hewitt replied that they could not refuse. It stated in their agreements that they had to be at the call of the Education Committee and managers.</p>
        <p>It was suggested that the matter should be deferred until Mr. Plinston would be able to attend. Perhaps, said a delegate, he will give us his _views.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shimmin replied that Mr. Plinston was their delegate, and they should instruct biln how to vote. (Hear, hear.) It was ultimately decided to defer the matter for a mouth. •</p>
        <p>Labour Representation. Dealing with the question of Labour. repre- sentation, Mr. Howard said the time was. now ripe for Warrington to be represented in the House of Commons by a Labour member, and he thought that if any body in the town should give the initiative, it should. be the Trades Council. (Hear, hear.) Therefore he moved that the trade union bfanches in the district be asked to send representatives other than their ordinary delegates to a conference to be called, so that it would infuse more energy than was ordinarily /displayed. The reason he thought they should have a Labour can- didate was that on looking round they saw that never in the history of the town ha d there been such an opportunity for the Labour forces gain- ing the seat. The solidarity and firmness of the workers in Warrington had never before reached such a high degree. He was confident if they Went ahead with the matter and got the moral and financial support of the trade unions, nothing would stand in their way. The power of representation was something to value, and not to be cast away for a mere shadow or a promise of something they might get when they were 70. Mr. Law seconded. A conference would test what the Warrington working men 'thought about the idea. It was ultimately decided to relegate the matter to the Executive Committee. "</p>
        <p>Board of Guardians. Mr. Taylor, with the idea of securing direct i representation on local bodies, moved that the Council take steps to have a represen- tative nominated for the Board of Guardians' election, which would take place shortly. He thought the time had come when they should select suitable men to sit on that body. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>- Mr: Hambleton Pointed out the difficulty of attending morning meetings. Mr. Dukes, however, thought that there were many trade unionists who were free at the time of the meetings. (Hear, hear.) The matter was left in the hands of the Executive Committee.</p>
        <p>In their 86th annual report the council of the Royal Botanical and Horticultural Society of Manchester and the Northern Counties record that the kiociety's indebtedness, 'which seven years ago stood at £3,000, has been reduced to less than ,t 3,000.</p>
        <p>RAIL NATIONALISATION.</p>
        <p>Clerks Discuss the Great Waste Cal By Present System.</p>
        <p>TRAINS RUN AT A LOSS.</p>
        <p>A strong case, illustrative of the urgent n° of the nationalisation of British railways, *I made out in favour of such a proposal At. meeting of the Warrington branch of the 131 way Clerks' Association, held in the Win"o3 leigh Cafe. The most prominent speak; was Mr. S. Lomax, of Bolton,. and ' presented his case in a convincing mann(' Mr. G. Merrick presided over an encourae attendance, which reflected the interest tli section of railway employes have in the (111 tion.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Lomax said the subject of the nati alisation of railways was one which was cc lag before the country more prominently than it had ever done, because the Governrql had appointed a Royal Commission to go 1,1 the question, possibly with the view of natl' alisation in the very near future. Last 51 the Trade Union Congress appointed a depu tion to see the Premier, and Mr. Asquith S' he was not convinced on the question, but prepared to make inquiries as to how natil alisation worked in 'other countries. Forti ately for their Union, their General Secret+ had already been deputed to visit the Contial to inquire into the working of the railw there.</p>
        <p>Haphazard Inauguration. When railways were instituted there was system aimed at; Parliament granted 110w°,11, to every company who came along, promising fulfil certain conditions. They used to tin( railway chairmen at the half-yearly meetivc speaking very strongly of the advantages ° competition, but shareholders were wise all( did not put all their eggs into one basket. Th 4 had shares in other companies, but they fon; one part of their shares were bei°' used to defeat another part investe‘ in other companies. These men I'l wise, and they insisted upon the riv°: combining so as to do away With the excessil° competition in existence. Power had beel, given for amalgamations, conditions being nivi that the Board of Trade had power to see el.; the agreements were carried out in the pub" interests.</p>
        <p>3 le</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>100 Instead of 313.</head>
        <p>100 Instead of 313.</p>
        <p>In the United Kingdom t-lie;e—w. ere 313 seinli ate railway companies, but so far its actil working was concerned, they might be nilljr bered at slightly over 100, the smaller Coe panies leasing their lines to the larger. 1111, early state of affairs had been passing aw,,f very slowly, until now they had great clot bines in the railway world which were n° worked in the interests of the public as a wholei for charges were being increased. There mu% be uniformity in connection with raibvnYs 8.0 a whole, and they found men of standing le the trading world recognising the importan of railway nationalisation from the trade point of view. But what were to be the,tot. ditions Of emgoyment? Would they, thi ran' way workers, be any better off under nation' alisation than they were under the present con" ditions? He thought they would. _ _</p>
        <p>_ Wages tow, Profits High. d During a certain period while the wages 11!. been very slightly increased, the profits of tuv companyooo.hiltd,he risen son fruo,ams that 'o°lrlthere 41 uP '' of the workers, for ite4eB;l"2''' speeding a less staff it allowed that the workers doing more more work than before. Lord Claud itamilton—(laughter)—had had r! go to America to find a man suitable for 11 railway. (Laughter.) They knew him, and I lie could get a man who would work for noel: ing, Lord Claud would have him. The condi' tions on the Great Eastern were practically All.et worst in the whole of the country, and It seemed that the new man was to be brought, With a view of introducing the American hue ling tactics into this country, and to get hill dividends, but they must see to it that he di not get them from the clerks. Wasted Resources. Under the present system of railway eontre! in the United Kingdom, the average life of waggon was seventeen years, but so far as thl° country was concerned, the waggon was onli used seventeen weeks out of the 52. Therefor° for the greater part of the year it was more a less standing idle or being run over the WI empty, which, as they knew, was a waste 0 power. Further, waggons were utilised more the foreign railways than they were in thl country, because here they had to have regn7 to the ownership of the waggon. If the ways belonged to the State the waggons coil,il be used to their full capacity, and there woui° be a great saving to the community as a wholei The failure of competition was acknowledge" , when railway companies agreed to working agreements. The large surplus of income over 1 expenditure oil our railways was made up froo ! two sources: underpaid labour and cost of trail; sit. They as railway employes were concernwith the question of underpaid labour, alie:,,i i when they compared their labour with the" people doing the same class of work in other( places they found they were at the bottom 19 the scale.</p>
        <p>Clerks Must Organise. - In the past they had lacked organisation, ad now they were trying to make up for it, and 10 was pleased to say that so far as their Associ, tion was concerned they never were in such ° healthy state as they now were. (Applause.) It was not a difficult problem to transfo the railways from private to State ownershiP; inasmuch as they would proceed more or let by way of the business arrangements whiel were taken in hand by the corporations 0 various towns when they took over the tral, woys from private companies. As they kneel there was already an Act of Parliament call Mr. Gladstone's Act of 1844, which provided for railways being taken over, if built after 1.84: but it was likely that another Act would passed to deal with present conditions. 111° proposals now made were that there would be., a time limit, and the railways would be takel over by giving Government Stock for the preseLi holdings in the companies, so it would simply a transfer, practically speaking. 1" shares to the same value now held by shsre. holders in the railways. Minister of Posts and Railways. 1 They proposed that the Postmaster-Genet:l should be designated the Minister for Posts 01/ Railways, so that they could he worked unild one head. A Commission would be appoint,, to control the undertaking, and it would composed of Government and other repreBeot:3; tives. Even that would not be sufficieo because the Commissioners would not have 011ie wide knowledge of the requirements of tulit country as a whole, hence it was proposed tilij there should be a railway council, which w°lll6l be representative of local interests. It 30%0 still be necessary for trade unions to exist, op they did at present, for no Government, like, d private company, would give them what b_ler- wanted unless they were compelled. whate:be might be their private opinions as regarded transfer, or private or public ownership, t." p had not simply to look at the thing fovioe railway worker's standpoint. They must 11 0 some regard to the interests of the public il;tit whole, but when railway workers were one 010 of twelve of the adult members of the ni population they ought to have some Toice the management of the concern.</p>
