      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <p>'Batley After a Drawn Game at Wilderspool Wins</p>
        <p>the Re-play</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>VICTORY BY ONLY itnE POINT.</head>
        <p>VICTORY BY ONLY itnE POINT.</p>
        <p>a.o early in Warrington are out of the cup-ties. This fate -! 1,1.0 a the competition comes as a surprise, Jut there can be no doubt that Warrington are large degree responsible for, bringing this ate nPon themselves.- They lost a ' golden op- Portunity at Wilderspool on Saturday, when theY allowed Batley to make a draw, and then It Mount 6 Pleasant on Tuesday they met with "l'ettt by thile. points to 5, after leading 5-0 at half-</p>
        <p>ty,7210 Warrington men did not play in these ' Matches us one expected them to do, and as were capable of doing. One could hardly that they were the same side which beat "-"-• lielens and Huddersfield. The forwards , . chiefly at fault. Batley, on the other hand, 1,Z6 extremely lucky. Their try which equal- MB game on Saturday, was the essence of ";nd the three goals by Which they won ell r:JeY—one dropped and two penalties—were ""Y ones of a most pronounced character.</p>
        <p>A BRILLIANT TRY. 4214'3-thing considered, Warrington were the 4i§h`rtr team at Batley, although they did not ta!:11° it in may pronounced degree. The great "take they made was not to have been more lig..irressive in the first halt; when they had a 2,,lll„"irind in their favour. They allowed. the defence to hold them too cheaply. Then onr, stile first half Chesters was given marching f aera, and this added to Warrington's discern- ort,</p>
        <p>The try scored by Warrington in the first half</p>
        <p>By 66 Scrum.”</p>
        <p>THE DRAWN GAME.</p>
        <p>Forwards Out of Form; the Backs . Overburdened With Work,</p>
        <p>Warrington undoubtedry missed their chances in the game at Wilderspool. As a whole, the team did not play up to par. It Was evident from the start something was seriously amiss, , and when one found time to analyse the situa- tion one came to the impression that the fault! lay with the forward line. The six at Wilders- pool did not seem the same six which did so well at St. Helens seven days before. All through the backs were overburdened with work. What went wrong with the forwards on Satur- day it is difficult to say. They clearly had a day off, and Batley were not slow to take advantage of it. The Batley tactics were just of the type to suit such an occasion, and while there was plenty of hustle in the game, the finer points were entirely absent. Hustle was the only pos- sible game open to Batley, and they made the greatest -possible use of it,</p>
        <p>MONOTONOUS SCRAPPINESS. The scrappiness of the game became most 'monotonous. In cup-ties one always expects a degree of scrappy play, but that at Wilderspool must surely have excelled that seen in the other I cup-ties combined. There was scarcely a bit of scientific passing witnessed, and both sets of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>RYLANDS' CUP FIGHTING VICTOR.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS' CUP FIGHTING VICTOR.</p>
        <p>Photograph by Daniels.</p>
        <p>. • RYLANDS' TEAM, 1913-14. Including some players who figured in the Cup Rounds, but not lb the Final: tack row : F. Potter, G. Warren, and W. Burgess (secretpxY). , Standing. C. Harding (trainer), J. Oaten bY (instructor), J. Brandwood, J. Broadhurst, P. Morris, T. Mason, J. Whittaker, 11. Morris, lad P. Brooks (trainer). . , Sitting: J. Gallop; J. Griffiths, A. Stretford (captain),' S. Carter, F. Dorrocks, and G. Floyd. . Front : T. Laws and A. Ilorrocks. , Holders of the Lancashire Junior Cup and Medals, Manchester Shield, and Manchester League Cup.</p>
