      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <p>...</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <p>Defeated in Cup Semi-Final After Giving Their</p>
        <p>Worst Display This Season.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY BEAT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>By " Litiosman.”</p>
        <p>Owing to the result of the semi-final tie in the Cheshire Amateur Cup, the hopes of Latch- ford have been wiped away. It was a sad dis- appointment to the many supporters of Latch- ford who made the journey to Altrincham to see their favourites defeated. They one and all declared it to be one of the worst exhibitions they had given this season. The "gate" was about .£2o._</p>
        <p>pWortaariiington and District League the i.t match was decided at West , Allivoleßsututilelinebetween the Heathens and Great San- key. The latter proved victors by the odd goal in three. Other matches had to be cancelled. The Divex Club wrote to the secretary 9f the League cancelling the fixture with the Adult, School and at the same time notifying their intention' of withdrawing from the competition, owing to thz,ir inability to get a team together to comply with their fixture list. The fixture between the Weaver Refiners and Crosfield's Reserve had to be cancelled owing to , the latter being unable to raise a team. The matches in the Second Division had to be postponed owing to the ties in the gine/. Mcaal Competition.</p>
        <p>--- CHESHIRE AMATEUR CUP. Semi-final tie. Latchford v. Cranford. On the ground of the Altrinchant Club. The opening exchanges wets vigorously eorite§fed, and Cranford were the first to become danger- ous, but ther advance a-as CllOlO4 1/Y- tiff,tide. From the free kick the Latchford players took up the running, but were met by a pair of stub- born backs. Fogg on two occasions missed easy chances, and then Newton get in a capital shot at the other end, which Kite saved. Brace- 'girdle was next called upon to clear. The players did not show Much of the scientific nature of the game. They were too excited to settle down to their usual styles. The Latch- ford PlaYerg BPPenred to be off their game alto- gether, their half-backs being very weak, and Drinkwater got past the backs, only to see Kite make a splendid save. He fell full length and put the ball round the post. The corner kick proved fruitless. Later Kemp scored for Cran- ford, and they maintained their lead up to the interval. On resuming the game was very fast, and the Cranford forwards made a smart attack. Kite was quickly called upon to save. The ball was returned and llaspell, in attempting to clear, kicked into his own goal, and placed Cranford further ahead. Cranford for a time completely overplayed their opponents, and were soon three clear goals ahead. Following these reverses the Latchford players showed improvement. Good combination by Fogg, Lees and smith ended in the former making a splendid centre, which Smith met and banged the ball into the net, scoring the best goal of the match. Result: Cranford, three goals; Latchford, one goal.</p>
        <p>the visitors' goal, W. Taylor beat Nancollis with a low shot. Half-time arrived with the scores one goal each. - On restarting the game was played at a very fast pace. The home team were given a free kick, from which Monks scored, thus giving the home team the lead. Result: Fairfield, two goals; Weston, one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. First Division.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. First Division.</p>
        <p>Jinones, Harrison and Glover, Denny , Stockton Heath v. Great Sankey. On the West Avenue enclosure in their re- turn League fixture. Teams: Stockton Heath— aSnimdcGocafko;rdS;anslimeiythrlsdagGilols,lg; Stubbs. Great Sankey—Bond; Glover and Stephens; H. Ditchfield, Gardiner and Payne; Darlington, Illidge, J. Ditchfield, Poulter and Houghton. Referee, J. T. Hadkiss. The open- ing exchanges were in favour of the visitors. A good piece of play by 11. Ditchfield drew out the home defenders, and then a neat pass put Darlington in an easy scoring position, when Glover nipped in and robbed him cleverly and cleared the goal. After this escape the home team improved, and the ball was quickly trans- ferred from end to end, but owing to good back play on both sides neither goalkeeper was seriously troubled. No score was registered in the first half. In the second portion good play by Glover (of the Heathens) set his forwards going from mid- field. They made a strong attack on the San- aot key goal, when Stephens tripped Harrison in- side the penalty area. Denny took the kick and l ac e the attackball i non theh e placed it a couple of yards wide of the goal. The Sankeyites replied with dn home goal, and J. Ditchfield judiciously passed nt centre,out to whichllou g hton' who returned a splendid placed the home goal in danger. The home backs erred in judgme , and Dar- lingnet.to The nipped in and Pe Heathens got into the visitors' terri- tory, and were awarded a penalty for a foul on Jones. Stubbs being entrusted with the kick, be made the scores equal. The excitement now on both sides was very high, and several stop- for minor injuries took place. The visit- pages ors, playing a very determined game, gradually forced the home team to defend their goal, and Gifford WAS penalised for jumping, H. Ditch- field took the kick and scored the second goal for the N. litors. Following nig the game de- veloped to the kick-and-rush style, and no fur- ther goals were added. The result Was: Great Sankey, two goals; Stockton Heath, one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. First Round._</head>
        <p>MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. First Round._</p>
        <p>v. St. ge—rrYound. Players: Brewery— George's Mission. e's—Roberts; CWPayinitdotenhrse;Pß°:lroleowßreerreyvia.nd Percival; Briscoe, Astall c oßnetfeesrt:de, each end being and Dawson; Bostock, Barnes, Rutter, Living- and Dawson and dd Tvulelrya;andoulSdter• , GDeuamrgbavin and E. Marsden; S. Hands, Yates, P. Hands, L. Mars- and it was Bamber. The ' G. only a minute was a repetition gamevdiesni teadnwdaaiTat ehreonrmae ntpeisfoyn; ao f bard first, from the interval when Ruttgeerviiscnsiotroerds fgoarvethae Brewery. The second half surprising exhtion, an are to be coniPli- monted on their game. P. Yates equalised with which completel ablo)atrhiasnaetasnijokfe pilnaaynenrser7ordkTihn a grand shot, Y beat Clayton. The home teara began th Press severely, and Briscoe had hard lines with a shot that hit the upright. No further score vial; made, and the game ended in a draw of one goal each.</p>
        <p>Fairfield V. Weston. On the Fairfield ground, with the following players: Fairfield—Howard ; Monks and Webb; W. Sk-elmardine, Osborne and Jackson; 11. Skel- mardine, Hollows, W. Taylor, B. Taylor and Appleton. Weston Nancollis; Rowe and Barnes; Flanagan, Frazer and Hope. Referee,lazlehurst, Aspey and Leach; Oldfield, Conway, B. Howell. The game was keenly fought and each side missed opportunities. After thirty Minutes' play the visitors were awarded a penalty kick, which was taken by Aspey, and his side gained the lead. The homesters nowo played vigorously, and after several attempts on</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>Excelsior v. Orford. St. John's.</head>
        <p>Excelsior v. Orford. St. John's.</p>
        <p>Owing to the keen rivalry between these teams this tie was considered the tit-bit of the draw. The following players lined up: • Excelsior— Rigby; Boyd and Morris; O'Neil, Spencer and Welsby ; Walker, Leah, Williams, Leah and Hughes Cox. St. John's—Forber ; Harrison and Scarisbrick; Stretford, Denny and Smith; Meachin, Tickle, Millward, Hankinson and Smalley. Referee, G. H. Rushton. Losing the toss Millward set the ball rolling for the John's and the visitors were soon in the home terri- tory, only to be repulsed by Boyd. Midfield play followed until the home forwards made an advance, and a centre from Welker was finely cleared by Harrison, he homesters next had the best 9f. I:ne ekchanges. Leah seat in a high shot, which Ferber cleared splendidly, and the ball going to midfield, Tickle and Millward transferred play to tlios home end, where Meachin with a 20 yardP) shot beat Rigby oil the way. This reir.et44l acted tight the' home team, and they played more keenly and assailed the visitors' goal, but Forber and his backs stubbornly resisted for a time. A Couple of corners to the home team proved of no 'Mit but B. Leah managed to equalise just, before the interval. _</p>
        <p>Upon resuming both teams played a hard,ond keen game. Millward was penalised for a foul on Spencer, but Boyd placed over the bar. The homesters continued to attack and the John's defence were severely tested. Forber made a grand clearance at the expense of a corner from a shot by W. Leah. The flag-kick was fruitless, and end-to-end play followed. The visitors made a rush on the home goal, and Spencer handled inside the area. Rigby rose to the occasion by saving the resultant kick, and the game became more exciting, both teams being inclined to play the man instead of the ball, but eventually Smalley got past the backs and la-eat Rigby with a high shot. No further scoring was done, and the John's ran out winners of a hard and exciting match by•two goals to one. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>Orford Athletic v. Longford United. _</head>
        <p>Orford Athletic v. Longford United. _</p>
        <p>_ These teams opposed each other with the fol- lowing: Orford Athletic—Jen?iings; Rutter and S. Woodhouse; Fi,tzgerald, Timmins and Man- nion; Backhouse, Fallon, Stanton, Rigby and Mellitt. Longford United—Barber; Mosby and boulder; Lea, Sherratt and Darbyshire; Ilor- ton,.A. Finch, Ifolloway, Helsby and Finch. Referee, S. C. Essenhigh. , Finch opened the score with a shot that completely beat Jennings. Following this the visitors were let through by a mistake by Rutter, and A. Finch added second goal. The Athletic did not appear capable of holding the United forwards, and the play was chiefly at the home goal. Finch, the visitors' captain, registered the third goal, and directly after boulder scored a fourth goal. The home forwards could not get past 'the visit- ors' defence, and Jennings, in the home goal, made several smart saves until Lea beat him just before the interval. In the second half A. Finch added a sixth goal for the United, and when .time wits called his side were easy winners by six goals to none.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>ASSOCIATION_ ITEMS.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION_ ITEMS.</p>
        <p>P. Broadhurst, brother of J. Broadhurst, late of Latchford Rees., is showing some fine form between the sticks for Emmanuel Institute. Heacock, Emmanuel Institute's outside-right, scored his first goal of the season on Saturday. Good luck to him! Emmanuel Institute have made a fine capture in Walter Eaton. The Latchford Club unexpectedly had• to bid good-bye to the Cheshire Amateur Cup. They were anticipating bringing the cup to Latchford. They never consulted Cranford about it. The Latchford supporters declare it to be the worst exhibition the Latchford players have given this season. Great Sankey defeated the Heathens at West Avenue. By doing this they have assured themselves of the championship. Great Sankey will now have their name in- scribed on the Dodd Challenge Shield. Ditchfield was the best man in the field. "Herbert" scored as usual from a penalty kick. ,1 Glover (Heathens) was their best half-back. He robbed Darlington of what appeared to be 11 a certain goal. The entries for the Minor Medal Competition have come up to expectations. There were some of the ties played last week- end. St. George's Mission made a draw with the Brewery. Roberts, Brown and Tully played a grand de- fensive game for the Mission. They were defeated by Second Leaguers. Orford Athletic were easily defeated by Long- ' ford United. Jennings, in goal, and Woodhouse, at back,' were the best for the Athletic. The Brothers Finch, lloulder and Lea shared the goals. The tit-bit was the meeting of the Excelsiors and the John's. Ferber and Rigby gave good exhibitions be- tween the uprights for their respective teams. B. Leah scored for the Excelsior, and Meachin and Smalley scored for the John's. The second round in the Medal Competition will be continued to-day. TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Padgate United pla;'-.-Gr-a-pp—e-n.h.a.11 St. Wil- frid's, at Padgate, with: Nork; Warburton and Sankey; Hodkinson, Critchley and Freeman; James Houghton, John Houghton, Gilbert, Wil- hams and Hardman.</p>
        <p>_ Emmanuel Institute meet Ohl Botelerians, at ' the King and Queen ground, Padgate-lane, with: P. Broadhurst; P. llughes and W .New- ton: A. Oakes, S. Banner and W. Ayres; P. Heacock, Walter Eaton, S. Roberts, A. Hughes' and W. Eaton. Latchford meet Lostock Gralam at Thelwall- lane with: Kite; J. W. Bardsley and Robinson; Downs, E. Webb and Siddall; Smith, P. Webb, Lees, Fogg and Haspell. Orford St. John's play Lymra at Pepper- street, Lymm, with a team selected from: Forber, Harrison, Scarisbrick, Smith (J.), Smith (W.). Stretford, Atherton. Denny,, Meachin, Millward, Hankinson, Smalley, Red- ford, Tickle and Sudell.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. Altrincham Amateur Cup. Latchford v. Lostock Gralam. Warrington and District League. Weston v. Stockton Heath; S. C. Essenhigh. Daresbury v. Weaver Refiners; R. S. Brand- reth. St. Barnabas' v. Fairfield; R. Rule. Warrington Minor Medal Competition. Second Round. . Acton Village v. Bank Quay Recs.; B. Howell. Vulcan Mission v. Army Service Corps; W. Dennett. Fairfield United v. Weaver Refiners; J. Sher- lock. Re-Played Medal Tie. St. George's Mission v. Wilderspool Brewery; G. Bamber: Widnes and District Alliance. .</p>
        <p>Stockton. Heath Reserve v. Widnes County Reserve.</p>
      </div>
