      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>CRICKETERS-BOWLERS.</head>
        <p>CRICKETERS-BOWLERS.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club.</p>
        <p>MR. THOMAS COOPER AS PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club was held at the Lion Hotel on Tuesday. There was a fine muster of members, and, in the unavoidable absence of the President (Sir Gilbert Greenall), Mr. Wil- liam sileock (an ex-president) was elected to the chair. . _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Lowe (the honorary treasurer) explained the accounts, which showed that the present financial standing of the club was the best ever known in its history. There was a credit balance of .R 93 is. 6d. The treasurer, officials and commit-lee, and particularly the House Committee, were congratulated upon the excellent results obtained.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks ko Sir Gilbert Greensll (the late president) was unanimously passed, and his name was added to the list of vice-presi- dents.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion, in proposing Mr. Thomas Cooper as president, said he could honestly say that they had had a long list of distinguished and•himourable names in the past in their list of presidents, but he felt sure that Mr. Cooper would rank worthy to be associated v ith all those who had gone before, more especially as for the past twenty years he had been actively associated with the interests of the club, and had, perhaps, done more than most of them to- wards making the club both successful and a place where members could thoroughly enjoy themselves.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. Palmlough seconded, and the reso- f ution was enthusiastically carried.</p>
        <p>THE NEW PRESIDENT'S SPEECH. Mr. Silcock then vacated the chair, and the new President, in acknowled,zing the enthusi- astic manner in which his name had been re- ceived, said whilst he thoroughly appreciated the honour they had done him, he took up the position with mixed feelings, for, looking down the long roll of distinguished and excellent presidents the club had had, they would well understand any hesitancy he might have in accepting such an important trust. He maintained that the club had for many years, and was still, meeting a much-needed re- quirement in the town. It had a history, and it had traditions of which they were justly proud, and he would promise them to do his best to maintain the fair name and good reputation of the club. Rightly or wrongly, the club at one time was looked upon as a little selfish and exclusive, but, whether this was merited or not, it was a thing of the past, and he contended that it was now one of the most friendly and sociable clubs in the country. As evidence, he related the experience of an elderly member coming to hiui at the close of last season and saying: "I am glad I joined this club, for I have enjoyed every moment I have been on the ground." That, said Mr. Cooper, was the kind of feeling which ought to obtain, and which the members should individually and collectively do their best to promote and foster. Their own pleasure might be the primary object sought,l but it ought not to be their sole aim. 1 In such a large club, numbering nearly NW members, they must of necessity have many changes and losses, and they had to deplore, since last they met, the deaths of two worthy vice-presidents, in the persons of Mr. William Richardson and Councillor Birtles. They were both esteemed townsmen of Warrington, and each in his sphere of life did a useful and im- portant work. The club was honoured to have had them as presidents, and was much the poorer by their deaths. NEW OFFICERS ELECTED. The following officials for the ensuing year were appointed : Honorary treasurer, Mr. Robert Lowe; collector and assistant treasurer, Mr. R Lowe, jun.; honorary secretary, Mr. C. H. tinsworth; match secretary, Mr. B. Silcock; captain first team, Mr. W. Richardson; captain second team, Mr. W. V. Turton; deputy-captain first team, Mr. S. L. Partridge; deputy-captain second team, Mr. C. Hawthorn; committee for cricket, Messrs. B. Silcock, W. Richardson, W. V. Turton and S. L. Partridge; committee for bowling, Messrs. W. S. Haddock, J. Rigby, E. Wallington and K. Bradford; committee for Itennis, Messrs. E. J. Byerley and G. Jack; sec- retary tennis club, Mr W. S. Edwards; secretary bowling club, Mr. George Brown; captain bowl- ing club, Mr. R. T. Fairclough; auditor, Alder- man Tinnion.</p>
        <p>An interesting discussion followed as to the necessity of .bowling members wearing rubber soled footwear when playing on the greens, and it was unanimously resolved that the recom- mendation should be carried into effect and that in future every member must play in rubber soles when bowling on the greens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>YACHTING.</head>
        <p>YACHTING.</p>
        <p>Fiddler's Ferry Sailing Club.</p>
        <p>The eighth annual dinner of the above club was held on Saturday at the Ferry Hotel, Fiddler's Ferry. About 65 guests sat down to a splendid dinner, the catering of Host H. Burthem giving every satisfaction. Amongst' the guests were Mr. W. Kelsall (president of the club), Mr. E. Pickthall (secretary), Mr. H. Burthem (treasurer), Mr. T. Bennett (commo- dore), and Mr. W. H. Smalley (late secretary). r There were a large number of visitors present, including Mr. J. Green Chappell (of the Royal Mersey Yacht Club and commodore of the Tran- mere Sailing Club), Messrs. R. Ilitehmough and J. McCoy ( honorary treasurer and honorary secretary of the Tranmere Club) and Messrs. T. Ward, H. Taylor, and J. Lythgoe (of the same club).</p>
        <p>The toast "The Kindred Clubs" was proposed by Mr. W. H. Smalley, who said it was a great pleasure to them, as members of the Ferry Club, to have the members of the kindred clubs with them that night. Mr. R. Hitchmough (Tranmere Club) said he was pleased to see the way the Ferry Club was Progressing and to notice the younger blood among the club. The Ferry Club had more difficulties to contend with than the Tranniere Club had. At Rock Ferry one was not troubled so much with sand banks, &amp;c. The Commodore proposed "Our President" (Mr. W. Kelsall), and the toast was drunk with musical honours.</p>
        <p>A successful programme was contributed to. by the following: Mr. Herbert Bennett, "Pal of mine"; Mr. George Davies, "Death of Nelson"; Mr. Colgulioun. Scotch comedian; Mr. Farring- ton, Lancashire comedian (who was the "star" of the evening); Mr. Oddy, "Asleep in the deep"; Mr. Dale, comic song; and Mr. Tickle, sentimental.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>Prize-winners.</head>
        <p>Prize-winners.</p>
        <p>The prizes were distributed to the successful competitors by the Commodore. A series of four races had been held with the following result : First race: 1, H. Burthem (Comrade); 2, J. Whittaker (Nina). Second race: 1. IL Bur- them; 2, J. Whittaker. Third race: 1, H. War- burton (Thelma); 2, S. Meacock (Pastime). Fourth race: 1, H. Warburton; 2, S..Meacoetk• There was a tie for the handsome cup presented by the President, which was won by H. Bur- them (Comrade), after two run-offs with H. Warburton (Thelma). This was the second time that 11. Burthena had won the cup. The novice race resulted: W. Kelsall, gold medal (helmsman of the Comrade), 1; W. Ben- nett, barometer (helmsman of the Pastime), 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>ILNGLING.</head>
        <p>ILNGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Associa-</p>
        <p>tion.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE. DELEGATES IN WARRINGTON. The delegates from all centres held their first I meeting of the year in Warrington last Satur- day. A deputation from our local club met them at the L. and N.-W. Station and escorted them to the Bay Horse Club-room, where the meeting was held. After business tea was pro- vided by Mr. T. Roberts, and several member* took the visitors round the town, several calling at the headquarters, Gladstone Arms. The object of these meetings is to call dele- gates from different centres to have a voice in the working of the N.A.A. Each centre secretary or delegate goes with the object of furthering the cause of angling, such as taking over waters, asking the railways for better privileges, and working the financial business. The association provides every delegate with out-of-pocket es. penses. The Warrington delegates' report of the meeting will appear next week.</p>
        <p>FINE GOWY ROACH. Although we have not had large quantities of roach from the Gowy this year—in fact, the returns have been very poor—we are at last treated to two very fair specimens, Mr. F. Trigger being responsible for both. One turned the scale at 11b. soz. 13dr.; the other was 11b. 2oz. 9dr. Other members' results were mostly blank.</p>
        <p>There is not much time left now if these have to be beaten from the GowY, but, weather per- mitting, there will be a fair crowd on Sunday next, it being the last Sunday before the close season.</p>
        <p>THE WEAVER. It is now definitely settled that the Northwich and district has taken over the Weaver. What the charge will be to societies who frequent this river for match fishing is not yet known, but from what can he gathered the expense is not going to be great. If not,' then it will be a good thing for anglers in general that a society on the spot haw taken it over. If the fee is too high, we think they will drive many clubs to fish other waters where angling is free. ThG rules and charges will he put through these columns as soon as they are to hand.</p>
        <p>THE CANALS. Some indifferent sport has been had both at Norton and Runcorn way, Messrs. Hill and Moores being responsible for 121 b. of good roach in three hours' fishing. Other members visited P on next week, the canal a few days after, and returned with- out a scale. The specials from headquarters for this stretch of water will be put and members may rest assured that all species will be catered for.</p>
        <p>NOTICE. Club night at Gladstone Arms on Monday next.</p>
        <p>Coopers' Arras club night on Monday next. We have already collected a fair amount of specials. A. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>HUSBAND AND WIFE. Alleged Serious Assault.</p>
        <p>A wounding case of intense gravity was men- tioned at the Borough Court, before the Mayor and other magistrates on Monday, when James Thompson, puddler, 6, Silver-street, was charged with unlawfully and maliciously wounding his wife, Sarah Jane. It was alleged she was struck on the bead with a hammer.</p>
        <p>Details of the alleged offence were given by the Chief Constable, who produced the short- handled, heavy-headed hammer. The prisoner, stated the Chief, is a man of drunken habits. and does little or no work, whilst his wife earns the money to keep the house going. At two o'clock on Saturday he was drunk and his wife, had to go out selling greengrocery. She left the prisoner asleep on the sofa, and he was still asleep when she returned. On going out to make some purchases she locked the door, and when she arrived back at 10 o'clock the pris- oner wanted to know why she had locked the door. She gave him some explanation, but re- fused to argue with him. Prisoner then went out for some beer and asked his wife to have some. She refused and went on with her work about the house. Prisoner came up behind her and struck her on the head with the hammer. She fell insensible, bleeding profusely. Some time afterwards she recovered, and after trying to get the hammer off him he came at her a second time. She managed to wrest the weapon from him and threw it kinder the table. She went from place to place in the house, and the floor was simply covered with blood. Informa- tion came to hand and the woman's wounds were dressed at the Infirmary. P.C.'s Harrison and Moody brought the man to the Police Station. He was partially drunk and was charged in the morning, when he was sober. I ask for an ad- journment, concluded the Chief, as in all prob- ability this case will have to go for trial. The woman had a bad injury to the scalp. The Clerk (to prisoner): Have you anything to say against the adjournment? Prisoner: I know nothing at all about it.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: You will be remanded for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. R. T. Fairciough (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>other magistrates.</p>
        <p>Barracks Raidecl—William Butters (22), 1, Matthews'-buildings, .Lowmoor, Bradford, was charged on remand with having stolen a gent's bicycle, a quantity of clothing and 12s. 6d. in money, value £lO in all, from Orford Barracks. —The Chief Constable said the prisoner enlisted in the Special Reserve of the South Lancashims in the name of Arthur Stocks. He ransacked 1 the pockets of his comrades in the barrack room and cleared out with the bicycle. Some of the articles had been recovered from the residence of his parents, and they expected to recover the bicycle that day. It was very hard for the men, who worked hard, and could not afford to lose much, that things should be stolen from them. Prisoner was a deserter from a Yorkshire Regi- ment, but he joined the Special Reserve on December 11—Prisoner was sent to prison for six months.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst and Bolton, Councillors Wilkinson and Dr. Joseph, and Messrs. S. Wright, W. Roberts, G. Pilling, and T. Locker. Pat's "Night Out."—Patrick Shaughnessey, a farm labourer from 'Thelwall, was ordered to pay 10s., including costs, for being drunk and disorderly and refusing to quit the Packet House Inn on Saturday night. He was fighting with another man, when P.S. Woollacott went to the inn and dragged him out. It was only with the assistance of another constable that the prisoner was got to the Police Station.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. Before Councillor Henry Roberts (in the chair), Colonel Edelsten and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. A Broken Nose.—Thomas Kelly, 5, Stanley. street, was charged with assaulting Matthew Carr on Saturday.—The Chief Constable said prisoner and a man named Shaughnessey were arguing in the Packet House Inn. Carr was de- sirous of stopping the row and told prisoner that Shaughnessey was big enough to eat two of his sort. Prisoner struck Carr a vident blow and felled him. The House Surgeon at the Infirm- ary said Carr had a broken nose. The police were desirous of making inquiries with regard to another charge, and asked for a remand, which was granted. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Treated Ceniently.—Douglas S. Neil, Prospect House, Crow Wood, Widnes, was summoned for riding a motor cycle at a speed dangerous to the public in Bridge-street.—P.C. Andrews, who was on point duty at the Bridge, said defendant came down Bridge-street at 20 to 25 miles per hour. He signalled to P.C. Armstrong at the end of the bridge, but defendant could not be stopped and proceeded up Wilderspool-road. He returned and was then stopped and said the engine was a powerful one, and that he had only been riding two days. If there had been aear on the bridge there would have been a serious accident. —The Chief Constable said the defend- ant had regretted the incident and had also pleaded guilty. He thought it was an accident, and asked the Bench to deal leniently with de- fendant.—Neil was ordered to pay the costs; on promising "not to undertake any such fool- hardy business again." Treated With I mpunity.—Emmarrael Cooke Margett, 16, May-street. Burnley, was summoner,' for leaving a heavy waggon in Sankey-street thus causing an obstruction.—Detective S mars said defendant was. in a public-house with other men for fully 15 minutes. When told that he was causing an obstruction in the narrow portion of the street he became very impudent fe —Thede- fendant Chief had failed f ailed Constable te appear pointed out that a treated de the whole thing with impunity. The Police pressed the matter strongly.—P.S, Sankey cor- roborative evidence, and defenda ant g-aayse 40s. and costs or one month's ira prison mentfid. fined</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. Before Messrs. J. W. Hayward (in the chair), C. Jackson Holmes and W. T. Williams. "Jim, Plead for Me."--Sarah Ann Higham, 96, Hume-street, was charged at the instance of her husband With being an habitual drunkard. —Higharrt said his wife was continually drunk and would not look after the home. lie was willing to take her back if she would sign the pledge and keep the home all right.—lnspector Wainwright said the woman had previously promised to keep the pledge to Mr. Firth, but she had broken it.—Prisoner: Jim plead for me, and let me come home.—The case was ad- journed for one mouth to see if there was any improvement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>PIGEON FLYING.</head>
        <p>PIGEON FLYING.</p>
        <p>Preparations for Coming Season.</p>
        <p>The West Lancashire Saturday Federation of Homing Pigeon Societies have appointed the following officials :—President, Mr. W. J. Shaw; vice-presidents, J. Lowe (Lowton), T. Mason 1 (Warrington), F. Boyle (Sankey Bridges), W. Wright (Latchford), J. Traverse (Burtonwood), T. Newton (Lymm), T. Malkin (Warrington): vice-chairman, S. Taylor; convoyer, S. Taylor, Lower Walton; reserve convoyer, F. Boyle, Warrington; auditors, G. Crank and F. Nicholls; delegates to Lancashire Combine, President and Secretary; official measurer, A. Moseley, Levenshulme, Manchester; official time-keeper, W. H. Paice; treasurer. T. Mal- kin; secretary, W. H. Paice, 14, Miller-street. Warrington. The headquarters are at the Lion Hotel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>DATES OF THE RACES.</head>
        <p>DATES OF THE RACES.</p>
        <p>_ The following are the stages and dates of races for old birds: Bath, May 8; Bournemouth, May 14; Weymouth, May 21; Jersey, June 3; Rennes, Lancashire Combine, June 16; Nantes, Lancashire Combine, July 4. Federation optional pool races: Bournemouth, Weymouth. Jersey, Rennes and Nantes. For young birds the dates are: Worcester, August 7; Gloucester, August 14; Swindon, Lan- cashire Combine produce, August 21; Bath, August 28..</p>
