        <p>1 9 1 4 1 9 1 8 I'M LEAVING WARRINGTON. My visits to Warrington are now quickly drawing to a close, and those who need my advice and help must consult me on Wednesday next certain. The calls I am receiving from other districts make it very probable that this will be my final visit. I ask you, therefore, to take time by the forelock and con- sult me then, as I shall not be able to return until 1918—think of it, four more long years a misery and unhappiness. My advice costs you nothing, and if I cannot do you any good I will tell you so. My belts have been supplied to the London Hospital and other large medical institu- tions, and are recommended by the medical profession. In fact, I number many doctors' wives amongst my patients—could you wish for better proof of the efficacy of my appliances? Note Date of Closing Visit. eONSULT ME ON WEDNESDAY NEXT. Ruptures, Women's Internal Weaknesses, Displacement, etc., Cured and Relieved Without Operations or Internal Implements. Our delicate organisations render us liable to contract internal ailments which, if not attended to in time, may cause us years of suffering. Most , women, especially the younger ones, generally do their utmost to keep the iti iLtri IS*, nature of their ailments to themselves, iin many cases not even telling their ••----t;.:.....r,, ... relatives—this o f false tu od esno . doubt Any from thought a oso sense flncseonsulting a specialist is immedi- •'' -.Ci'''.A., e . ti. litroy. : „. 1 ately stampeded. Why do you not ac- cept the inevitable and consult a lady specialist—one of your own sex, who has had over twenty years' experience amongst cases similar to yours, and who has cured and relieved over 90,000! Whether it be internal weak- ness, displacement, rupture, or kindred complaints, I can cure you. Read these Local Testimonials. WARRINGTON LETTER. LYMM TESTIMONIAL. "Dear Madam,—You will be pleased " Dear Madam, Your crusade to learn that already I have derived against the use of internal 