        <p>"Such a wonderful skin-healer as Zam-Buk cannot be too widely 'known," said Mrs. E. Lowe, of 20, Coombt Road, Chiswick, London, N., to a London reporter. "When Dora (7) was recovering from measles, scurfy patches on her head caused the hair to come out in handfuls. I took Dora for four months to the West London Hospital. The dry scurfy patches, however, broke into wet sores. The child must have had from thirty to forty of these itching, smarting sores. Even through linen bonnets the tore her head in a shocking manner. "My husband now suggested Zam-Buk which had healed his ulcerated leg. The very first dressings of this rich balm soothed Dora's itching skin. ZAM=BUK Proves a Real Boon. "r soon realised what a blessing Zam-Buk is. The sores stopped discharging as I persevered with it, and there was a steady improvement until all the eruptions were cleared away and Dora's scalp was sweet and clean. The child's hair is now thick and healthy again. "When my husband's right leg was badly ulcerated, Zam-Buk was the only thing that relieved the pain and healed the sores midi new healthy skin. "Zam-Buk also rid me of a very troublesome attack of scalp disease." i,No comZam-Buk,on ointment or salve can ever do the same good as Zam-Buk, which is a pure and unique er h balm. completely free from animal Ms. Sold in sealed boxes only, at and NS. Of all chemists and drug stores. Refuse all imitations and substitutes.</p>
