        <p>the Batley sextet did not hesitate to put this to an advantage. In addition, they were favoured by a stiffer breeze behind than fell to the lot of Warrington in the earlier part of the game. The Batley try was a tricky one. Just.before it came Ruddick intercepted a pass, knocked on, and attempted to drop at goal. From the sub- sequent scrum Brooksby got the ball and made a bluff attempt to pass out, and when the War- rington defence was deceived, swung the ball over the breaking-up pack to Thomas, who caught it smartly and got over the line. Lyons failed with the kick. •Warrington made strenuous efforts to get the lead in the latter stages of the game, when they outplayed their opponents, but failed, and an unsatisfactory draw of three points each resulted. The Warrington forwards, as already stated, were weak. Young was more active in the War- rington half-back line. Waywell was conspicuous in the threequarters, and once more the slow methods of the centres were very noticeable. Jolley was not so sure as usual, although he did some good work. ALL PAID. It is not often that we have the chance of stating the precise number present at a football match, but last Saturday this was made possible by the fact that, according to the cup rules,, all paid. Members, honorary members, everybody. were included in this sweeping condition, so that the 9,980 who passed through and were. regis- tered by the turnstiles represented the actual " crowd," as well as the "gate," which totted up to the satisfactory sum of £2.52 18s. The " gate" at Batley on Tuesday amounted to £lBO, making a total of over £432 iOMth two matches. WARRINGTON'S TEAM FOR TO-DAY. Young Goes to Threequarter. In view of the stiff match with Hull at Wil• derspool to-day, the Warrington team has been re-arranged. Ruddick is substituted by Brookes at threequarter, and Young partners Waywell on the left wing. " Stan" is an old three- quarter, and the pair ought to prove a fast wing, capable of scoring. Dainteth goes to half-back,</p>
        <p>Shield. This season they reached the final stage of the N.U. Cup Qualifying Competition; when they were beaten by Wigan Highfield 11 points to 8 points. Such is a brief outline of a history that is creditable not only to the club, but to the town. It is a record that will require a good deal to equal. Mr. William Burgess, the secretary, is a hard worker for the club, and certainly plays his part cleverly and well, while he is supported by an energetic committee and a loyal band of players. The actual team that won the final in the Lancashire Junior Cup this year was P. Morris. • Griffiths, Carter, Horrocks, Laws. Warren, Gallop. Stretford (captain), Brandwood, Whittaker, Mason, H. Morris, Broadhurst</p>
        <p>'* a kt,a. brilliant one. Nicholas began the fateful 1144tcl.,„%ltletit. After gaining a bit of ground he ta" the ball on to Dickenson, and George in 415 flt it to Brookes on the right wing. From 44,1"0., ition it skimmed into the hands of ClareA Thelwall lad rushed through opposition under the bar. This try alone was Of winning the match. Jolley kicked al, and at the interval Warrington led by -43ar points. BATLEY'S LUCK BEGINS. Was „leapped by the absence of Cheaters, who kee4 ' ,u,,("Yed o have struck a Batley man in the th ,trrin.g ton did not show they felt it in tell rt. few minutes, but a man short began to I*-staa.„„lt'r on. The Warrington side from tim ac .c ran straight for the Batley line arb an 1. gat il'"lned certain, when Lyons, by sheer luck, ball and kicked it dead. F 434%. adopted the kick and quick follow-up for tr,Risas the wind had greatly increased in Us genie told. Lyons was kicking the ati forward, when, to the surprise of the . :For „e3citos;_the 1)01 sailed between the uprights ;il-°l)Peil goal. Pt was a lucky bit of work. their ',(l to 'Batley infusing .more energy into ,nlaY than necessary, and Leek was sent off With Chesters company. e5l 12 inen /a-side, the game became more hrroin -.Niel-loins': Young and Witywell proved ent with sonic good work, which pied Ilatl44st: Lyon!, got two penalty goals, and won by a single point. 8 B 41 PILLS can be relied. upon .to c.:11,ar4.e either sex all acquired or constitutional dis- l_fr(s,Tl the 'Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains w,ard„: "„acit, Free from Mercury. Established up- .i.Llern;,,io' 50 vsars In boxes 4s Ed. each, of all 7137eW:0,..44a111..5e-B,attni:,t, Medicine Vendors throughout sixty stamps by the maker_s.— coiti...yit.)e.oln and 'Midland Counties Drug Co., Lin- \ Vt. •</p>
        <p>Ithreequarters were more intent upon the kick: and-rush-style than upon the telling game of passing. Warrington, even in their scrappy way, had more than their share of the play, and . they should not have 'allowed Batley to make a draw for scores of chances had been sent a-begging in the most tantalising way. Many times prior , to Warrington's score in the first half it looked as if someone would get over, but the same old ' mishap occurred every time the ball was played into the hands of Lyons, or those of some other Batley defender. WARRINGTON'S ONLY TRY. The try awarded to Warrington was really due to the initiation of • Young, and possibly this was his best bit of work during the day. He put in a fine run, and then sent the ball on to Wayv.-ell. The latter kicked shortly over Lyons and, as he attempted to follow up, was obstructed by the back. The referee did not hesitate to award a try, as three times out of four Waywell would have scored simply. Jolley made a poor attempt during the conversion period, and a good chance to notch the five points was lost. From this incident until half-time the game was strongly in favour of Warrington, but score they could not. The "Gallant Youths'" tack- ling proved extremely sound, and this, coupled with the fact that the Warrington forwards were lazy, mulled every possible chance. The sides turned round at the interval, with War- ringtim leading only by the dangerous majority of three points. • THE SCORE EQUALISED. The second half was even more scrappy. The Warrington forwards seemed to be pumped, and</p>
        <p>l and Tranter re-appears in the forward line ' The sides are expected to be:— Warrington. Jolley. Waywell, Young, Dickenson, Brookes. Nicholas, Dainteth. Cox, Fearnley, Thomas, Clare, Tranter, Skelhorne. • Herridge. Allen, Darmody, Holder,. Once, Taylor, Devereux, Anderson. Harrison, Batten, Gilbert, Francis. Rogers. Hull. liiek-ott-at 3.30. Warrington "A" play St. Helens Bees. at St. Helens,,with : Bramhall ; Baker, Steel, eherrett. Shakeshaft; Ruddick, Brown; Cheaters, Har- mer, Naylor, Critchley, Green, Pouncey. RYLANDS' RECS. TEAMS. In the Manchester and District League Rylands' Recs. play Pendleton, at Gorsey-lane (kick-off 3.30), with: F. Horrocks; Griffiths, P Morris, A. Horrocks, Laws; Warren, Floyd; Stretford, Whittaker, Mason, Brandwood, H. Morris, Broadhurst. In the St. Helens and District League Rylands' Recs. "A" meet Sherdley Athletic, at St. Helens, with: Potter; Scarisbrick, Riley, Barton, Hard- man; Britton, Cummings; ViTelsby, S. Cooper, Peers, H. Cooper, Jackson, Dumbell. The Waterfoot Ankle-Strap Company are clos- ing down their works at Waterfoot and remov- ing to.rnore commodious premises at Irwell Mill, Bacup, where the business will he continued under the title of Hargreaves and Crowther, in- corporated with the Waterfoot Ankle-Strap Company. The firm at present employs 80 work- people.</p>
