        <p>HOCKEY. STOCKTON HEATH v. OXTON. Stockton Heath were at home to Oxton, and after a very fast keen game won by two goals against one. From the bully Oxton got into the home circle, Howard being penalised for kicks. From the free hit Bainbridge got through and 'opened the scoring for Stockton Heath. Play was very even until 10 minutes from half-time, when Oxton pressed strongly, Heyward scoring. half-time: Stockton Heath, one goal; Oxton, one goal. Play in the second half started very fast, both goalkeepers being troubled. The home team were now playing up strongly, Bainbridge giv- ing Stockton Heath the lead after 20 minutes. Play continued very fast and even, until just on time, when Stockton Heath pressed strongly. Baxter taking a fine centre from Eyre and scor- ing. The referee, however, disallowed the point, Oxton claiming that the ball had been into I touch. Result: Stockton Heath, two goals; Oxton, one goal. Stockton Heath are away to West Derby to- day, who are a greatly improved side this season, and are going very strong at present.</p>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY. WARRINGTON ATHLETIC CLUB. FIVE MILES CHAMPIONSHIP. Several thousands of spectators, attending the Warrington v. Batley Cup tie, waited to see the decision of the local club's five miles cham- pionship, and a very interesting race resulted in a popular win for "Billy" Bolton, who de- lighted his Walker's Brewery workmates with splendid running. The Longford Wire Works' men saw the I difference night duty makes to Chris. Vose; Fletcher, Russell's men must be hoping to see Herbert Holbrook fitter for subsequent events, and a like remark applies to the Moore people, who saw Frank Antrobus start much too slowly to get on fighting terms. Result: 1, W. Bolton; 2, C. Vose; 3, T. F. Turton ; 4, F. Antrobus; 5, A. Gregson; 6, J. Hanson; 7, F. Baker; 8, H. Jepson. Money from Rubbish. Old boots and shoes which, when quite past wearing by anyone, find their way into our dust- bins, are extremely valuable, and find their way again into our houses in most artistic form. They are soaked in water to remove the dirt, all the nails and threads are picked out, and the leather reduced to a thick pulp, from which wallpapers, screens, Sze., are made. The finer the original quality of leather the better it takes the bronze and old gold of the designs which make these hanging things of beauty. Book- binders and framemakers also know full well the value' of this pulp, and carriage-builders press it into sheets, which are invaluable for the roofs of the most luxurious vehicles.</p>
