        <p>- 110111ttY. LATCHFORD JUST BEATEN. Latchford were defeated by 3-2 at Wilming- ton Park, but were decidedly unlucky. Win- nington were the best side in the first half, and five minutes after the interval were leading 2-0, but then a great change came over the game, and up to the end Latchford were on top, and Pearson equalised by scoring two clever goals. Winningtori, however, broke away in the last few minutes, and Capper scored a winning ." goal. For Latchford Detcher and Billington w,ste a good pair of backs, and Allanson, Wilkinson and Albinson were grafters at half-back. Wi- lkinson's placing to his forwards was good. C. Newbold made a most successful debut in tlie• first team in the awkward position. of outside- left, and got some rattling centres across. Pear- son, although closely shadowed by Deakin,,wats in form at centre-forward, and along with Knight did some tricky work. Starkey and Dale on the right wing came into the picture, more in the second half, and Dale got 60/0,, splendid centres across. The second eleven beat Winnington secoa4 at Latchford by three goals to nil. This ii tt first time in the history of Latchford secona that they have been able to defeat Winnington twice in one season. McCauley, minards And W. Starkey scored tor Latchford. Teams for To-day. The first team meet Southport to-day with..." J. Bruhl; P. Allanson and C. Billing-tom;JAlbinson, J. B. Wilkinson and A. Knight; Br A. Dale, J. P. Cleminson, J. E. Pearson, A. G. Marsden and H. IL Colton, Umpire, Mr. Grounds. Bully off 2.45. The second team are away to Hyde lit with: G. Myne.kyrne; W. T. Eccles and 11. V. Fletcher H. Emes, F. C. Newbold and T. Banks; .1.• 3. AfoCouleY. W. S. Furness, W. H. Minards, W. Starkey and A. Starkey. Train leaves Central Station 1.35.</p>
